window.ATLAS_CONTENT = {"meta":{"tagline":"INTERSTELLAR ANOMALY REVIEW CONSOLE","objects":{"3i":{"designation":"3I/ATLAS · C/2025 N1","loebScale":3,"pillNote":"REVIEW ACTIVE","loebScaleHistory":"RANK 4 JUL 2025 · HELD 4 DEC 2025 · REDUCED TO 3 MAR 2026 (QUIET JUPITER PASS)","anomalyCountNote":"LOEB'S FINAL PUBLISHED TALLY: 22 (2026-03-12) — REGISTER TRACKS 26 GRANULAR CASES · TIMELINE CURRENT TO 2026-08-18","datasetVerify":"TIMELINE DATASET: CORRECTED · COMPARISON DATASET: CORRECTED · ANOMALY CASES INDIVIDUALLY FACT-CHECKED"},"1i":{"designation":"1I/'OUMUAMUA · 1I/2017 U1","loebScale":4,"pillNote":"RETROSPECTIVE FILE","loebScaleHistory":"THE ORIGINAL CASE — BIALY-LOEB LIGHTSAIL 2018 · RETRO-RANKED ON THE 2025 LOEB SCALE","anomalyCountNote":"11 CASES ON FILE","datasetVerify":"TIMELINE DATASET: CORRECTED · ANOMALY CASES INDIVIDUALLY FACT-CHECKED"},"2i":{"designation":"2I/BORISOV · C/2019 Q4","loebScale":0,"pillNote":"NATURAL CONTROL","loebScaleHistory":"THE CONTROL CASE — AN ORDINARY COMET FROM ANOTHER STAR. FILE THIN BY DESIGN.","anomalyCountNote":"5 NOTABLE ITEMS","datasetVerify":"TIMELINE DATASET: CORRECTED · ITEMS INDIVIDUALLY FACT-CHECKED"},"fb":{"designation":"CNEOS FIREBALL REGISTER · ATMOSPHERIC IMPACTS","loebScale":null,"pillNote":"IMPACT CATALOG","loebScaleHistory":"NO LOEB-SCALE RANK — THE SCALE IS APPLIED TO TELESCOPIC OBJECTS, NOT TO CATALOG ROWS. TWO CLAIMED INTERSTELLAR METEORS HERE, NEITHER CONFIRMED OUTSIDE THE CATALOG THAT REPORTED THEM.","anomalyCountNote":"7 CASES ON FILE — IM1 (2014-01-08), IM2 (2017-03-09), THE 2026 RATE QUESTION, AND TWO CLAIMS TESTED AGAINST OUTSIDE DATASETS RATHER THAN ARGUED","datasetVerify":"CNEOS ROWS LIVE FROM THE JPL FIREBALL API · RATE CASES CROSS-CHECKED AGAINST AMS EYEWITNESS REPORTS AND GLOBAL METEOR NETWORK PHOTOMETRY · THE VOLCANO AND 2013-PRECEDENT CLAIMS AGAINST NOAA VOLCANO POSITIONS AND THE NUFORC SIGHTING ARCHIVE · EVERY COMPUTED FIGURE RE-DERIVED BY tools/fireball_rate_check.py"}},"quotesVerify":{"verdict":"CORRECTED","counts":{"VERBATIM":9,"CORRECTED":2,"PARAPHRASE":1,"SECONDARY":23},"note":"9 OF 35 QUOTES MATCHED CHARACTER-FOR-CHARACTER AGAINST THE PRIMARY SOURCE. THE REST CITE MEDIA WHOSE PRIMARY TEXT IS NOT PUBLICLY FETCHABLE AND ARE MARKED AS SUCH RATHER THAN PRESENTED AS VERIFIED."}},"anomalies":[{"object":"3i","id":"A-01","title":"Anomalously large size / interstellar mass budget","date":"2025-07-08","observation":"Early photometry made 3I/ATLAS far brighter than expected for an interstellar object at ~4.5 AU: if the light came from a bare nucleus (albedo ~0.05), its radius would be ~10 km (~20 km diameter) — a mass Loeb put at roughly a million times 'Oumuamua's and a thousand times Borisov's (his July paper says over ten-million-fold and hundred-thousand-fold vs Borisov's core). Later HST/JWST work capped the nucleus far smaller (Jewitt et al. HST: radius ~0.22-2.8 km; Loeb's final essay uses a 2.6 km diameter). A separate September 2025 Loeb-Cloete-Veres analysis claimed the absence of pre-perihelion non-gravitational acceleration independently forced a mass above ~33 billion tons (diameter >5 km).","loeb_take":"Loeb (arXiv:2507.05881, submitted 2025-07-08) argued the interstellar rocky-material reservoir cannot deliver ~20-km objects at this detection rate — one would expect millions of 'Oumuamua-sized objects for each object of 3I/ATLAS's mass scale, so it is either a comet with a small core (<0.6 km) or a member of an implausibly rare population exceeding the interstellar mass budget. In his November 2025 likelihood ranking he assigned the mass anomaly P<0.001, and he kept the 'mass budget discrepancy' as anomaly #1 of his final 22-item list, arguing planetary-disk reservoirs miss the required mass by orders of magnitude even for the revised 2.6-km diameter.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"[paraphrase] The inferred nucleus diameter of 2.6 kilometers and the number density of its parent population (assuming a natural comet) exceed the mass reservoir of planetary disks around low-metallicity stars by orders of magnitude.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"The brightness is dominated by a dust coma, not the nucleus; Hubble imaging (Jewitt et al., Aug 2025, arXiv:2508.02934) constrained the nucleus radius to <2.8 km (albedo 0.04, lower bound ~0.22 km), eliminating the population problem. Kilometer-scale interstellar comets are consistent with expected ISO size distributions, and Rubin Observatory is expected to find many more. Jason Wright (AstroWright) notes Loeb's later 33-billion-ton mass floor rested on assuming non-gravitational-acceleration measurement precision of 1e-10 AU/day^2 when the actual precision is ~1e-7 — off by ~1000x.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.05881","https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02934","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-02","title":"Ecliptic-plane alignment (~5 deg, 0.2%)","date":"2025-07-16","observation":"3I/ATLAS's retrograde hyperbolic trajectory (inclination ~175 deg) lies within about 5 degrees of the ecliptic — the orbital plane of the planets. For a randomly oriented incoming interstellar trajectory, Loeb computed a ~0.2% chance of such alignment.","loeb_take":"In the July 2025 paper 'Is the Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS Alien Technology?' (Hibberd, Crowl, Loeb; arXiv v1 submitted July 16, 2025), this was the founding anomaly: a low retrograde tilt to the ecliptic 'offers various benefits to an Extra-terrestrial Intelligence,' making the path look like a designed reconnaissance trajectory with optimal access to the inner planets. After introducing the Loeb scale in August 2025 (arXiv:2508.09167), Loeb kept this among the core set anchoring his rating — 'there are eight anomalies that endow it with a rank of 4 on the Loeb scale.'","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"aligned to within 5 degrees with the orbital plane of the planets","quote_source":"'3I/ATLAS is Fading Away, Leaving Us to Ponder Over Its 22 Mysterious Anomalies', Medium, March 12, 2026","official_explanation":"Mainstream astronomers call this a textbook a-posteriori probability fallacy: the specific property was chosen after observing it ('he chose specific aspects of the comet's orbit to compute probabilities for after he knew what they were' — Jason Wright), and with any object some ~0.2%-level coincidences are guaranteed among the many orbital parameters one could examine. AstroWright (Nov 9, 2025) labels it a 'classic fallacy and misuse of probability theory,' citing Hector Socas-Navarro's detailed critique; there is also mild survey selection bias toward ecliptic discoveries. Dynamically the orbit is unremarkable for a galactic-disk object.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12213","https://lweb.cfa.harvard.edu/~loeb/HCL25.pdf","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.09167"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-03","title":"Fine-tuned arrival geometry (Mars/Venus/Jupiter passes)","date":"2025-07-16","observation":"The arrival time placed 3I/ATLAS within tens of millions of km of three planets: ~29 million km (0.19 AU) from Mars on Oct 3, 2025, ~0.65 AU from Venus in November, and 53.6 million km (0.358 AU) from Jupiter on Mar 16, 2026. Loeb estimated a combined probability of order 0.005% (he later listed P=0.00005) for a random interstellar object to thread this sequence.","loeb_take":"In the July 2025 paper and later essays, Loeb argued the timing looks 'fine-tuned' for planetary reconnaissance — exactly the flyby program a probe designer would choose. This was one of the original 8 anomalies behind his rank-4 assignment and remained #3 in his final 22-item list.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Astronomers respond that any object traveling in the ecliptic plane will necessarily pass 'near' planets, so the claim double-counts the ecliptic-alignment anomaly; AstroWright calls it question-begging, noting Loeb treats both close approaches and non-close approaches as anomalous. Tens of millions of km are distant passes with no operational significance. NASA states all evidence points to a natural comet.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://lweb.cfa.harvard.edu/~loeb/HCL25.pdf","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-04","title":"Perihelion hidden behind the Sun","date":"2025-07-16","observation":"Perihelion occurred Oct 29, 2025 (Oct 29-30 across time zones) at 1.356 AU while 3I/ATLAS was on the far side of the Sun as seen from Earth (solar conjunction Oct 21) — unobservable by ground-based telescopes from late September until it re-emerged in early December 2025.","loeb_take":"In the July 16, 2025 arXiv paper (Hibberd, Crowl & Loeb, arXiv:2507.12213), Loeb and coauthors flagged that this geometry would let a technological probe execute a clandestine reverse Solar Oberth maneuver — an optimal braking burn at maximum solar depth — while hidden from Earth. In his ranked anomaly lists this is folded into the 'fine-tuned arrival time' anomaly (P=0.00005, Nov 23, 2025 Medium list), and he explicitly called for post-conjunction astrometry to check for maneuvers.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The eclipse by the Sun from Earth of 3I/ATLAS at perihelion, would allow it to conduct a clandestine reverse Solar Oberth Manoeuvre, an optimal high-thrust strategy for interstellar spacecraft to brake and stay bound to the Sun.","quote_source":"Hibberd, Crowl & Loeb, 'Is the Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS Alien Technology?', arXiv:2507.12213 (submitted Jul 16, 2025)","official_explanation":"Perihelion viewing geometry is set by chance orbital phase; solar conjunctions like this are routine for comets. Moreover the object was NOT unobserved: STEREO (Sep 11-Oct 2), SOHO/LASCO (Oct 16-26), GOES-19 CCOR-1, PUNCH, and Parker Solar Probe's WISPR (Oct 15-Nov 5) imaged it through conjunction and perihelion, Mars orbiters (MRO/HiRISE, ExoMars TGO) imaged it at the ~Oct 3 Mars flyby, and Europa Clipper observed it Nov 6. It re-emerged on a trajectory consistent with a natural comet; the small (~3.7-sigma) non-gravitational acceleration reported by JPL/MPC is attributed by most astronomers to ordinary cometary outgassing.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"timing","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12213","https://lweb.cfa.harvard.edu/~loeb/HCL25.pdf","https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/3i-atlas/","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-05","title":"Sunward anti-tail / collimated sunward jet","date":"2025-08-05","observation":"The Hubble image of July 21, 2025 showed the dust distribution extended toward the Sun, about ten times longer than wide, with no classical dust tail; a sunward anti-tail re-appeared in July-August and again in November 2025, eventually reaching ~1 million km. Mars HiRISE imaging on Oct 2, 2025 also showed a glowing extension ahead of the object along its direction of motion.","loeb_take":"Loeb argued this is a genuine sunward-pointing jet, not the perspective illusion seen in thousands of normal comets (P<0.001 in his Nov 23, 2025 likelihood-ordered list), and that sustaining a collimated sunward plume is more naturally explained by a thruster pointed at the Sun for braking than by sublimating ice.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Anti-tails and sunward fans are well-documented comet phenomena; Jewitt's HST modeling explains the geometry with large, slow-moving dust grains ejected from the sunlit hemisphere that lag the nucleus, combined with projection effects. AstroWright notes the underlying mechanism has been understood for ~50 years and similar features appear in comets like 17P/Holmes and C/2016 R2.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","https://thedebrief.org/3i-atlas-displays-more-anomalies-as-latest-data-confirms-strange-object-has-developed-an-anti-solar-tail/","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-06","title":"CO2-dominated coma; water only ~4% of plume","date":"2025-08-25","observation":"JWST NIRSpec observations of Aug 6, 2025 at 3.32 AU (Cordiner et al., arXiv:2508.18209) found a carbon-dioxide-dominated gas coma with CO2/H2O = 7.6 +/- 0.3 (~8:1) — among the highest ratios ever observed in any comet, ~4.5 sigma above the trend for solar-system comets (survey median ~0.12). By mass the plume was ~87% CO2, ~9% CO, and only ~4% water.","loeb_take":"Loeb listed the near-absence of water — the defining volatile of familiar comets — as a minor anomaly at P<0.1 in his 13-item likelihood-ranked list (Nov 23, 2025), suggesting the 'gas plume' might not be cometary sublimation at all but sunlight releasing ices and dust accumulated on the surface of a technological object during its interstellar journey. It is not a separate item in his final 22-anomaly list (Mar 12, 2026), compiled after post-perihelion water detections.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The gas plume surrounding 3I/ATLAS contains only 4% water by mass, a dominant constituent of familiar solar-system comets.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","official_explanation":"A CO2-rich nucleus is natural for a body formed near its parent disk's CO2 snowline or processed by galactic cosmic rays over billions of years (arXiv:2510.26308); at >3 AU water sublimates inefficiently while CO2 vents freely. Water/OH production rose strongly closer to the Sun — MeerKAT detected 1665/1667 MHz OH absorption on Oct 24, 2025 (ATel #17473), and SOHO/SWAN measured ~3.2e29 water molecules/s near perihelion — and interstellar comets are expected to show compositional diversity.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18209","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","https://www.universetoday.com/articles/3iatlass-coma-is-largely-carbon-dioxide","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-07","title":"Nickel emission without iron, and nickel far above the coma's ordinary gas","date":"2025-08-28","observation":"VLT/UVES spectroscopy (early Jul-Aug 21, 2025; ~4.4 to 2.85 AU inbound) detected strong atomic nickel (Ni I) emission with iron (Fe I) undetected — by Aug 21 the Ni production rate was 4.6 +/- 0.7 g/s versus CN at 17.6 +/- 2.0 g/s, rising steeply (Q(Ni) proportional to r^-7.7). Keck II/KCWI imaging (Aug 24, published Oct 2025) confirmed the Ni is concentrated within ~600 km of the nucleus (e-folding radius 593.7 +/- 14.8 km vs 841 km for CN). The Ni/CN ratio (~0.1) is orders of magnitude above the solar-system comet median and higher than 2I/Borisov.","loeb_take":"Loeb stressed that release of nickel without iron matches the industrial nickel-refining process via nickel tetracarbonyl, Ni(CO)4 — writing that nickel without iron 'is a signature of industrial production of nickel alloys' and that the carbonyl channel is 'an extremely rare and exotic possibility in comets, whereas it is a standard technology for industrial nickel refining.' He treats the nickel-to-cyanide ratio as a second and separate count against a natural reading: at roughly 0.1 it sits orders of magnitude above the median for solar-system comets and above 2I/Borisov, the only other interstellar comet with comparable measurements. The argument is that the absence of iron says something about the MECHANISM, while the Ni/CN ratio says something about the AMOUNT — that whatever is releasing this nickel is releasing far more of it than ordinary cometary chemistry produces, relative to the gas the coma is otherwise made of.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"is a signature of industrial production of nickel alloys","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'A Steeply-Rising Production of Cyanide and Nickel Without Iron in the Gas Plume Around 3I/ATLAS', Medium, Aug 28, 2025 (verified)","official_explanation":"Ni and Fe emission had already been detected together (at roughly equal abundance) in ~20 solar-system comets and in 2I/Borisov (Manfroid et al. 2021), with low-temperature carbonyl/metal-organic sublimation a leading natural mechanism. For 3I/ATLAS, the observing teams themselves proposed natural in-situ Ni(CO)4 (or Ni-organic/PAH complex) formation and photodissociation near the nucleus — which predicts exactly the observed central concentration — with Fe carbonyl being less volatile and Fe I lines harder to excite/detect at large distances. Fe was subsequently detected as 3I/ATLAS approached the Sun (arXiv 2509.26053), and the Ni/Fe ratio dropped rapidly with decreasing heliocentric distance, trending toward normal comet values. Astronomers (e.g. Jason Wright) call it an extreme but chemically explicable value, not evidence of artificial nickel plating. THE RATIO ARGUMENT HAS A DIFFERENT ANSWER FROM THE IRON ONE, and it turns on the denominator. Ni/CN can be large because there is a great deal of nickel or because there is very little CN, and 3I/ATLAS is independently known to be poor in exactly the gas species that sit in the denominator: its coma is CO2-dominated, with water at only about 4% of the plume, measured over the same inbound stretch. A ratio taken against a depleted denominator is not the same claim as an excess in the numerator, and nothing in these spectra separates the two. Two further features of the measurement cut the same way. The Ni and CN emission do not come from the same volume — nickel is concentrated within an e-folding radius of 593.7 +/- 14.8 km of the nucleus while CN extends to 841 km — so the ratio compares two differently distributed species and partly reports where each is produced rather than how much of each exists. And the whole measurement is heliocentric-distance dependent: these spectra were taken from 4.4 down to 2.85 AU, and the Ni/Fe ratio that looked equally extreme over that same stretch fell rapidly toward ordinary comet values as the object approached the Sun. The extreme numbers live in the far-out regime for both ratios, which is what a volatility-and-excitation effect looks like and is not what a bulk composition looks like. The honest position is that the Ni/CN value is genuinely at the edge of the measured distribution and that the catalog of comets measured this way at these distances is small.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18382","https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11779","https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.26053","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-steeply-rising-production-of-cyanide-and-nickel-without-iron-in-the-gas-plume-around-3i-atlas-536e20674303"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-08","title":"Extreme negative polarization","date":"2025-09-05","observation":"Pre-perihelion polarimetry with FORS2/VLT, ALFOSC/NOT and FoReRo2 (phase angles 7.7-22.4 deg) revealed an unprecedentedly deep, narrow negative polarization branch: minimum -2.7% at 7 deg phase angle with an inversion angle of ~17 deg — a combination never seen in any asteroid or comet, including 2I/Borisov (arXiv:2509.05181, Sept 5, 2025).","loeb_take":"Loeb listed this at P<0.01 as a medium anomaly: light-scattering behavior unprecedented among all known comets, possibly related to the anomalous sunward anti-tail, and another entry in a chain of coincidences whose combined probability he multiplied to argue 3I/ATLAS is 'extremely rare and mysterious'. It stayed as #21 in his final 22-item list.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"The measuring team's own conclusion is natural: the polarization is extreme but continuous with behavior of trans-Neptunian objects and Centaur Pholus, implying fine, possibly water-ice-bearing aggregate dust — evidence 3I is a distinct type of comet, expanding known diversity. AstroWright: negative polarization itself is normal for comets; this is just a stronger case, and no one has proposed why a spacecraft would produce it.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"polarization","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05181","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/extreme-negative-polarization-of-3i-atlas-75d01d13d5c7","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-09","title":"Arrival direction matches the 1977 Wow! Signal","date":"2025-09-28","observation":"Loeb noted that 3I/ATLAS's back-traced position in mid-August 1977 (RA 19h40m = 295 deg, Dec -19 deg) lies ~4 deg in RA and 8 deg in Dec (~9 deg total) from the direction of the famous narrowband 'Wow! Signal' detected by Ohio State's Big Ear telescope on Aug 15, 1977 (RA 19h25m = 291 deg, Dec -27 deg). He put the chance of two random sky directions aligning that closely at ~0.6% (P=0.006).","loeb_take":"In 'Was the Wow! Signal Emitted from 3I/ATLAS?' (Medium, Sept 28, 2025) Loeb speculated the 1977 signal could have been radio emission from 3I/ATLAS itself, calculating that a transmitter at its then-distance of ~600 AU would have needed 0.5-2 gigawatts — 'the output of a typical nuclear reactor on Earth.' He kept the directional coincidence as anomaly #12 of his final 22-item list (March 12, 2026): arrival 'coincident with the radio Wow! Signal to within 9 degrees, with a probability of 0.6%.'","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The chance of two random directions in the sky being aligned to that level is about 0.6 percent.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'Was the Wow! Signal Emitted from 3I/ATLAS?', Medium, Sept 28, 2025","official_explanation":"Critics note 9 degrees is enormous in radio-astronomy terms — many times the Big Ear beam size. Penn State's Jason Wright (AstroWright, Nov 9, 2025) wrote '9 degrees from the Wow! Signal is a long way away!... it very definitely did not come from the direction of the Wow! Signal,' calling the anomaly 'ridiculous.' In 1977 the object was ~600 AU away with no plausible natural emission mechanism, and the Wow! Signal itself is widely attributed to terrestrial interference or a transient natural hydrogen-line source (e.g., Abel Mendez's cold hydrogen cloud hypothesis).","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/was-the-wow-signal-emitted-from-3i-atlas-d18d4f0d1f1e","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://www.newsweek.com/interstellar-object-could-be-mysterious-wow-signal-source-astronomer-10846853","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-10","title":"Non-gravitational acceleration at perihelion","date":"2025-10-31","observation":"JPL's post-perihelion orbit solution (Davide Farnocchia, Oct 30, 2025) required a radial non-gravitational acceleration with A1 ~ 1.6e-6 AU/day^2 normalized at 1 AU — equivalent to a radial acceleration of ~135 km/day^2 (~9e-7 AU/day^2) at the 1.36 AU perihelion distance, plus a transverse component of ~60 km/day^2. As the data arc grew the coefficient dropped: ~4e-7 AU/day^2 by Nov 24, then ~6.8e-8 AU/day^2 under a 1/r^2 (CO2-sublimation) model. In 2026 Loeb continued the thread, arguing (Mar 2026) the acceleration is not directed purely away from the Sun and linking it (Apr 2026) to the object's three-jet system.","loeb_take":"In 'First Evidence for a Non-Gravitational Acceleration of 3I/ATLAS at Perihelion' and 'Afterthoughts...' (both Oct 31, 2025), Loeb calculated that producing the initial acceleration by rocket-effect outgassing would require 3I/ATLAS to shed on the order of a tenth to a sixth of its mass near perihelion (his figure quoted as ~13% in critiques) — at least 5.5 billion tons from a >=33-billion-ton nucleus — which should have produced a massive observable gas cloud by December. Absent that cloud, he argued, the acceleration 'might be regarded as a technological signature' of an internal propulsion system.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"If we do not observe a massive cloud of gas around 3I/ATLAS in December, then the reported non-gravitational acceleration near perihelion might be regarded as a technological signature.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/afterthoughts-on-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-at-perihelion-97c609e24fc6","official_explanation":"Non-gravitational accelerations are standard comet behavior; the measured value shrank more than 20-fold as more astrometry accumulated, and the final magnitude is consistent with ordinary volatile outgassing (arXiv:2511.07450 finds CO-driven outgassing reproduces it with no exotic volatiles needed). Early solutions were inflated by systematic astrometric biases — ~0.5 arcsec tailward coma-photocenter offsets and the October solar-conjunction data gap (arXiv:2603.00782) — and Jason Wright noted that even 13% mass loss near perihelion 'is hardly weird' for a comet.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"acceleration","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/first-evidence-for-a-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-at-perihelion-2698f6a453fe","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/afterthoughts-on-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-at-perihelion-97c609e24fc6","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/updates-on-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-89ee7220c5d7","https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.07450"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-11","title":"Anomalous brightening and blue color at perihelion","date":"2025-10-30","observation":"Analysis of STEREO-A (SECCHI HI1/COR2), SOHO/LASCO C3 and GOES-19 CCOR-1 coronagraph imagery (Zhang, Lowell Observatory & Battams, NRL; arXiv:2510.25035, submitted Oct 28, 2025) showed 3I/ATLAS brightening near perihelion roughly as r^-7.5 (±1.0) — far steeper than typical comets (r^-2 to r^-4) — while its color turned bluer than the Sun, unusual because dust comae normally redden reflected sunlight.","loeb_take":"Loeb flagged it on Oct 30, 2025 as the ninth anomaly on his running list, asking whether the object 'employs a power source hotter than the Sun'. In his Nov 23, 2025 likelihood ranking he placed it in his least-anomalous category at P<0.1: it 'brightened faster than any known comet' while bluer than sunlight, which he suggested might be 'a signature that its engine turned on' near perihelion rather than sunlight-driven sublimation. It appears as item #22 on his final 22-anomaly list (March 12, 2026).","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"Near perihelion, 3I/ATLAS brightened faster than any known comet and was bluer than the Sun","quote_source":"'3I/ATLAS is Fading Away, Leaving Us to Ponder Over Its 22 Mysterious Anomalies', Medium, March 12, 2026 (same wording in 'Anomalies of 3I/ATLAS, Organized by Likelihood', Nov 23, 2025)","official_explanation":"A gas-dominated coma explains both facts: rapid CO2/CO/H2O outgassing close to the Sun drives steep brightening, and gas emission bands (CN, C2, CO+) are intrinsically blue, dominating over reddish dust in broadband coronagraph photometry. Zhang & Battams themselves conclude the blue color is consistent with gas emission contributing a substantial fraction of the visible brightness. Fast-moving interstellar comets get less heating time inbound, so late, sharp activity onsets are expected.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.25035","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-rapidly-brightens-and-gets-bluer-than-the-sun-near-perihelion-3bf100df8390","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","https://skyandtelescope.org/astronomy-news/updates-on-interstellar-comet-3i-atlas-new-images-many-tails-and-non-gravitational-forces/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-12","title":"Extreme mass combined with extreme speed","date":"2025-11-23","observation":"From the smallness of the non-gravitational acceleration (<15 m/day^2, from 4,022 MPC astrometric observations May 15-Sep 23, 2025) given Webb-measured outgassing of ~150 kg/s, Loeb and colleagues derived (arXiv 2509.21408, Sep 25, 2025) a minimum nucleus mass of ~33 billion tons and minimum diameter ~5 km — roughly a million times 'Oumuamua and a thousand times Borisov — while 3I/ATLAS also moves faster than both (hyperbolic excess speed ~58 km/s vs 26 km/s for 'Oumuamua and 32 km/s for Borisov). After perihelion, Loeb revised the mass estimate down to ~1 billion tons (~100,000x 'Oumuamua) once a small non-gravitational acceleration was detected, but kept the mass-speed pairing on his anomaly lists.","loeb_take":"In his Nov 23, 2025 likelihood-ranked list Loeb assigned this P<0.001 (a Category I 'major' anomaly): known reservoirs of interstellar material should not regularly deliver objects ~a million times more massive than 'Oumuamua, and the mass-speed combination compounds the statistical strain. It appears as anomaly #20 of his final 22 (March 12, 2026). The large nucleus mass also feeds his separate 'mass-budget discrepancy' argument (anomaly #1 of the 22): the implied population of 3I-like objects would exceed the heavy-element mass available in interstellar space.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The nucleus of 3I/ATLAS is roughly a million times more massive than 1I/`Oumuamua and a thousand times more massive than 2I/Borisov, while moving faster than both","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","official_explanation":"Jason Wright (AstroWright, Nov 9, 2025) shows the mass floor rests on claiming a non-gravitational-acceleration precision of 1e-10 AU/day^2 when the real precision is ~1e-7 — a thousand-fold error — and notes Loeb's own non-gravitational-acceleration anomaly uses data at the coarser precision ('he can't have it both ways'); mainstream size estimates keep the nucleus km-scale (Hubble upper limit ~2.8 km diameter). The higher speed is separately explained by mainstream kinematics: 3I/ATLAS's velocity is consistent with an origin in the Galaxy's older, dynamically hotter thick-disk population, implying a kinematic age of ~7-10 Gyr — consistent with later isotope evidence of great age.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.21408","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-13","title":"Perijove distance equals Jupiter's Hill radius","date":"2025-11-22","observation":"The predicted minimum distance from Jupiter on Mar 16, 2026 — 53.445 +/- 0.06 million km — matched Jupiter's Hill radius (53.502 million km) to within one standard deviation, about one part in a thousand. Loeb put the probability of the coincidence at less than 0.00004 (about 1 in 26,000, scaled to Jupiter's orbital diameter) and noted the non-gravitational acceleration measured near perihelion shifted the perijove by ~0.1 million km — exactly the course correction needed to produce the match.","loeb_take":"In 'An Extraordinary New Anomaly of 3I/ATLAS' (Nov 22, 2025), Loeb argued a passage precisely at the Hill radius — where Jupiter's Lagrange points L1 and L2 sit — is where an artificial object could release technological devices into bound Jovian orbits with minimal fuel and orbital corrections, and suggested Juno or other human-made orbiters at Jupiter could search for new objects orbiting the planet.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"the level of non-gravitational acceleration was finely tuned","quote_source":"'An Extraordinary New Anomaly of 3I/ATLAS', Medium, November 22, 2025","official_explanation":"Astronomers called the statistics post-hoc numerology: astrophysicists including Scott Manley and David Kipping publicly flagged flaws in Loeb's probability analysis, and critics (e.g. Steve Desch, Jason Wright) noted such 'fine-tuning' probabilities are computed after the fact from one of many possible 'special' distances. The prediction itself dissolved: the actual March 16, 2026 pass occurred at 53.6 million km (0.358330 AU) — slightly outside the Hill radius — with ESA's JUICE observing an ordinary cometary coma with dust and ion tails, and no new satellites of Jupiter were reported.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-remarkable-new-anomaly-of-3i-atlas-420065c2cddf","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/q-a-about-3i-atlas-a-week-before-its-closest-approach-to-jupiter-2d41a2bcccfd","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-14","title":"Dust particle size paradox","date":"2025-12-25","observation":"Sustaining the ~400,000 km sunward anti-tail jet requires dust grains far larger than the sub-micron particles that dominate ordinary comet comae: Loeb brackets them between ~1 and ~100 microns (characteristic radius ~10 microns) — big enough to resist solar radiation pressure, small enough for gas drag to accelerate to jet speeds. Millimeter-scale or larger grains are excluded because the total ejected dust mass needed to produce the observed scattered sunlight would become implausibly large for the nucleus.","loeb_take":"Loeb kept this as anomaly #16 of his final 22-item list (Mar 12, 2026): the glow is dominated by particles much larger than common sub-micron interstellar dust, yet they cannot exceed millimeter size without mass-budget inconsistencies — an anomalous size bracket for a comet. From ~10-micron grains he estimated ~10 million kg of dust shed (~3.3 kg/s) and asked whether 3I/ATLAS accumulated such grains in a molecular cloud.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"3I/ATLAS is therefore anomalous in the size of the particles that dominate the glow of its sunward jet.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-sheds-particles-that-are-much-bigger-than-common-sunlight-scattering-dust-703cc2220df0","official_explanation":"Refereed HST analysis (Jewitt & Luu 2025, ApJL) self-consistently explains the sunward fan that evolved into an anti-sunward tail as large grains (~100 microns) ejected slowly (~5 m/s) — behavior well documented in low-activity and distantly active comets — and an MNRAS study of the physics of cometary anti-tails as observed in 3I/ATLAS reaches similar conclusions. Mass-loss estimates fit cometary norms once realistic size distributions (not single-size grains) are used; grain properties vary widely between comets and no paradox is recognized in the refereed literature.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-sheds-particles-that-are-much-bigger-than-common-sunlight-scattering-dust-703cc2220df0","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-dust-mass-shed-by-3i-atlas-b4ed260addef","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/adf8d8"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-15","title":"Rotation axis aligned with sunward direction","date":"2025-12-16","observation":"A wobbling high-latitude jet observed pre-perihelion (Serra-Ricart, Licandro & Alarcon, arXiv 2512.12819) yielded a spin-axis solution (sky-projected PA 280.7 deg, ~15.48 h rotation period). Loeb inferred from this geometry that the nucleus spin axis was aligned to within 8 degrees of the sunward direction as 3I/ATLAS entered the solar system, and put the probability of a random spin pole landing there at 0.5% (P=0.005). An earlier Loeb & Scarmato coma-morphology analysis had found a looser 10-20 degree alignment (1.5-6% probability).","loeb_take":"First flagged Dec 16, 2025 as the '14th anomaly'; it became anomaly #6 of the final 22. Loeb argued a sun-pointing spin axis gives the object a permanent dayside and nightside, and that if the antipodal jet geometry requires a second sublimating ice pocket placed near the opposite pole with similar precision, the improbability compounds multiplicatively to 0.005 x 0.005 = 2.5e-5 - fine-tuned orientation he suggests is more naturally read as a maintained attitude than a random tumble.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The probability for that is 0.5%.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"Spin-pole solutions derived from fuzzy coma and jet features are highly model-dependent (Loeb's own earlier analysis gave 10-20 degrees, and independent coma-shape fits allowed two different poles), and seasonal activity biases naturally select for apparent sunward alignment since sun-facing active regions dominate observed morphology. A published natural mechanism also exists: torque from asymmetric ice sublimation can drive the spin axis toward the sunward direction, and a ~90-degree obliquity nucleus with a sun-pointed pole naturally explains dayside-dominant sublimation - no design implication required.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-14th-anomaly-of-3i-atlas-alignment-of-its-rotation-axis-with-the-sunward-direction-at-large-e8a08a0705d4","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.12819"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-16","title":"Wobbling high-latitude sunward jet","date":"2025-12-16","observation":"A pre-perihelion study (arXiv:2512.12819, submitted 2025-12-14; Serra-Ricart, Licandro & Alarcon, A&A 705, L3) detected a faint high-latitude jet in the inner coma at PA 280±10 degrees across seven August 2025 nights, with a periodic 7.74±0.35 h modulation consistent with a high-latitude jet precessing around the nucleus spin axis (implied rotation period 15.48±0.70 h). Loeb argued the sunward wobble requires the jet's base to lie within 8 degrees of the sun-facing rotation pole, and assigned that pole-proximate location a probability of 0.5%.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #7 on his 22-anomaly list: the jet's base sits improbably close to the sun-facing pole (0.5%). Combined with the matching post-perihelion coincidence at the opposite pole (anomaly #8), he squares it: 0.5% x 0.5% = 0.000025 (2.5e-5) that sunward anti-tail jets appear both before and after perihelion by chance.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The observed wobble of the pre-perihelion anti-tail jet in the direction of the Sun (as reported here during July and August 2025) requires the base of the jet to be within 8 degrees from the sun-facing pole, with a probability of 0.5%.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"The discovery paper itself treats this as ordinary comet physics: high-latitude vents that wobble with rotation are documented in many comets (e.g., 67P, Hale-Bopp), and a near-polar active region is exactly what sustained sun-facing illumination would activate. The 'independent' probabilities Loeb multiplies are not independent — they describe the same geometry.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.12819","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-anti-tail-of-3i-atlas-wobbled-before-perihelion-3a0d286a6ad3","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-17","title":"Identical collimated jets before and after perihelion","date":"2025-12-17","observation":"After perihelion, 3I/ATLAS re-established a collimated sunward anti-tail jet with essentially the same ~8 degree opening angle and orientation as before perihelion, extending to ~1 million km in Dec 15, 2025 imaging — despite the intervening 16-degree (16.4 deg) gravitational deflection of its trajectory, which Loeb noted is exactly twice the jet's opening angle.","loeb_take":"In his Dec 17, 2025 essay Loeb combined the pre- and post-perihelion pointing likelihoods (0.5% each) into P = 0.5%^2 = 0.000025 (2.5e-5, ~1 in 40,000) — one of the smallest single-coincidence probabilities he quoted for any 3I/ATLAS anomaly — arguing persistent precision pointing through perihelion resembles station-kept thrust. This essay is where he consolidated the count at 15 anomalies (formalized next day in 'The 15 Anomalies of 3I/ATLAS'); he kept the object at rank 4 on his Loeb Classification Scale pending public release of data bracketing the Dec 19 perigee.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The fact that a tightly collimated jet appears as the sunward anti-tail both before and after perihelion (while reversing direction at perihelion relative to the direction of motion), has a tiny probability of occurring at random, equal to the square of 0.5% or 0.000025.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","official_explanation":"A jet tied to the nucleus spin axis SHOULD look similar before and after perihelion: gravity deflects the orbit, not the spin axis, so no independent coincidence exists. Josh Winn (in a Dec 18, 2025 exchange Loeb published) objected that Loeb's statistical questions are constructed after already knowing the observations; Jason Wright added that sunward anti-tails/jets are precedented in comets (17P/Holmes, C/2016 R2, C/1961 R1, explained in the literature decades ago) and that multiplying a-posteriori probabilities is statistically invalid.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-15-anomalies-of-3i-atlas-should-we-pay-attention-to-them-if-they-were-not-forecasted-77375f9974d5","https://www.ibtimes.co.uk/avi-loeb-keeps-3i-atlas-rank-4-suspense-builds-new-data-looms-1764870"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-18","title":"Jet base 'insulation' on the nightside","date":"2025-12-17","observation":"Because 3I/ATLAS's rotation axis is nearly aligned with the sunward direction, each anti-tail jet's launch base sits on the nightside for months at a stretch (the post-perihelion jet's base was on the nightside before perihelion, and vice versa), yet the jets are active only while their base faces the Sun.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #9 of Loeb's 22-item wrap-up list (March 2026; first argued as point #8 in his Dec 17, 2025 essay): for the bases to stay dormant through months of darkness they must be extremely well insulated, but heat should naturally conduct throughout a comet's body — so, per Loeb, the on/off pattern hints at engineered surfaces or controlled venting rather than passive ice.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"For these bases to be active only when facing the Sun, they must be well insulated on the nightside for a period longer than several months. However, heat would naturally flow by conduction throughout the body of a natural comet, making this insulation requirement difficult to satisfy.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, '3I/ATLAS Maintained a Sunward Jet After Its Gravitational Deflection by 16 Degrees at Perihelion', Medium, Dec 17, 2025 (repeated as anomaly #9 in the Mar 12, 2026 22-anomaly list)","official_explanation":"Dayside-dominant activity is textbook comet behavior, and real cometary crusts are naturally superb insulators: Rosetta's MIRO/VIRTIS instruments measured 67P's thermal inertia at roughly 10-60 J m^-2 K^-1 s^-1/2 — a powdery, thermally insulating surface where solar heat penetrates only centimeters. Heat conduction through a porous nucleus is extremely inefficient, contradicting the premise that warmth should spread to the nightside; no exotic insulation is required. No agency-level rebuttal addresses this numbered claim specifically, but the underlying thermophysics is standard.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2018/08/aa33104-18/aa33104-18.html"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-19","title":"Gravitational deflection exactly twice the jet opening angle","date":"2025-12-17","observation":"In a Dec 17, 2025 essay, Loeb calculated that solar gravity bent 3I/ATLAS's velocity vector by ~16 degrees (2GM/(b*v^2) = 0.286 rad = 16.4 deg) at perihelion, and noted this is almost exactly twice the ~8-degree opening angle of the narrow sunward anti-tail jet measured in pre-perihelion imaging; Hubble images from Dec 12 and 27, 2025 showed the sunward jet persisting after the deflection.","loeb_take":"Loeb flagged the 2:1 ratio as one more geometric coincidence in his anomaly ledger — it became anomaly #10 of the final 22 (Geometric Coincidences) in his March 12, 2026 wrap-up essay. He also used the geometry constructively: a jet wobbling within an ~8-degree cone about a fixed spin axis could produce the sunward anti-tail before perihelion and a counter-jet after it.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The gravitational deflection of 3I/ATLAS by 16 degrees at perihelion, is exactly twice the opening angle of the anti-tail jet.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","official_explanation":"No mainstream astronomer published a rebuttal to this specific angle ratio. The general response to Loeb's coincidence claims (e.g., Jason Wright's critiques of his fine-tuning statistics) applies: the two angles arise from unrelated physics — the 16-degree deflection is routine orbital mechanics for any body with that perihelion distance and speed, while the jet opening angle is a soft, method-dependent measurement — and comparing enough angle pairs post hoc guarantees some near-integer ratios.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-20","title":"SPHEREx icy-grain signature disappearance","date":"2026-01-11","observation":"SPHEREx detected strong water-ice absorption in the coma pre-perihelion (Aug 2025, arXiv:2508.15469), but on re-observation in December 2025 the H2O-ice spectral signature had mostly disappeared while activity rose sharply — H2O gas emission ~40x brighter — and new coma species appeared (CN, organic C-H, CO2, CO) along with refractory organo-silicaceous dust (arXiv:2601.06759, Lisse et al., RNAAS, posted 2026-01-11).","loeb_take":"Loeb listed the vanishing ice signature plus newly appearing gas-phase organics (CH3OH, H2CO, CH4, C2H6) and a ~20x jump in water production as anomaly #17 of the 22-anomaly list in his March 2026 'fading away' essay. [paraphrase] He argued the transformation was not predicted in advance, and that organics surviving cosmic-ray bombardment over an interstellar journey would require burial under at least 10 meters of material.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"the spectral signature of ice disappeared in the SPHEREx data after perihelion","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, Medium, '3I/ATLAS is Fading Away, Leaving Us to Ponder Over Its 22 Mysterious Anomalies' (March 2026)","official_explanation":"The SPHEREx team describes expected post-perihelion evolution: solar heating sublimated the icy grains (removing the ice absorption signature) and drove the nucleus into much higher activity, releasing additional gas species. The paper's abstract describes marked evidence for development into a cometary body fully sublimating all its ices; press coverage called it a 'full cometary awakening'.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.06759","https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15469","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://phys.org/news/2026-01-spherex-imaging-reveals-sublimation-3iatlas.html"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-21","title":"Rare Sun-Earth axis alignment (Jan 22, 2026)","date":"2026-01-13","observation":"On Jan 22, 2026, 3I/ATLAS crossed the Sun-Earth axis at a phase angle of just 0.69 degrees (near-perfect opposition), with the phase angle below 2 degrees for about a week (Jan 19-26) and the anti-tail direction projected toward Earth at the moment of alignment.","loeb_take":"Loeb (with Mauro Barbieri, arXiv:2601.08624) announced the alignment on Jan 13, 2026 primarily as a 'narrow but well-defined observational window' to measure the opposition surge and probe whether the dust is pristine (fluffy fractal aggregates) or thermally processed; in his final March 2026 recap he then catalogued it as anomaly #11 of 22 — the alignment with the anti-tail pointing at Earth joining his chain of improbable geometric coincidences.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The unique near-opposition geometry of 3I/ATLAS on 22 January, 2026 provides a narrow but well-defined observational window.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-rare-alignment-of-3i-atlas-with-the-sun-earth-axis-on-22-january-2026-b1bea4a553f7","official_explanation":"Opposition alignments are a function of Earth's orbital motion, not of the object: every outer body passes near the Sun-Earth axis roughly once per synodic period (what was unusual here was only the very small 0.69-degree minimum phase angle). Mainstream astronomers used the same window productively — Hubble imaged 3I/ATLAS during the alignment, and coordinated photometry measured an opposition surge of about 0.2 mag with ~3-degree width and a linear phase slope of ~0.026 mag/deg, values consistent with ordinary cometary dust. Calling Earth's own viewing geometry an anomaly of the object is a category error.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"timing","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-rare-alignment-of-3i-atlas-with-the-sun-earth-axis-on-22-january-2026-b1bea4a553f7","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08624","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/hubble-images-of-3i-atlas-during-its-rare-alignment-with-the-sun-earth-axis-on-january-22-2026-7ee2c20a70f0"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-22","title":"Methane volatility puzzle","date":"2026-01-29","observation":"Methane (CH4) was first detected in the coma only after perihelion — JWST/MIRI spectra on 2025 Dec 15-16 and 27 (2.20 and 2.54 au outbound) measured CH4 at 13.7% and 27% of the water production rate — despite CH4's volatility lying between CO2 and CO, both of which were detected well before perihelion (JWST/SPHEREx, Aug 2025, saw strong CO2 and CO but no CH4). Under simple sublimation ordering, CH4 should have appeared alongside them inbound.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #19 of his final 22-anomaly list (the 'Possible Bio-Signature' item): the out-of-order appearance of volatiles violates the layered-ice sublimation sequence expected for a natural comet, suggesting to Loeb that the plume chemistry may not be simple ice sublimation — he floated abiotic (deep-buried CH4) and speculative biological interpretations.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The volatility of methane (CH4) is between that of carbon dioxide (CO2) and carbon monoxide (CO), yet methane was detected in the gas plume around 3I/ATLAS only after perihelion, while CO2 and CO were detected long before perihelion.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"Comet nuclei are heterogeneous: the JWST/MIRI team (Belyakov et al. 2026) attributes the late CH4 onset to past depletion of methane from the outermost processed layers, with the observed CH4 emerging from relatively unprocessed subsurface material once perihelion heating reached deeper layers — or to compositional heterogeneity of the nucleus combined with seasonal illumination effects. Volatile release order varies among well-studied solar-system comets, so a late CH4 onset is within natural diversity.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22034","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-massive-nucleus-of-3i-atlas-and-its-puzzling-methane-outgassing-based-on-new-data-from-the-72e540d8fe46","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-23","title":"Three symmetric mini-jets at 120 degrees","date":"2026-01-19","observation":"Larson-Sekanina rotational-gradient filtering of Hubble Space Telescope images (key frames 2026-01-14; pattern persistent in frames from Nov 30, Dec 4, 12, 27, 2025 and Jan 7, 2026) removed the circular coma glow and revealed three mini-jets around the nucleus separated from each other by approximately 120 degrees in sky projection, plus a fourth prominent sunward anti-tail jet extending about 10 times farther. Loeb reported the jet structure wobbles by +/-20 degrees with a 7.1-hour period.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #13 of the final 22: Loeb highlighted the engineered look of three jets 'symmetrically separated by 120 degrees' from each other, noting none points anti-sunward as a normal tail would, and on 2026-04-16 published an essay (with Toni Scarmato) linking this three-jet system to the measured non-gravitational acceleration via rocket-effect thrust — discussing whether the jets are 'technological thrusters or pockets of ice' and citing a proposed 3+1-thruster configuration as an optimal steering architecture.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The 3 mini-jets are separated by 120 degrees from each other in sky projection","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/are-the-three-mini-jets-coming-out-of-3i-atlas-at-120-degree-separation-a-technological-signature-ba748a017aa0","official_explanation":"Multiple discrete vents are common on comet nuclei (Rosetta imaged dozens on 67P), and apparent regular spacing is a projection and small-number effect: with three jets, some near-symmetric arrangement is unremarkable. Jet-driven torques and acceleration from asymmetric outgassing are standard comet dynamics.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/are-the-three-mini-jets-coming-out-of-3i-atlas-at-120-degree-separation-a-technological-signature-ba748a017aa0","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/linking-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-to-its-symmetric-system-of-three-jets-847859455a16","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-24","title":"Sideways (non-radial) non-gravitational acceleration","date":"2026-03-03","observation":"A February 2026 reanalysis of the orbit fits (Spada, Krolikowska & Dones, arXiv:2603.00782) showed the non-gravitational acceleration was not purely directed away from the Sun (as rocket-effect sublimation predicts to first order): Loeb highlighted that the tangential component was comparable in magnitude to the radial one, whereas NASA/JPL's fit had the radial component about 5x the tangential — itself well above the ~0.03-0.05 transverse-to-radial ratio typical of solar-system comets.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #14 of his 22-anomaly list (March 12, 2026): a sideways push is hard to get from sun-driven sublimation but natural for directed thrust; in his April 2026 analysis with Toni Scarmato (arXiv:2604.18773) Loeb connected it to the symmetric three-jet system, noting the jets could be 'technological thrusters or pockets of ice' — i.e., a candidate steering mechanism.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The non-gravitational acceleration of 3I/ATLAS was not directed away from the Sun but had a substantial sideways component","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"Standard cometary NGA models (Marsden A1/A2/A3) have always included transverse and normal components, produced by rotation-lagged outgassing — sublimation peaks in the comet 'afternoon', displacing thrust from the sunward line. The mainstream rebuttal (Spada et al. 2026) is that the fitted transverse component A2 is the least stable parameter — highly sensitive to data selection, parameter correlations, and orbital phase coverage — while total acceleration estimates agree with JPL's solution and require no exotic physics.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"acceleration","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-is-not-directed-away-from-the-sun-f478b82f7175","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/linking-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-to-its-symmetric-system-of-three-jets-847859455a16","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00782"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-25","title":"Anomalous isotope abundances (D/H, 12C/13C, 14N/15N)","date":"2026-03-10","observation":"JWST (Cordiner et al.) reported deuterium-enriched water with D/H more than ten times higher than known comets, and 12C/13C of 141-191 (CO2) and 123-172 (CO), above typical solar-system values; VLT (Opitom et al.) found 12C/13C = 147 (+87/-40) and 14N/15N = 343 (+454/-124), over twice the ~150 typical of solar-system comets.","loeb_take":"In 'New Anomalies in the Isotope Abundances of 3I/ATLAS' (Mar 10, 2026) Loeb accepted the papers' inference of formation below 30 K in a metal-poor environment 10-12 billion years ago, but argued that ancient low-metallicity stellar populations cannot supply enough rocky material — they 'miss the required mass budget by at least three orders of magnitude'. The same day he reduced 3I/ATLAS to rank 3 on the Loeb scale, conditional on an uneventful Jupiter pass.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"miss the required mass budget by at least three orders of magnitude","quote_source":"'New Anomalies in the Isotope Abundances of 3I/ATLAS', Medium, March 10, 2026","official_explanation":"The measuring teams interpret the isotopes as strong evidence for a natural, extremely ancient comet formed at freezing temperatures early in the Milky Way's history — the isotopic signature of great age, not technology. The mass-budget objection depends on Loeb's contested large-mass estimate; for a km-scale nucleus no reservoir problem arises.","loeb_scale":3,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/new-anomalies-in-the-isotope-abundances-of-3i-atlas-1bb3790207f1","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/q-a-about-3i-atlas-a-week-before-its-closest-approach-to-jupiter-2d41a2bcccfd"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-26","title":"Extreme methanol enrichment (CH3OH/HCN) measured by ALMA","date":"2025-09-15","observation":"Roth, Cordiner and colleagues used the Atacama Compact Array of ALMA to detect methanol toward 3I/ATLAS on 2025 August 28, September 18 and 22, and October 1, and hydrogen cyanide on September 12 and 15 — pre-perihelion, at heliocentric distances from 2.6 down to 1.7 AU. Three results came out of it. The two molecules outgassed DIFFERENTLY: HCN production was depleted in the sunward hemisphere of the coma while methanol was enhanced in that same direction. Methanol showed production from coma sources beyond 258 km from the nucleus at 99% confidence, consistent with release from icy grains behaving as miniature comets, though low signal-to-noise on the long baselines that probe the smallest scales stopped the authors from ruling out methanol as a purely nucleus-derived species. HCN, by contrast, was indistinguishable from direct sublimation off the nucleus. And the methanol production rate rose sharply from August through October, with an uptick near the inner edge of the water sublimation zone at 2 AU. The headline number is the ratio: CH3OH/HCN of 124 (+30/-34) on September 12 and 79 (+11/-14) on September 15 — among the most methanol-enriched values measured in any comet at radio wavelengths.","loeb_take":"The enrichment is read as a fuel signature rather than a chemistry result. In the strongest version of the argument, methanol is the propellant of a magnetohydrodynamic drive: it ionises readily into plasma, it can be seeded with the nickel that optical spectroscopy detected, and an MHD thruster would explain in one stroke the collimated sunward jets, the three mini-jets spaced at 120 degrees, and the sideways component of the non-gravitational acceleration. The sunward enhancement is taken as pointing the same way — exhaust directed at the Sun rather than volatiles boiling off it. Combined with the elevated deuterium as reactor fuel, the picture offered is a self-contained propulsion package: deuterium to burn, methanol to throw, nickel alloys in the exhaust.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"Methanol is the perfect propellant here, and ALMA proved that it's bursting with it.","quote_source":"ANGRY ASTRONAUT · YOUTUBE 2026-03-16 · TRANSCRIBED FROM AUTO-CAPTIONS, NOT A PUBLISHED TEXT","official_explanation":"The measurement is real, was published in a refereed letter, and the enrichment is genuinely extreme. What it is not is unprecedented — and the paper says so itself in the same sentence. The one object that beats 3I/ATLAS on this measure is C/2016 R2 (PanSTARRS), a comet that formed in OUR solar system. If a native comet can be more methanol-enriched than the interstellar visitor, then this ratio cannot by itself mark an object as foreign, let alone as manufactured. Three further points come straight from the data. First, CH3OH/HCN is a RATIO, and it rises just as readily when HCN is scarce as when methanol is abundant — 3I/ATLAS was already known to be depleted in nitrogen-bearing volatiles, which lifts the quotient without requiring anything unusual in the numerator. Second, the two values are one noisy quantity measured three days apart: 124 (+30/-34) and 79 (+11/-14) overlap inside their own error bars, so quoting them as a span from 70 to 120 reports the scatter as if it were a trend. Third, the extended methanol source is textbook cometary physics rather than an exhaust plume — icy grains lifted into the coma, warmed by sunlight, releasing gas as they go, which is exactly why methanol appears enhanced on the sunward side where the grains are hottest, while HCN coming straight off the nucleus does not. The sharp rise from August to October tracks the inner edge of the water sublimation zone at 2 AU, which is where a sublimation-driven increase is supposed to happen. The honest residue is that this composition is rare and interesting: an object mixed in a different environment from ours, carrying a volatile inventory we have essentially one solar-system analogue for. That is a strong statement about where it formed. It is not evidence of a propellant tank.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20845","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ae433b","https://public.nrao.edu/news/alma-detects-extremely-abundant-alcohol-in-interstellar-comet-3i-atlas/","https://www.almaobservatory.org/en/press-releases/alma-detects-extremely-abundant-alcohol-in-interstellar-comet-3i-atlas/"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-01","title":"Origin at the Local Standard of Rest (the 'buoy at rest')","date":"2017-10-31","observation":"Before entering the solar system, 'Oumuamua was moving at only ~10 km/s relative to the Local Standard of Rest (LSR): its heliocentric velocity was (U,V,W) = (-11.5, -22.4, -7.7) km/s (26.32 km/s total), which in the LSR frame leaves negligible radial (U) and vertical (W) galactic motion and only a ~11 km/s lag in the rotational (V) component. Mamajek (arXiv:1710.11364, submitted Oct 31, 2017) computed that its velocity sat within 5 km/s of the median UVW velocity of local field stars and that fewer than ~1 in 500 field stars in the solar neighborhood have velocities so close to that local median (essentially the LSR). The Sun itself moves faster relative to the LSR (~18 km/s peculiar motion) than 'Oumuamua did.","loeb_take":"Loeb argued this is exactly where you would park an artificial object meant to survey passing stars — or to hide its origin: in 'Six Strange Facts about 'Oumuamua' (arXiv:1811.08832, Nov 2018) he noted only one star in 500 moves as slowly in the LSR frame and called the LSR 'the ideal frame for camouflage,' avoiding association with any particular star. In 'Extraterrestrial' (2021) he counted the ~1-in-500 kinematic coincidence among the improbabilities any natural ejection model must explain, since ejected rocks inherit their parent star's velocity plus an ejection kick — 'Oumuamua was effectively at rest in the galactic frame and the solar system ran into it.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"Perhaps 'Oumuamua was like a buoy resting in the expanse of the universe, and our solar system was like a ship that ran into it at high speed.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'Extraterrestrial' (2021) — verbatim; same buoy-and-ship analogy repeated in 2019-2021 interviews. The LSR/1-in-500 'camouflage' argument is in 'Six Strange Facts about 'Oumuamua' (arXiv:1811.08832; Scientific American, Nov 20, 2018).","official_explanation":"Young objects ejected at low velocity from nearby young stellar systems naturally cluster near the LSR before gravitational scattering ('disk heating') ages their kinematics; the low LSR velocity implies 'Oumuamua is dynamically young (<~100 Myr — Hallatt & Wiegert 2020, arXiv:1911.02473, AJ; Hsieh, Laughlin & Arce 2021 linked it to the ~30 Myr Carina moving group and a molecular-cloud origin, arXiv:2105.14670, ApJ). The ISSI team (Nature Astronomy 3, 594, 2019) treated the kinematics as pointing to a young planetesimal population, not design.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.11364","https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08832","https://www.scientificamerican.com/blog/observations/6-strange-facts-about-the-interstellar-visitor-oumuamua/","https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02473"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-02","title":"Extreme lightcurve elongation (~10x brightness swings)","date":"2017-11-20","observation":"Meech et al. (Nature 552, 378; published 2017-11-20 with ESO release eso1737) reported brightness variations by a factor of ~10 (about 2.5 magnitudes) every ~7.3 hours as the object rotated. The original fit implied an axis ratio of up to ~10:1; later reanalyses accounting for phase-angle effects put the most likely aspect ratio at ~6:1 (6±1) — still more elongated than any of the hundreds of thousands of asteroids and comets ever measured in the solar system (typical bodies are ~2:1 or 3:1).","loeb_take":"Loeb repeatedly listed the record-setting elongation as strange fact number one: no known solar-system body comes close, and an extreme aspect ratio is what an artificial thin structure (cigar or disc seen edge-on) would produce. In 'Six Strange Facts' (arXiv:1811.08832, also published in Scientific American on 2018-11-20) he noted the shape inference, combined with the later acceleration anomaly, pushed him toward a thin-membrane geometry rather than a rocky ellipsoid.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Natural shaping mechanisms proposed: tidal fragmentation of a body during a close stellar or planetary encounter can spin off highly elongated splinters (Zhang & Lin, Nature Astronomy, April 2020, reproduced ~10:1 and even more elongated fragments); prolonged interstellar-dust erosion or collisional grinding can also elongate small bodies. The ISSI team (Nature Astronomy 3, 594, 2019) called the shape unusual but consistent with a purely natural origin, noting that lightcurve amplitude is enhanced at the observed phase angles (19-27 deg), softening the required ratio to ~6:1; McNeill et al. (2018) independently derived 6±1 from lightcurve inversion, and Vazan & Sari (MNRAS 493, 1546, 2020) argued irregular surface reflection properties could mimic part of the amplitude, allowing an even less elongated body.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/nature25020","https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1737/","https://arxiv.org/pdf/1811.08832","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-019-0816-x"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-03","title":"Complex tumbling (non-principal-axis rotation)","date":"2017-12-01","observation":"Photometry from multiple telescopes could not be phased to a single rotation period; Fraser et al. (Nature Astronomy, arXiv 2017-11-30, published 2018-02-09) and Drahus et al. (Nature Astronomy 2018, Gemini North data) showed 'Oumuamua is tumbling — rotating in an excited non-principal-axis state with lightcurve periodicities near ~7.3-8.7 h (Drahus: ~7.34 h average; Belton: 8.67 h). Belton et al. (ApJL 856, L21, April 2018) formalized the spin-state families from 818 observations over 29.3 days. Fraser et al. computed a damping timescale for the tumbling of at least a billion years, so it likely tumbled through interstellar space since a violent event (e.g. a collision) long before reaching us.","loeb_take":"Loeb counted the excited spin as another oddity constraining natural origins (a collision or fragmentation is needed to set it tumbling) and argued the tumbling made the outgassing explanation for the acceleration harder: citing Rafikov (2018), he noted that cometary jets should have torqued the object and rapidly changed its spin — Rafikov showed outgassing strong enough to supply the acceleration would drive spin evolution within days (even rotational breakup) unless the jet's torque lever arm was finely tuned, yet no spin change or sudden kicks were observed. Separately, Loeb cited Mashchenko's (2019) lightcurve-torque modeling approvingly for its conclusion that the shape is disc-like at ~91% confidence — though Mashchenko's own best fit (a thin disc under moderate outgassing torque of comet-like amplitude) actually supports the natural-comet reading.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"showing no change in spin or sudden kicks as routinely observed from localized jets","quote_source":"Loeb, 'On the Possibility of an Artificial Origin for `Oumuamua', Astrobiology review (lweb.cfa.harvard.edu/~loeb/Loeb_Astrobiology.pdf), citing Rafikov 2018","official_explanation":"Tumbling is common among small solar-system bodies after collisions; Fraser et al. attributed it to a collision in 'Oumuamua's birth system before or during ejection. Belton et al. showed several spin states fit the data, including ones consistent with either cigar or pancake shapes. Rafikov (2018) argued the observed spin stability limits any outgassing torques — a point against the comet interpretation — but later modeling (Mashchenko 2019; Seligman et al.) showed outgassing concentrated at the substellar point can supply the acceleration while leaving the tumbling period essentially unchanged.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-018-0398-z","https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.11530","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-018-0440-1","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/aab370"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-04","title":"Breakthrough Listen radio silence","date":"2017-12-13","observation":"On 2017-12-13 (3:45-9:45 pm ET) Breakthrough Listen observed 'Oumuamua with the Green Bank Telescope across four bands (L, S, X, C; 1.1-11.6 GHz), accumulating ~90 TB of raw data over the 2-hour on-source observation plus calibration scans. No artificial narrowband signals were found. The published analysis (Enriquez et al. 2018, Res. Notes AAS 2, 9) set limits sensitive to omnidirectional transmitters with power as low as ~0.08 W — a fraction of a cellphone — at the object's distance.","loeb_take":"Loeb (who chairs Breakthrough Starshot's advisory committee) endorsed the search and treated the null result as consistent with, not fatal to, the artifact hypothesis: a defunct piece of technological debris or a passive lightsail need not transmit. In 'Extraterrestrial' he argued most artifacts we encounter would likely be dead 'space junk' of other civilizations, so radio silence carries limited evidential weight.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"The mainstream reading is straightforward: a natural rock does not transmit, and the deep non-detection removed the most testable technosignature prediction. The SETI Institute's Allen Telescope Array had already observed it 2017-11-23 to 12-05 (Harp et al. 2019, Acta Astronautica) and independently found no signals down to ~10 W EIRP at 1 GHz (rising to ~100 W at 7 GHz), and the ISSI team's Nature Astronomy review found no compelling evidence for an artificial origin, citing the radio quiet as consistent with a natural body.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://breakthroughinitiatives.org/news/15","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2515-5172/aaa6c9","https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.02814","https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.09355"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-05","title":"Population density too high for ejected rocks","date":"2018-03-06","observation":"Scaling from Pan-STARRS's single detection, Do, Tucker & Tonry (ApJL 855, L10, published 2018-03-06) inferred a space density of 'Oumuamua-like objects of ~0.2 per AU^3 — a mass density of ~4 Earth masses of ejecta per cubic parsec, requiring each star to account for over 10^16 ejected bodies. The required planetesimal disk mass exceeds all estimates of the solar system's own planetesimal disk by an order of magnitude; Loeb noted the density is 2-8 orders of magnitude above the pre-detection prediction of Moro-Martin, Turner & Loeb (2009), and rounded the requirement to ~10^15 (a quadrillion) objects per star.","loeb_take":"Loeb ('Six Strange Facts', arXiv:1811.08832, and book 'Extraterrestrial') argued each star would need to eject ~10^15 'Oumuamua-like objects — 2-8 orders of magnitude more than he and Moro-Martin had predicted in 2009 — an implausible budget if they are random rocks. A random population of 10^15 solar sails per star is equally unreasonable, so he argued targeted trajectories 'add explanatory power': far fewer artifacts are needed if they aim for the habitable zones of stars rather than sampling all orbits randomly. Separately, Siraj & Loeb (New Astronomy 2022) ran the same mass-budget attack against the nitrogen-iceberg model, and Loeb repeatedly cited Moro-Martin's 2018-2019 finding that ejection scenarios fall short.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"Each star in the Milky-Way needs to eject 10^15 such objects during its lifetime to account for the inferred population, much more than anticipated based on the Solar System.","quote_source":"Loeb, 'Six Strange Facts about 'Oumuamua', arXiv:1811.08832 (Nov 2018; also Scientific American, 2018-11-20)","official_explanation":"The ISSI team (Bannister et al., Nature Astronomy 2019) countered that with a single detected object there are no firm constraints on the size-frequency distribution, so the measured number density cannot be converted to a mass density: plausible SFDs with a few percent of mass in ~50 m fragments match the observed density with only 0.02-15 Earth masses ejected per star, making the 'higher than expected' claim unsupported. Do et al. themselves proposed Oort-cloud shedding by post-main-sequence stars as a supplementary source; Zhang & Lin (2020) and Desch & Jackson (2021) each predict abundant small fragments. Moro-Martin (2018, 2019), however, found the tension persists under many assumptions — the debate hinged on the unknown size distribution, which detections of 2I and 3I are only now starting to constrain (km-scale ISOs are far rarer, ~10^-4 to 10^-2 per AU^3).","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/aaae67","https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.02821","https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08832","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-019-0816-x"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-06","title":"Non-gravitational acceleration with no visible coma or tail","date":"2018-06-27","observation":"Micheli et al. (Nature 559, 223; published 2018-06-27) detected, at ~30-sigma significance, a non-gravitational acceleration of 'Oumuamua of 4.92 +/- 0.16 x 10^-6 m/s^2 at 1 AU, pointed radially away from the Sun and falling off roughly as 1/r^2. Yet deep imaging (VLT, HST, Gemini) and Spitzer showed no coma, no dust tail, and no detected gas — the usual engine of such accelerations in comets. Explaining it with outgassing would need ~10 kg/s of gas with a gas-to-dust ratio >100x any known comet.","loeb_take":"This is the anomaly that launched the artifact hypothesis: an excess sunward-decaying push with no visible propellant is precisely the signature of solar radiation pressure on a thin, low-mass-per-area object. Loeb called the combination of acceleration plus no coma the single most anomalous fact about 'Oumuamua, and in later essays assigned the natural-comet explanation a low likelihood given the additional fine-tuning (no spin-up torque, no infrared detection) it requires.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Micheli et al. themselves concluded comet-like outgassing is physically viable if 'Oumuamua has unusual thermal/compositional properties (large grains, low dust). The ISSI team (Nature Astronomy 2019) endorsed volatile outgassing below detection thresholds. Dedicated exotic-ice models followed: hydrogen iceberg (Seligman & Laughlin, ApJL 896, L8, 2020), nitrogen-ice fragment (Desch & Jackson, JGR Planets, 2021), and radiolytic H2 trapped in water ice (Bergner & Seligman, Nature 615, 610, March 2023). A minority view (Katz 2019, arXiv:1904.02218) disputed the acceleration itself, but the 30-sigma detection is broadly accepted.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"acceleration","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-018-0254-4","https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.02218","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-019-0816-x","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-022-05687-w"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-07","title":"Bialy-Loeb lightsail hypothesis (the 0.3-0.9 mm sail)","date":"2018-11-12","observation":"Bialy & Loeb (ApJL 868, L1; arXiv 2018-10-26, published 2018-11-12) showed that solar radiation pressure alone reproduces the observed 1/r^2 excess acceleration (Micheli et al. 2018) if 'Oumuamua's mass-to-area ratio is ~0.1 g/cm^2 - i.e., a thin sheet only ~0.3-0.9 mm thick for normal material densities. They calculated such a thin film would survive interstellar dust/gas ablation and tidal/centrifugal (tensile) stresses over galactic distances.","loeb_take":"The paper offered two readings: a naturally produced ultra-thin object of a new class, or an artificial origin - 'a lightsail, floating in interstellar space as debris from advanced technological equipment' - with a 'more exotic' alternative that it was a deliberate probe. Loeb expanded this into 'Extraterrestrial' (Jan 2021), arguing the artifact hypothesis was the most economical explanation of all the anomalies jointly and publicly calling artificial origin a 'serious possibility'; in his August 2025 Loeb Scale paper (arXiv:2508.09167) he retroactively ranked 'Oumuamua Level 4 ('critical threshold' - technological explanation must be considered), versus Level 0 for 2I/Borisov and Level 4 for 3I/ATLAS.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"Alternatively, a more exotic scenario is that 'Oumuamua may be a fully operational probe sent intentionally to Earth vicinity by an alien civilization.","quote_source":"Bialy & Loeb 2018, ApJL 868, L1 ('Could Solar Radiation Pressure Explain 'Oumuamua's Peculiar Acceleration?'), Section 5 - exact sentence verified against the published text","official_explanation":"Curran (A&A 649, L17, 2021, ''Oumuamua as a light sail: Evidence against artificial origin') showed light sails asymptotically approach a terminal velocity, so a sail with 'Oumuamua's parameters (thickness <~1 mm, mass ~4x10^4 kg) would need roughly half a billion years just to reach the Sun from its closest likely system of origin - making an artificial sail very unlikely. Zhou et al. (A&A 667, A108, 2022) added that a tumbling sail is inconsistent with the data: <1.5% probability of matching the observed lightcurve amplitude and ~0.4% probability of remaining visible in all 55 post-discovery observations. The ISSI team (Nature Astronomy, 2019) found all observations consistent with a purely natural origin, and porous fractal-aggregate models (Moro-Martin 2019; Luu, Flekkoy & Toussaint 2020) offered a natural route to lightsail-like area-to-mass ratios.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"acceleration","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/aaeda8","https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11490","https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2021/05/aa41283-21/aa41283-21.html","https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/abs/2022/11/aa44119-22/aa44119-22.html"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-08","title":"Spitzer infrared non-detection: small and shiny","date":"2018-11-14","observation":"Spitzer stared at 'Oumuamua's position for 30.2 hours at 4.5 microns (IRAC channel 2) on 2017-11-21/22 UT and saw nothing, with a 3-sigma flux upper limit of 0.3 microJy (Trilling et al., AJ 156, 261; published 2018-11-14). This caps the effective spherical diameter at <98, <140, or <440 m (for low/middle/high thermal beaming) and forces the albedo above 0.2, 0.1, or 0.01 respectively — plus tight 3-sigma upper limits on outgassing: CO2 < 9e22 and CO < ~9e21 molecules/s.","loeb_take":"Loeb highlighted that the preferred solutions make 'Oumuamua small and at least ~10x more reflective than typical solar-system comets and asteroids — consistent with a polished metallic or icy-clean artificial surface — while the CO/CO2 limits simultaneously starve the outgassing explanation for the acceleration of its most common propellants. Smaller size also means the non-gravitational push acted on a lower-mass object, easing the radiation-pressure requirement.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Trilling et al. proposed that if 'Oumuamua is a comet-like body, low-level post-perihelion volatile emission after its 0.256 AU perihelion passage could have deposited a fresh, bright icy mantle, explaining the high albedo; water vapor (invisible at 4.5 microns, ~6e25 molecules/s needed) could still drive the acceleration. The nitrogen-ice model (Jackson & Desch 2021) naturally yields a high albedo (~0.64, N2 frost like Pluto's surface) and a small body (~45 x 44 x 7.5 m), fitting Spitzer's limits, as does the hydrogen-iceberg model.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/aae88f","https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08072","https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasa-learns-more-about-interstellar-visitor-oumuamua/","https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2020JE006706"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-09","title":"Pancake, not cigar: the 91% oblate fit","date":"2019-06-09","observation":"Mashchenko (MNRAS 489, 3003; arXiv:1906.03696, submitted 9 June 2019) fit the full October-November 2017 lightcurve with a physical tumbling-plus-torque model and found disc-shaped (oblate) bodies reproduce the deep brightness minima with 91% likelihood, versus only 16% for cigar shapes, which additionally suffer an orientation fine-tuning problem. The best-fit body is a thin slab of ~115 x 111 x 19 m — an aspect ratio near 6:6:1 (assumed geometric albedo 0.1) — under a modest outgassing torque whose amplitude, relative to the non-gravitational acceleration, matches Solar System comets.","loeb_take":"Loeb seized on this: the popular cigar was an artist's conception, while the data statistically favor a flat pancake at 91% confidence — far closer to a sail geometry. In essays and in 'Extraterrestrial' he emphasized that a razor-thin disc is even harder to make naturally than a cigar (Mashchenko's fits even allow a millimeter-thin sheet), and — following Rafikov's 2018 argument that outgassing torques should have measurably spun the object up — that the required torque balance further strains the invisible-outgassing story. The 2025 Loeb Scale paper (arXiv:2508.09167) again cites 1I's 'extreme geometry' among the accumulated anomalies justifying its Level 4 ranking.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Natural routes to oblate shapes exist: Seligman & Laughlin (ApJL 896, L8, 2020) showed sublimation mass wasting on an H2 iceberg monotonically raises the axis ratio, sculpting the body toward the observed 6:6:1 oblate form; Jackson & Desch's N2-ice fragment (JGR Planets 2021) likewise ablates to an oblate 45 x 44 x 7.5 m sliver — 'like a bar of soap', per the AGU release; and gravitational collapse of rotating pebble clouds can yield flattened planetesimals (Lorek & Johansen, arXiv:2401.05101 — a general formation result, not an Oumuamua study). By contrast, Zhang & Lin's tidal-fragmentation simulations (Nature Astronomy 2020) produce almost exclusively prolate, cigar-like fragments and argue the elongated interpretation of the lightcurve remains viable — so the mainstream view spans both shape families, with the pancake merely statistically preferred.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.03696","https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stz2380","https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/489/3/3003/5556541","https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07218"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-10","title":"Counter-explanation wars and Loeb's rebuttals (2019-2023)","date":"2023-03-22","observation":"Each natural model for the coma-less acceleration drew a published Loeb counter. Hydrogen iceberg (Seligman & Laughlin, ApJL 896 L8, 2020) vs Hoang & Loeb (ApJL 899 L23, 2020): H2-ice grains cannot form in dense GMCs (collisional heating sublimates mantles) and only bodies larger than ~5 km would survive the journey. Nitrogen fragment (Jackson & Desch / Desch & Jackson, JGR Planets 126, 2021) vs Siraj & Loeb (New Astronomy 92, 101730, 2022): the required exo-Pluto mass of heavy elements exceeds the total locked in all stars (95% confidence). Radiolytic H2-in-water-ice (Bergner & Seligman, Nature 615, 610, 2023-03-22) vs Hoang & Loeb (ApJL 951 L34, 2023): neglecting H2 evaporative cooling overestimated the surface temperature by a factor of ~9, cutting the thermal speed of outgassing H2 (and thus the thrust) by a factor of ~3 — too little to propel 'Oumuamua. A separate Nature Matters Arising by N. F. W. Ligterink ('Accelerating 'Oumuamua with H2 is challenging', Nature 623, E14, 2023-11-29) independently argued GCR radiolysis cannot make enough H2 in a water-ice body.","loeb_take":"Loeb's position: the succession of exotic, never-before-observed objects (hydrogen icebergs, nitrogen icebergs, 'dust bunnies', dark comets) invented to save a natural origin shows the anomaly is real, and each fails quantitatively — so an artificial thin object remains on the table. Bergner and Seligman published a formal Reply in Nature (623, E16, 2023) defending their model against Ligterink's Matters Arising (Hoang & Loeb's critique ran in ApJL, not Nature), and the mainstream treats the 2023 Nature paper as the leading closure of the case; Loeb explicitly does not.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"[paraphrase] Adding hypothetical objects we have never seen — hydrogen icebergs, nitrogen icebergs — to explain the first interstellar visitor is not simpler than considering an artificial origin.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, Medium essay ''Oumuamua Was Not a Hydrogen-Water Iceberg' (2023-03-23) and EarthSky interview 'Was 'Oumuamua a comet? Avi Loeb responds' (2023)","official_explanation":"The consensus anchor remains the ISSI 'Oumuamua team (Nature Astronomy 3, 594, 2019-07-01): every observed property is consistent with a purely natural planetesimal, and 'we find no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation'. Bergner & Seligman (2023) plus the emerging 'dark comet' population (Seligman et al. 2023-2024, small solar-system bodies with non-gravitational accelerations and no comae) are cited as showing 'Oumuamua-like behavior occurs naturally.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab963f","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/abab0c","https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2022NewA...9201730S/abstract","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-022-05687-w"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-11","title":"Retroactive Loeb Scale rank: Level 4","date":"2025-08-06","observation":"In 'The Loeb Scale: Astronomical Classification of Interstellar Objects' (Eldadi, Tenenbaum & Loeb, arXiv:2508.09167, submitted Aug 6, 2025; later published in the International Journal of Astrobiology as the 'Interstellar Object Significance Scale (IOSS)'), Loeb and coauthors defined a 0-10 anomaly scale extending the Torino Scale framework (levels 0-3 natural, 4-7 increasingly anomalous, 8-10 confirmed artificial) and retroactively scored the known interstellar objects: 1I/'Oumuamua = Level 4, 2I/Borisov = Level 0, 3I/ATLAS = Level 4 (at the time of writing). A follow-up, 'Quantitative Mapping of the Loeb Scale' (Trivedi & Loeb, arXiv:2509.06253, Sep 2025), formalized the scoring numerically, explicitly modeled on Sagan's quantification of the Kardashev scale.","loeb_take":"Level 4 marks 'Oumuamua as genuinely anomalous — multiple independent deviations from any known natural class (non-gravitational acceleration without a coma of gas or dust, extreme geometry, spectral properties unexplained by conventional models) — while Borisov's Level 0 shows the scale is not alien-hype: a normal comet scores zero. Loeb used the 1I-vs-2I contrast throughout 2025-2026 essays as the template for judging 3I/ATLAS, arguing anomaly-scoring should be systematic rather than dismissed case-by-case.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"[paraphrase] On the Loeb scale, 'Oumuamua ranks at Level 4 — the critical threshold, anomalous enough to warrant enhanced follow-up — while Borisov, a classical comet despite its interstellar origin, ranks Level 0.","quote_source":"Eldadi, Tenenbaum & Loeb, 'The Loeb Scale' (arXiv:2508.09167) and Avi Loeb Medium essay 'The Loeb Scale: Astronomical Classification of Interstellar Objects' (Aug 6, 2025)","official_explanation":"The scale is Loeb's framework, not community consensus — though it did pass peer review (published in the International Journal of Astrobiology). Critics note the ISSI 'Oumuamua team's 2019 Nature Astronomy paper ('The Natural History of 'Oumuamua') already found no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation, and that anomaly scales with author-controlled thresholds risk baking in the author's priors. No IAU or NASA body has adopted the scale; mainstream papers continue to classify 1I as a natural (if unusual) planetesimal and 3I/ATLAS as an active comet.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.09167","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-loeb-scale-astronomical-classification-of-interstellar-objects-62b909644351","https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06253","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/quantitative-mapping-of-the-loeb-scale-6b8b93554430"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-01","title":"Extreme carbon-monoxide enrichment — the most CO-rich comet measured inside 2.5 AU","date":"2020-04-20","observation":"Two independent teams reported on 2I/Borisov's coma in the weeks after its 2019-12-08 perihelion (q = 2.0065 AU, e = 3.3565). Bodewits et al. (HST/COS ultraviolet, four post-perihelion epochs from 2019-12-11 to 2020-01-13) derived Q(CO) = 6.4–10.7 × 10^26 molecules s^-1 (equivalent to 30–50 kg s^-1) and CO/H2O of at least 173% — 'more than three times higher than previously measured for any comet in the inner (<2.5 au) solar system,' where CO/H2O mostly ranges 0.2–23% with typical values near 4%. Simultaneous Swift/UVOT + HST/COS epochs (2019-12-19/22 and 2020-01-13) gave CO/H2O = 130–155% directly. Cordiner et al. (ALMA, 2019-12-15/16) measured Q(CO) = (4.4 ± 0.7) × 10^26 s^-1 and Q(HCN) = (7.0 ± 1.1) × 10^23 s^-1, giving CO/H2O ≈ 35–105%, HCN/H2O ≈ 0.06–0.16% (ordinary), and CO/HCN = 630 (+200/−340) — higher than any comet previously observed except the peculiar Oort Cloud comet C/2016 R2 (PanSTARRS), whose CO/HCN was 26,400 at rH = 2.8 AU. Excluding C/2016 R2, the next-highest CO/HCN was 238 ± 21 for C/2006 W3 (Christensen) at rH = 3.3 AU. The ALMA/NASA release put Borisov's CO abundance at 9–26× the solar-system comet average.","loeb_take":"Loeb has never treated Borisov's CO excess as a technosignature — it is chemistry, not engineering. In his retrospective framing Borisov is the one interstellar object that looked ordinary: a comet whose volatiles simply record a colder, more carbon-rich birth disk than the Sun's. In the 2025 Loeb Scale paper he co-authored (formally the Interstellar Object Significance Scale, IOSS; Eldadi, Tenenbaum & Loeb, arXiv:2508.09167, submitted 2025-08-06) Borisov is assigned Level 0 — fully consistent with natural processes — the lowest score of the three interstellar objects, against Level 4 for both 1I/'Oumuamua and 3I/ATLAS.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"Among the four interstellar objects, only Borisov appeared familiar. It was a comet, not much different from the analogous snowballs found in the Solar system.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'The Unfamiliar Nature of Interstellar Objects,' Medium, 2022-09-23 (verified verbatim)","official_explanation":"Both teams read the CO excess as a formation-environment signature, not an anomaly. Cordiner et al.: Borisov 'must have formed in a relatively CO-rich environment — probably beyond the CO ice-line in the very cold, outer regions of a distant protoplanetary accretion disk, as part of a population of small, icy bodies analogous to our Solar System's own proto-Kuiper Belt.' Bodewits et al. add that the volatile carbon-to-oxygen ratio is 'nearly six times higher than that of previously measured solar system comets,' and derive three constraints on the host system: it must be chemically distinct from ours, Borisov must have formed beyond a CO snowline (with CO ice condensing alongside H2O rather than trapped in clathrate), and the system must have undergone dynamical stripping of planetesimals from beyond that snowline — an M-dwarf host, whose CO snowline sits near 6.6 AU, fits well. Bodewits et al. call the result 'the first glimpse into the ice content and chemical composition of the protoplanetary disk of another star that is substantially different from our own.' Notably, the papers do NOT treat known CO-rich solar-system comets as equivalent cases: Bodewits et al. explicitly exclude C/2016 R2 (PanSTARRS) from the comparison because its 2.6 AU perihelion meant it 'never reached the inner solar system where water sublimation is fully effective,' no H2O was detected in its coma, and its dominant volatile is most likely CO2 rather than CO. CO-driven comets outside 2.5 AU do commonly show CO exceeding H2O, so a CO-rich nucleus is not unprecedented in kind — but Borisov's ratio inside 2.5 AU, where water sublimation is fully effective, is the record.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.08972","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-020-1095-2","https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.09586","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-020-1087-2"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-02","title":"Atomic nickel (and iron) vapor in a 180 K coma — and the solar-system comets that did the same thing","date":"2021-05-19","observation":"Guzik & Drahus (Nature 593, 375–378) detected gaseous atomic Ni I in Borisov's cold coma from VLT/X-shooter spectra taken 28, 30 and 31 January 2020 UT at a mean heliocentric distance of 2.322 AU (geocentric 2.064 AU) — equilibrium temperature ~180 K, far below the >700 K normally needed to vaporize metal-bearing dust. Production rate Q(Ni) = (0.9 ± 0.3) × 10^22 atoms s^-1, i.e. 0.002% of OH and 0.3% of CN, from a short-lived nickel-bearing parent with a 340 (+260/-200) s lifetime at 1 AU. Opitom et al. 2021 (A&A 650, L19; VLT/UVES, 12 epochs over ~21 h, 2019-11-15 to 2020-03-16, spanning 2.1 AU pre-perihelion to 2.6 AU post-perihelion) independently found Fe I and Ni I, measuring log(Ni I/Fe I) = 0.21 ± 0.18 for 2I against an average of -0.06 ± 0.31 for 17 solar-system comets — i.e. an ordinary ratio.","loeb_take":"Loeb explicitly uses Borisov as the metals baseline rather than an anomaly: he notes Borisov's Ni/Fe ratio matched ordinary comets, which is exactly why he treats 3I/ATLAS's early nickel-heavy, iron-poor plume as the outlier worth flagging. Under his own scale Borisov's metal chemistry contributes nothing to its Level 0 score.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"The interstellar comet 2I/Borisov showed nickel and iron with an abundance ratio that is similar to that observed in solar system comets.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'New Data on the Extreme Nickel Production of 3I/ATLAS,' Medium, 2025-10-01","official_explanation":"Published back-to-back in the same issue of Nature, Manfroid, Hutsemékers & Jehin (593, 372–374) showed the same phenomenon is ubiquitous at home: high-resolution UV-optical VLT/UVES spectra of about 20 solar-system comets collected since 2002 between 0.68 and 3.25 AU show up to about 40 Fe I and 25 Ni I emission lines, with Ni and Fe of the same order of magnitude (unlike the solar/meteoritic ratio, which runs ~10× more Fe). The paper weighs several release mechanisms — partial sublimation of Ni-rich sulfides such as pentlandite (needs >600 K), superheating of submicrometre fluffy aggregates, nanoparticle impact vapor (~1000 K) — and, on the strength of the Fe/Ni–carbon-oxide correlation, favors sublimation of low-temperature organometallic carriers, specifically the carbonyls Fe(CO)5 and Ni(CO)4, whose sublimation temperatures are only slightly above that of CO2 (later A&A work puts them near ~100 K, between CO2 and H2O); Ni(CO)4 sublimates ~10× faster than Fe(CO)5 near 300 K, which explains the cometary Ni overabundance. This pairing is the standard mainstream rebuttal invoked against the 3I/ATLAS 'nickel without iron' claim — and later VLT work (Hutsemékers et al. 2026, A&A 706, A43) showed 3I/ATLAS's log(Ni I/Fe I) fell from 1.27 ± 0.10 at 2.64 AU (the first epoch at which Fe I was detected at all; only lower limits, >0.99, exist at 3.78 and 3.14 AU) to ~0.3 near 1.9–2.0 AU, becoming indistinguishable from solar-system comets and from 2I/Borisov.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09305","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03485-4","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03435-0","https://www.eso.org/public/archives/releases/sciencepapers/eso2108/eso2108a.pdf"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-03","title":"March 2020 outburst and the transient 'double nucleus' — a textbook comet splitting event","date":"2020-03-30","observation":"Drahus et al. (ATel #13549, OGLE 1.3-m, Las Campanas) recorded two consecutive outbursts — ~0.3 mag between UT 2020 March 4.3 and 5.3 and ~0.4 mag between March 8.3 and 9.3, ~0.7 mag total in 5 days. Jewitt, Kim, Mutchler et al. (ApJL 896, L39), whose photometry independently shows a steady brightening from January 3 to February 24 indicating rising activity, tied the outburst to solids ejected with the comet outbound at ~2.8 AU: combined cross-section ~100 km^2 and ~2 × 10^7 kg in 0.1 mm grains — roughly 10^-4 of the nucleus mass for an assumed 500 m nucleus radius. HST then resolved a transient secondary on 2020-03-30 (cross-section ~0.6 km^2, dust mass ~10^5 kg). The secondary was absent in images on and before March 28 and gone again on and after April 3.","loeb_take":"Loeb has not treated the outburst or splitting as anomalous; both are standard comet behavior and are among the reasons he classes Borisov as a 'classical comet.' In his scale paper Borisov's coma onset and volatile-driven activity are cited as the evidence placing it in the green zone (Level 0), in direct contrast with 1I (no coma at all) and 3I (which he argues showed activity inconsistent with its spectrum).","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"In stark contrast, Borisov presented as a classical comet despite its interstellar origin","quote_source":"Eldadi, Tenenbaum & Loeb, 'The Loeb Scale: Astronomical Classification of Interstellar Objects', arXiv:2508.09167, 2025-08","official_explanation":"Jewitt et al. attribute the delayed appearance and rapid disappearance of the secondary to rotational bursting of one or more large (meter-sized) boulders under outgassing torques after ejection from the main nucleus — not a nucleus break-up. They characterize the events as 'minor events involving a negligible fraction of the total mass,' with the comet surviving the planetary region essentially intact. Outbursts and fragment shedding are common in solar-system comets.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.01242","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab99cb","https://www.astronomerstelegram.org/?read=13549","http://www2.ess.ucla.edu/~jewitt/papers/2020/JKM20.pdf"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-04","title":"'May be the first truly pristine comet ever observed' — anomalously high, spatially uniform polarization","date":"2021-03-30","observation":"Bagnulo et al. (Nature Communications 12, 1797) imaged Borisov in polarimetric mode with VLT/FORS2 from 2019-12-25 to 2020-03-20. Measured coma polarization Pr (R_SPECIAL filter) fell from 3.76 ± 0.08% at phase angle 28.48 deg (2019-12-25) through 1.19 ± 0.21% at 24.14 deg (2020-02-06) to -0.82 ± 0.13% at 18.06 deg (2020-03-20), with V- and I-filter values consistent (e.g. V: 3.35 ± 0.07% at 28.48 deg) — systematically higher than typical solar-system comets at matching phase angles. The only solar-system object with a comparable polarimetric curve is C/1995 O1 (Hale-Bopp), and unlike Hale-Bopp (whose polarimetric images showed jets and arcs), Borisov's coma is polarimetrically homogeneous.","loeb_take":"Loeb does not contest this result and has not framed Borisov's dust as anomalous in the technosignature sense. His consistent public position is that Borisov is the natural interstellar comet against which 1I and 3I should be judged — pristine but ordinary in kind. No Borisov-specific Loeb quote on the polarimetry exists; his general characterization is that Borisov 'appeared familiar', and his Loeb-scale paper (arXiv:2508.09167) rates Borisov Level 0 versus Level 4 for both 1I/'Oumuamua and 3I/ATLAS.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"Among the four interstellar objects, only Borisov appeared familiar.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'The Unfamiliar Nature of Interstellar Objects', Medium, Sep 2022 — https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-unfamiliar-nature-of-interstellar-objects-de8c855677e4","official_explanation":"ESO and the authors read the high, uniform polarization as evidence of an unprocessed, compositionally homogeneous dust population: Borisov apparently never passed close to any star before its 2019 solar passage, so its grains were never thermally or radiatively altered — making it more pristine than Hale-Bopp, which had made one prior solar passage (~2250 BCE). Co-author Alberto Cellino's polarization-plus-color analysis concluded the formation environment was not so different in composition from the early Solar System.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"polarization","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-22000-x","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8009915/","https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso2106/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.09167"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-05","title":"Compact sub-kilometre nucleus — small, ordinary, and tightly bracketed","date":"2019-12-11","observation":"Jewitt, Hui, Kim et al. (arXiv:1912.05422, published ApJL 888, L23) modelled HST high-resolution imaging and found the scattering is dominated by a coma of large particles (characteristic size ~0.1 mm) ejected anisotropically. Convolution modelling of the coma surface-brightness profile sets a robust upper limit of spherical-equivalent nucleus radius rn <= 0.5 km (geometric albedo 0.04 assumed); an independent constraint from the measured non-gravitational acceleration gives rn > 0.2 km (density 500 kg m^-3 assumed). So 0.2 km < rn <= 0.5 km, i.e. a few hundred metres.","loeb_take":"Loeb accepts the sub-kilometre nucleus at face value and uses it purely as a scale reference: he describes Borisov's nucleus as 'about a few hundred meters in radius,' and in his 3I/ATLAS essays states that 3I's nucleus is roughly a thousand times more massive than Borisov's and about a million times more massive than 1I/'Oumuamua. For Borisov the size is a datum, not an anomaly.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"about a few hundred meters in radius","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'The Unfamiliar Nature of Interstellar Objects,' Medium, 2022-09-23","official_explanation":"A few-hundred-metre icy nucleus with a large-grain, anisotropically ejected dust coma is entirely typical of active solar-system comets; the non-gravitational acceleration is fully accounted for by measured outgassing (unlike 1I/'Oumuamua, where acceleration occurred with no detected coma). Independent estimates (e.g. Hui et al. 2020; VISIR thermal work) converge on the same few-hundred-metre range.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.05422","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab621b","http://www2.ess.ucla.edu/~jewitt/papers/2020/JHK20.pdf","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-unfamiliar-nature-of-interstellar-objects-de8c855677e4"]},{"object":"fb","id":"F-01","title":"IM1 — the half-metre bolide that hit at 44.8 km/s","date":"2014-01-08","observation":"At 17:05:33 UTC, US Government sensors logged a fireball over the Bismarck Sea north of Manus Island, Papua New Guinea. CNEOS lists the row at 1.2°S 147.1°E, peak brightness at 18.7 km altitude, a pre-entry speed of 44.8 km/s and a total impact energy of 0.13 kt TNT — a body roughly half a metre across. As the catalog stood at the 2026-08-22 pull it was the fifth-fastest of the 360 entries that report a speed at all. Two things about that row matter more than the numbers themselves: CNEOS publishes no uncertainties on any field, and the sensors that produced it are classified, so the measurement cannot be independently audited. The published papers arguing this case quote a box centre of 1.3°S 147.6°E; the catalog now lists 1.2°S 147.1°E, having been revised in the interim.","loeb_take":"Siraj & Loeb reconstructed the velocity vector as hyperbolic — gravitationally unbound from the Sun at 99.999% confidence — with an asymptotic speed of 42.1 ± 5.5 km/s and a striking 58 ± 6 km/s relative to the Local Standard of Rest, which would place its origin outside the thin disk of ordinary nearby stars. In April 2022 the US Space Command chief scientist reviewed the classified data and signed a memo stating that the velocity estimate reported to NASA was sufficiently accurate to indicate an interstellar trajectory. Loeb adds two further arguments: IM1's inferred material strength ranks first out of 273 CNEOS fireballs, and it fragmented unusually low for a chondrite at that speed. In June 2023 his Galileo Project ran a magnetic-sled expedition over the CNEOS box and recovered hundreds of sub-millimetre spherules whose beryllium-lanthanum-uranium enrichment he reports as unlike any solar-system material.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"Its high LSR speed implies a possible origin from the deep interior of a planetary system or a star in the thick disk of the Milky Way galaxy.","quote_source":"WITH A. SIRAJ · ASTROPHYS. J. 939:53 (2022), ABSTRACT","official_explanation":"The critique attacks the one number everything rests on. Brown & Borovička (ApJ 953, 2023) compared USG fireball velocities against events with independent optical or infrasound data and found 10-15 km/s uncertainties at high speeds and radiant errors above ten degrees; their conclusion is that the simplest reading of IM1 is a substantially overestimated speed — and at a lower speed the orbit is bound and ordinary. That also dissolves the strength argument, because the ablation fits that make IM1 look ultra-strong only do so at the high assumed speed; the two 'anomalies' are one measurement, not two. Hajduková et al. (A&A 691, A8, 2024) went further: with no uncertainties published, nothing in the CNEOS data can confirm or reject an interstellar origin for any nominally hyperbolic entry. On the spherules, Gallardo (2023) matched the Be-La-U enrichment pattern to coal fly ash, a common shipping-lane contaminant, and Desch and colleagues argued a half-metre body arriving at 45 km/s would have almost entirely vaporised. In 2024 a team led by Benjamin Fernando reported that the seismic pulse used to pick the search site fitted a truck driving past the Manus Island seismometer, and placed the fireball more than 150 km from where the sled ran. Loeb answered that the expedition was guided by the 11-km CNEOS satellite box rather than the seismometer, and that Fernando's own infrasound ellipse (186 by 388 km) is large enough to contain it. The most sympathetic independent verdict is Socas-Navarro (2024), who redid the statistics and put the probability of an interstellar origin at 94.1% — significant, but short of the 3-sigma a discovery claim needs.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"speed-dist","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ac8eac","https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14267","https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17219","https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/"]},{"object":"fb","id":"F-02","title":"IM2 — the second candidate, and the problem of a single dataset","date":"2017-03-09","observation":"At 04:16:37 UTC a considerably larger bolide was logged over the North Atlantic well west of Portugal: 40.5°N 18.0°W, peak brightness at 23.0 km, 36.5 km/s, 1.1 kt of impact energy — roughly a metre across and about an order of magnitude more massive than IM1. Nothing was recovered, no independent optical or seismic detection of it has been published, and as with every CNEOS row the catalog reports no error bars.","loeb_take":"Siraj & Loeb identified this row as a second unbound candidate and argued the pair is the real result. Fitting a log-normal distribution to material strengths across the catalog, IM1 and IM2 rank first and third of 273 fireballs — 3.5-sigma and 2.6-sigma outliers, with a random-draw probability around one in ten thousand. Their reading is that meter-scale interstellar objects are systematically tougher than solar-system rocks, and that the implied abundance is embarrassingly large: for two such objects to be caught by Earth's atmosphere in a few years, roughly 40% of all refractory material would have to be locked up in meter-scale interstellar bodies, which they note is hard to square with ordinary planetary-system ejection.","claim_label":"LOEB ASSESSMENT","loeb_quote":"If IM2 is confirmed, this implies that interstellar meteors come from a population with material strength characteristically higher than meteors originating from within the solar system.","quote_source":"WITH A. SIRAJ · ASTROPHYS. J. LETT. 941 (2022), ABSTRACT","official_explanation":"The conditional in Loeb's own sentence is the whole objection: IM2 has never been confirmed by anything outside the catalog that produced it. The April 2022 US Space Command memo covered the 2014 event only. No expedition has looked for material, no independent detection has been published, and the velocity-uncertainty critique of Brown & Borovička applies with equal force — 36.5 km/s sits in exactly the regime where they measured 10-15 km/s errors, and shaving a few km/s makes the orbit bound. The strength ranking is derived from the same lightcurve and the same speed, so it is not independent evidence but a second reading of the first measurement. Hajduková et al. (2024) surveyed every nominally hyperbolic CNEOS entry and found none that the data can settle either way. The mainstream position is not that IM2 is impossible — it is that a single un-audited catalog, with the error bars withheld, cannot carry a claim this large on its own.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"speed-dist","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/aca8a0","https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14267","https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2024/11/aa49569-24/aa49569-24.html","https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/"]},{"object":"fb","id":"F-03","title":"Are fireballs increasing? Four instruments, and the one that sees daylight","date":"2026-08-15","observation":"Four datasets can speak to this, and no two of them measure the same thing. The American Meteor Society logs EYEWITNESS REPORTS — its unit is people, not photons, so it rises when cameras, phones and audiences do. NASA/JPL CNEOS logs US Government SENSOR DETECTIONS with an energy in kilotons, immune to how many people were watching, but with a hard floor: the smallest of its 1,069 rows is 0.048 kt and only 6 fall below 0.073 kt. The Global Meteor Network runs cameras that compute an ABSOLUTE MAGNITUDE for every meteor, with no human deciding whether to file anything — but it grew from 73 stations to 1,327 across this period, is blind in daylight, and is concentrated over Europe and North America. And NASA's Geostationary Lightning Mapper pipeline logs BOLIDE DETECTIONS from orbit — 11410 of them between 2017-07-23 and 2026-08-15 — assigning each an ordinal brightness. It was added to this case to answer the objection the other three cannot: staring down from geostationary orbit, IT SEES DAYLIGHT. 47.3% of its detections are daytime (5396 day against 5364 night), which is the half of the population a ground camera network never sees. Every one of the four has a bias that can manufacture a trend on its own. Only the statistics that normalise those biases away are worth comparing across years.","loeb_take":"The claim, made most carefully by the Angry Astronaut channel in August 2026, is that 2026 delivered a genuine spike in bright fireballs — and, crucially, that it is not merely an artifact of more people looking up. As put on 2026-08-23: in the first quarter alone, events drawing 50 or more reports more than doubled the recent average and events with 100-plus reports roughly doubled, so the larger, brighter events themselves increased. That is a real, checkable proposition, and it already anticipates the obvious objection rather than ignoring it. Checked against AMS itself, the figures are ACCURATE. Q1 2026 logged 25 events in the 51-99 report band against a 2021-2025 mean of 11.8, and 16 events above 100 reports against a mean of 8.8 — 41 high-report events where the recent average is 20.6. Even as a share of all events, which corrects for the dataset simply getting bigger, high-report events ran 1.715% of the total against a 1.067% norm: 1.61 times the usual proportion, the highest in the twenty years AMS publishes.","claim_label":"THE CLAIM AS MADE","loeb_quote":"The signal wasn't just more people looking up. The larger, brighter events themselves increased.","quote_source":"ANGRY ASTRONAUT · YOUTUBE 2026-08-23 · TRANSCRIBED FROM AUTO-CAPTIONS, NOT A PUBLISHED TEXT","official_explanation":"The AMS figures are right and still cannot carry the conclusion, because AMS counts reporters. A growing audience does two things at once: it surfaces more events AND it gives each event more reports, pushing events UPWARD through the bins. An event drawing 20 reports in 2022 draws 45 in 2026 and leaves the 11-25 band. So the flat middle bin is not evidence against the bias — it is the bias. The diagnostic is whether well-witnessed events outgrew the dataset as a whole, and they did not: in Q1 2026 all events ran 1.23 times the 2021-2025 mean while events with 11 or more reports ran 1.21 times it. Same population, shifted rightward. CNEOS, which cannot be moved by audience at all, shows nothing: 18 events from 1 January to 15 August against a 2016-2025 range of 18 to 31, with one event at or above 1 kilotonne. But CNEOS cannot settle it either, because essentially every event in this argument sits below its floor. The tiebreaker is the Global Meteor Network, which measures brightness directly. Normalised as bright meteors (absolute magnitude -4 or brighter) per detection — a ratio that holds however many cameras are running — Q1 2026 gives 0.286% against a 2021-2025 mean of 0.264%, a factor of 1.08 against year-to-year scatter running 0.131% to 0.440%. Across January to July, 0.275% against 0.256%. Flat, at both thresholds GMN records. The obvious confound is ruled out rather than assumed: had cameras grown more sensitive, fainter detections would swell the denominator and hide a real rise, but the median absolute magnitude holds between -0.09 and +0.05 from 2021 to 2026. THE DAYLIGHT OBJECTION HAS NOW BEEN ANSWERED BY A FOURTH INSTRUMENT, and it is the reason this case was rewritten. GMN sees nothing in daylight, and several of 2026's most prominent events were daytime — the Lake Erie bolide at 8:57 a.m. Eastern drew 222 reports across 16 states. NASA's GLM pipeline has no such blind spot: 47.3% of its 11410 detections are daytime. Over the same like-for-like window, 1 January to 15 August, its bright-category share runs 3.00% in 2026 against a 2021-2025 mean of 2.80% — a factor of 1.07, against year-to-year scatter of 2.03% to 4.38%. GMN's figure for its comparable window was a factor of 1.07. Two instruments built on different physics, one of them blind to half the sky's clock, agreeing to two decimal places is the strongest statement this register can make about the question. THE GLM CATALOG HAS ITS OWN TRAP AND IT WAS WALKED INTO DELIBERATELY: on 2025-03-06 its pipeline began auto-publishing detections that previously required human review, so 2025-26 contain a class of record earlier years lack. Filtering on the confidence field does not fix that — review lags, so it filters on how recent an event is. Running the statistic both ways does: restricted to human-published events only, the share is 3.10% against 2.88%, a factor of 1.08. The share survives the regime change; the bright COUNT does not, which is why the count is not quoted here. GLM also drifts in sensitivity the way GMN does not — its faint-category share climbs from 29.9% in 2019 to 71.4% in 2025 — so it corroborates GMN rather than standing on its own. Its coverage is the GOES field of view, the Americas and neighbouring oceans, not global. GMN's coverage is regional too, and its brightest bin is small and noisy. So this is not a refutation, and the central observation stands exactly as stated. What has moved is the burden: every measure that corrects for instrument growth is flat, and the cameras built to measure brightness did not record the change.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"glm-daylight","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://neo-bolide.ndc.nasa.gov/","https://fireball.amsmeteors.org/members/imo_fireball_stats/events_per_month_per_year","https://globalmeteornetwork.org/data/traj_summary_data/","https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/"]},{"object":"fb","id":"F-04","title":"The 2026 outbreak — the two events the sensors actually caught","date":"2026-08-14","observation":"Across the whole of 2025 and 2026, exactly FOUR CNEOS rows fall anywhere in the regions the 2026 fireball coverage discusses. Two of them are the marquee United States events, and both match the reported sighting to the minute. On 2026-03-17 at 12:56:42 UTC a 0.37 kt bolide was logged at 41.2 N 82.0 W — the northern Ohio shore of Lake Erie — at 45.0 km altitude and 14.9 km/s. Converted to local time that is 8:56:42 a.m. Eastern Daylight — 8:57 to the nearest minute, which is exactly the time reported for the daytime Lake Erie event that drew 222 reports across 16 states and Ontario. On 2026-08-14 at 07:48:36 UTC a 0.13 kt bolide was logged at 47.7 N 119.4 W in central Washington, 30.0 km altitude, 12.2 km/s — 12:48 a.m. Pacific Daylight, against the reported 12:48 a.m. for the event seen from northern Oregon into British Columbia with more than 250 reports. A CNEOS timestamp is published to the second and a fireball is witnessed at a specific minute, so agreement to a minute is close to a unique fingerprint. These are not date coincidences: CNEOS logs two or three events a month worldwide, and matching on date alone will happily pair a South Pacific row with a roof strike in Ohio.","loeb_take":"These events are presented as part of a wider 2026 pattern — an unusual density of bright, well-documented, often daytime bolides, several with sonic booms, some near volcanoes, and in the strongest form of the argument connected to material shed by 3I/ATLAS. The reading offered is not that every 2026 fireball is artificial: 'only a minority of the 2026 fireballs need be artificial. The majority, I'm sure, are ordinary meteors. The statistical excess of large events is what matters.' The Washington event is a fair example of why the pattern feels striking. NASA characterised it as roughly 16 inches across and about 170 pounds, travelling near 33,000 mph, and stated that it was NOT associated with the Perseids despite arriving two days after the shower's peak — a piece of asteroidal material on an unrelated trajectory, at a moment when the shower itself was producing no comparable daytime bolides.","claim_label":"THE CLAIM AS MADE","loeb_quote":"Only a minority of the 2026 fireballs need be artificial. The majority, I'm sure, are ordinary meteors. The statistical excess of large events is what matters.","quote_source":"ANGRY ASTRONAUT · YOUTUBE 2026-08-23 · TRANSCRIBED FROM AUTO-CAPTIONS, NOT A PUBLISHED TEXT","official_explanation":"Both events are ordinary in the catalog's own terms. At 0.37 kt and 0.13 kt they are unremarkable rows — the catalog's median is 0.200 kt — and both entry speeds, 14.9 and 12.2 km/s, sit at the SLOW end for a meteoroid, which is what asteroidal material on a bound solar-system orbit looks like. Anything arriving from interstellar space enters far faster: IM1 is listed at 44.8 km/s. Neither row carries the signature the interstellar reading requires. One number in circulation is worth untangling because the confusion recurs. The Washington event was described as breaking up with the energy of roughly 10 tons of TNT, while CNEOS lists 0.13 kt — 130 tons. These are different quantities: CNEOS publishes both a RADIATED energy, 3.8 x 10^10 J for this row, and a TOTAL IMPACT energy, and the radiated figure is only the fraction emitted as light. Quoting one against the other makes events look an order of magnitude apart when they are the same event. Checking the mass claim closes the loop: 7 tons at the listed 14.9 km/s yields about 0.19 kt of kinetic energy against 0.37 kt catalogued, which is the right order for a rough public figure. The deeper point is the count itself. Only four rows in two years fall in these regions — because nearly everything in this argument is below the CNEOS floor of roughly 0.05 kt. The sensors are not staying quiet about the 2026 fireballs; with a handful of exceptions they cannot see them at all. That is a limit of the instrument, not a verdict on the sky, and it is the reason this case can put two events on the map and no more.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"speed-dist","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/","https://ssd-api.jpl.nasa.gov/doc/fireball.html","https://fireball.amsmeteors.org/members/imo_view/browse_events","https://www.amsmeteors.org/fireballs/faqf/"]},{"object":"fb","id":"F-05","title":"Fireballs and volcanoes — testing the proximity claim","date":"2026-05-25","observation":"This is the one claim in the register that could be settled by arithmetic rather than argument, because both sides of it are already on file: 883 CNEOS events with coordinates, and 1,608 volcano positions published by NOAA's National Centers for Environmental Information. The test asks whether fireballs fall closer to volcanoes than chance allows, using a Monte Carlo with 1,000 trials per null model. Choosing the null is the whole test. Scattering random points over the globe would be wrong, because volcanoes sit on land and cluster in arcs, so any land or latitude bias in the detections would fake an association. Two nulls are used instead, both preserving the detections' own latitude distribution: a ROTATION null applies one random longitude offset to every event at once, keeping the event set's internal clustering perfectly intact while destroying only its alignment with volcano longitudes; a SCATTER null gives each event a fresh uniform longitude. The result is the same either way. Within 100 km of a volcano: 23 events observed against 32.8 expected by chance. Within 200 km: 78 against 82.3. Within 500 km: 260 against 250.5. Median distance to the nearest volcano: 838 km observed against 854 km by chance. Not one of those is significant, and the closest bin — the one the claim is actually about — sits BELOW chance.","loeb_take":"The claim is that bright fireballs keep coming down at volcanoes, too often to be coincidence, and that this is one of the strongest hints that some of them are directed rather than random. It has a long pedigree in UAP research — objects reported flying through eruption plumes, hovering over craters — and 2026 supplied vivid new examples. On 2026-05-25 a green fireball appeared to strike the flank of Mayon in the Philippines while cameras watched, with a bright object seeming to rise from the same spot afterwards; PHIVOLCS said a meteor burned up high in the atmosphere and the rising object was a satellite. Weeks later another meteor was filmed above an erupting volcano elsewhere in Asia, and lights were recorded near Popocatépetl in Mexico on 2026-07-15 and again on the 28th. As put on 2026-06-16: 'another meteor appears above an erupting volcano just a couple of weeks after the first encounter just like it, and also in Asia.'","claim_label":"THE CLAIM AS MADE","loeb_quote":"Another meteor appears above an erupting volcano just a couple of weeks after the first encounter just like it, and also in Asia.","quote_source":"ANGRY ASTRONAUT · YOUTUBE 2026-06-16 · TRANSCRIBED FROM AUTO-CAPTIONS, NOT A PUBLISHED TEXT","official_explanation":"The sensor record shows no association at any distance, and the reason the visual record does is stated in the coverage itself without being noticed: 'Five Vulks cameras are rolling.' PHIVOLCS — the Philippine Institute of Volcanology and Seismology — keeps cameras trained on Mayon's sky continuously, because that is what a volcano observatory is for. Active volcanoes are among the very few places on Earth with fixed cameras pointed upward twenty-four hours a day, recording, archived, and watched by people who publish what they catch. A fireball over farmland is seen by whoever happened to look up. A fireball over a monitored volcano is filmed in high definition from several angles and posted the same day. That difference alone is enough to manufacture the entire pattern, and it is exactly the kind of selection effect CNEOS does not have: its detections come from satellite sensors that do not care whether anyone is filming. So the catalog is the right instrument for this question, and it answers no. The clustering claim fails the same way — 'three in the same place' — with the median distance between an event and its nearest neighbour at 353 km against 354 km by chance, and 55 events having another within 100 km against 46.8 expected. THE LIMIT THAT CUTS THE OTHER WAY IS THE SAME ONE AS EVER, AND IT IS SERIOUS: neither the Mayon event nor the 2026-06-14 fireball has a CNEOS row at all, because both sit below the catalog's 0.048 kt floor. The nearest CNEOS row to Mayon in the entire record is 312 km away and from 2015. So this test says that the events big enough for satellites to see show no volcano preference; it cannot directly test the smaller events that the cameras actually caught. What it does establish is that the association does not exist in the one dataset immune to the camera effect — which is where a real physical association should have shown up first.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"volcano-dist","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://www.ngdc.noaa.gov/hazel/hazard-service/api/v1/volcanolocs","https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/","https://ssd-api.jpl.nasa.gov/doc/fireball.html","https://volcano.si.edu/"]},{"object":"fb","id":"F-06","title":"The 2013 precedent — a real change, and the wrong month to point at","date":"2013-11-11","observation":"The National UFO Reporting Center asks every witness to pick a SHAPE from a fixed list, and 'fireball' is one of the options. That makes the archive the one dataset in this register where the word means what a person decided it meant, rather than a kilotonne figure or a camera-measured magnitude — and it is exactly the field the 2013 claim is about, so it is the right file to open. nuforc.org returns 403 to a datacenter address, as several sources this project wants do; the route that works is the planetsig/ufo-reports mirror, which geocoded and time-normalised the archive in 2014 and kept the shape column. It holds 80,332 reports from 1906-11-11 to 2014-05-08, of which 78,400 carry a shape and 1,932 do not. TWO PROPERTIES OF IT GOVERN EVERYTHING BELOW. It is scrubbed: the mirror's own unscrubbed set is documented at 88,874 records and the file this project reads holds 80,332, rows with an unresolvable location or an unparseable duration having been dropped — so counts here run below NUFORC's own. And it stops in May 2014, which means it can test the 2013 half of a 2013-versus-2026 parallel and cannot test the 2026 half at all. It is also 81% United States: 65,114 of the 80,332 rows. Every share quoted here is over shape-bearing rows, and the unlabelled ones are counted apart rather than folded in.","loeb_take":"The claim is a historical rhyme rather than a measurement: that 2026 is not the first time a wave of fireball reports has swamped the usual statistics, and that the last time it happened was 2013 — the year of Chelyabinsk. As put on 2026-08-23, Cheryl Costa, writing the New York Skies column for the Syracuse New Times, was reviewing the NUFORC database in November 2013 and found a dramatic nationwide spike in orange and reddish fireball sightings in the first two weeks of the month: about 238 reports logged as of the 11th, roughly average in overall volume, but about 50 of them — near 20% — of the fireball variety, against a normal share near 7% that ranked fourth among roughly 30 categories. THE BASELINE FIGURES ARE ESSENTIALLY EXACT. Across the whole archive, fireball is 6,208 of 78,400 shape-bearing reports — 7.92% — and it ranks fourth of 29 shapes, behind light (21.1%), triangle (10.0%) and circle (9.7%). Against 'about 7%, fourth of roughly 30', that is as good as a remembered statistic gets. The November elevation is real too. Through the 11th the archive holds 262 shape-bearing reports with 37 fireballs, 14.1%; restricted to the United States, which is what 'nationwide' means here, 226 reports and 33 fireballs, 14.6%. The volume figure lands almost on the nose — 227 US rows against 'about 238'. So the shape of the claim survives contact with the data: an ordinary month by volume, running roughly twice the long-run fireball share.","claim_label":"THE CLAIM AS MADE","loeb_quote":"Roughly 50 of them, about 20%, were of that fireball variety. Normally these ranked fourth among roughly 30 categories and only make up about 7% of sightings.","quote_source":"ANGRY ASTRONAUT · YOUTUBE 2026-08-23, RELAYING CHERYL COSTA · TRANSCRIBED FROM AUTO-CAPTIONS, NOT A PUBLISHED TEXT","official_explanation":"What the month is measured AGAINST is where it comes apart, and the answer is more interesting than a correction. The 7.92% figure is the average of the whole archive, and the archive is more than a century long. The fireball share was not 7.92% in 2013 and had not been for years. Annually: 5.28% in 2003, 4.85% in 2004, 5.62% in 2008, 5.86% in 2009 — then 8.56%, 10.72%, 14.54%, 13.97% through 2010, 2011, 2012 and 2013. The share roughly TRIPLED across four years and then stayed there. Pool the twelve months immediately before the one in question, November 2012 through October 2013, and the fireball share is 14.33%. November 2013 came in at 14.37% for the full month and 14.1% through the 11th. It was not a spike against the baseline that was current; it was that baseline, to two decimal places. Ranked against every complete month from January 2000 to April 2014 — 172 of them, none missing — it comes 14th, and the record holder is July 2012 at 22.26% (195 of 876), comfortably clear of it. FOUR THINGS CUT THE OTHER WAY AND BELONG HERE. First and largest: the tripling is real, unexplained, and precisely the kind of change the claim is reaching for — the column found the right dataset and pointed at the wrong month, three years late. NUFORC cannot say whether that rise was the sky, the arrival of a camera in every pocket, or the word 'fireball' spreading through popular usage, and this project cannot say either. Second, the shape field is a self-assigned label; 'fireball' here is what a witness called it, not a measurement, and it is not the same quantity AMS or CNEOS report under that name. Third, the archive is a scrubbed mirror of a database that is itself edited after the fact, and Costa was reading it live in November 2013 — her snapshot is not reproducible, and the gap between her roughly 50 fireballs and the mirror's 33 US ones is the size of that difference. Fourth, and it constrains the whole comparison: the mirror ends 2014-05-08, so nothing in 2026 can be checked against it. What the archive does show, twice, is that the label carries real signal. Fireball share is seasonal exactly where it should be — 10.94% in July and 10.11% in December against 6.03% in April, which is the Perseids and the Geminids appearing in a database of public shape guesses. And it shows what the archive cannot see: on 2013-02-15, the day Chelyabinsk exploded over Russia, NUFORC logged 11 reports, 2 of them fireball, and February 2013 finished at 8.08% — among the quietest months of its year. The largest airburst since Tunguska is essentially absent from it, because it happened where the reporters are not. That is the honest summary of this file: the 2013 precedent is real as a change in the record and ordinary as a month, and this dataset can neither confirm nor deny the 2026 half of the parallel it was offered for.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"nuforc-share","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://github.com/planetsig/ufo-reports","https://nuforc.org/","https://fireball.amsmeteors.org/members/imo_fireball_stats/events_per_month_per_year","https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/"]},{"object":"fb","id":"F-07","title":"Fireballs and nuclear sites — the oldest positional claim, and the wrong instrument for it","date":"1948-12-05","observation":"In early December 1948 aircrews over New Mexico began reporting brilliant green objects that flew too slowly and too flatly to be meteors. The Air Force put Dr Lincoln LaPaz, the University of New Mexico meteoriticist who had built the country's first serious fireball-recovery programme, onto the problem. His conclusion unsettled everyone: the green fireballs were not ordinary meteors, and they appeared preferentially near the installations that mattered most — Los Alamos, Sandia, Kirtland. Project Sign and then Project Grudge investigated. The public verdict settled on unusual natural phenomena; LaPaz never accepted it. THIS FILE TESTS WHAT CAN BE TESTED AND SAYS PLAINLY WHAT CANNOT. The console ships 883 CNEOS events with coordinates, and the World Resources Institute publishes positions for 195 nuclear power reactors across 31 countries. That supports the same Monte Carlo that answered the volcano claim in F-05. It does not support the claim LaPaz actually made, for two reasons that belong here rather than in a footnote. The CNEOS record begins on 1988-04-15, forty years after his fireballs, so none of his events are in it. And these are POWER REACTORS: the weapons complex he was talking about is a few dozen sites with no openly licensed positional dataset, far too few for a Monte Carlo to say anything about.","loeb_take":"The nuclear connection is the most persistent positional claim in the whole subject, and it is not one man's. LaPaz's green fireballs are the opening chapter; the body of it is testimony gathered over decades from people who worked at the sites — the March 1967 Malmstrom Air Force Base episode in which a flight of Minuteman missiles is reported to have gone off alert while an object hovered above, the December 1980 Rendlesham Forest sightings beside the Bentwaters weapons store, and the interviews Robert Hastings assembled from former missile officers. The shape of the claim is consistent across all of it: whatever these objects are, they take an interest in fissile material and in the machinery built around it, and they have done so since the first bombs were assembled. Stated at that level the claim is not vague and it is not trivial — it predicts a real spatial preference, and it has a large and specific evidentiary base behind it in the form of first-hand accounts from people with clearances and careers to lose.","claim_label":"THE CLAIM AS MADE","loeb_quote":"He also noted that the green fireballs seemed to appear preferentially near sensitive military and scientific installations.","quote_source":"ANGRY ASTRONAUT · YOUTUBE · TRANSCRIBED FROM AUTO-CAPTIONS, NOT A PUBLISHED TEXT","official_explanation":"START WITH WHAT THIS TEST CANNOT DO, because it is most of the answer. Every event in the CNEOS catalog is an atmospheric airburst — a rock arriving at tens of kilometres per second and releasing energy in kilotonnes. The nuclear-connection testimony describes something else entirely: structured objects, often hovering, observed from the ground by guards and officers at close range. Those are not the same phenomenon, and a null result in a catalog of bolides cannot refute a body of eyewitness accounts about craft over silos. It is the wrong instrument, and the honest thing is to say so before running anything. THE SIZE FLOOR MAKES THAT WORSE, and it is the limit that matters most. The smallest row in the catalog is 0.048 kt and only 195 of 1069 fall below 0.1 kt — energies that take a metre-scale rock arriving at tens of kilometres per second. The objects in this testimony are routinely described as far smaller than that: beach-ball scale, motorcycle scale, something a guard could see the shape of. Nothing that size deposits enough energy to register here at any distance from anything, so most of the phenomenon the claim describes is excluded from the dataset before the geometry is even considered. Nor is there a better public dataset to switch to. Camera networks like the Global Meteor Network solve for meteor trajectories and discard anything that does not fit that model; satellite imagery is far too coarse; infrasound arrays have the same energy floor. The only instruments that record small, slow objects at all are human ones — sighting databases such as NUFORC, whose limits are laid out in F-06, and the official US UAP reporting, which publishes narrative case summaries rather than coordinates and so cannot feed a test of this kind either. What the catalog CAN answer is narrower and still worth having: does the population of objects large enough for satellites to detect show any positional preference for nuclear sites? Run against 195 reactors with the same two nulls used in F-05 — both preserving each event's own latitude, one rotating the whole set by a single random longitude offset and one redrawing every longitude independently, 600 trials each — the answer is mostly no, with one honest exception that is reported here rather than buried. Within 100 km: 11 events observed against 8.8 expected, p=0.290 and 0.248. Within 500 km: 68 against 71.5, p=0.702 and 0.692 — below chance. Median distance to the nearest reactor: 2682 km observed against 2687 and 2690 by chance, p=0.592 and 0.448. THE EXCEPTION IS THE MIDDLE BIN. Within 200 km there are 34 events against 24.8 expected — a 37% excess, and it reaches p=0.025 under the scatter null, though only p=0.070 under the rotation null, which is the conservative one because it leaves the events' own clustering intact. Two things keep that from being a result. Three radii were tested, and one marginal value among three correlated tests is close to what chance produces. More telling is the SHAPE: a real proximity effect is strongest where the proximity is tightest, and this one is absent at 100 km, present at 200 km, and gone again at 500 km. A bump in the middle with nothing at the centre is not what an attraction looks like. Restricting to the 85 reactors already commissioned by 1990 — the ones that existed for most of the catalog's span — leaves 22 events within 200 km and a median of 3266 km, no better. ONE MORE THING THE TARGET LIST GETS WRONG. The association as it is usually stated is with radiation and fissile material in general, not with weapons specifically — which would take in research reactors, enrichment and reprocessing plants, spent-fuel and waste storage, and industrial and medical sources. What is tested here is 195 civil POWER reactors, because that is the only category with an openly licensed global position list. A wider target set would be a better test of the claim as people actually make it, would roughly double the number of sites, and would move the null; building it is a real piece of outstanding work rather than a quibble. AND THE SELECTION EFFECT HERE IS THE SAME ONE AS THE VOLCANOES, arrived at independently. LaPaz was employed by the New Mexico installations and based among them; the observer network reporting to him was their own staff, under standing orders to watch the sky. Sightings concentrate where trained, instructed, cleared observers are already looking up — which is exactly what a nuclear weapons complex in 1949 was. That does not make the reports false. It means the geography of the reports is at least partly the geography of the watchers, and the one dataset immune to that effect does not reproduce the pattern.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"nuclear-dist","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/","https://datasets.wri.org/dataset/globalpowerplantdatabase","https://www.archives.gov/research/military/air-force/ufos","https://www.ngdc.noaa.gov/hazel/hazard-service/api/v1/volcanolocs"]}],"timeline":[{"date":"2025-05-07","title":"TESS pre-discovery detections begin","kind":"observation","description":"NASA's TESS spacecraft serendipitously imaged 3I/ATLAS from 2025-05-07 to 2025-06-02, 55 days before discovery, at roughly 6.4 AU from the Sun. Precovery photometry suggests the object was already active at ~6 AU, implying sublimation of ices more volatile than water.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02499","https://arxiv.org/html/2507.21967v2","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ae1f91"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20250507"},{"date":"2025-05-22","title":"Earliest ZTF precovery images","kind":"observation","description":"Zwicky Transient Facility precovery detections date back to 2025-05-22 (identified on individual unstacked images by Seligman et al.), with additional detection runs 2025-06-14 to 2025-06-21 and 2025-06-22 to 2025-06-29 identified within hours of the discovery report. ATLAS's own archives held precoveries from 2025-06-05 to 2025-06-29.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.02757","https://arxiv.org/html/2509.08792v1"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20250522"},{"date":"2025-06-21","title":"Vera C. Rubin Observatory serendipitous imaging","kind":"observation","description":"The Rubin Observatory captured 3I/ATLAS by chance during science-validation imaging starting 2025-06-21, ten days before discovery — among the highest-quality early data on the object.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://arxiv.org/html/2507.13409"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20250621"},{"date":"2025-07-01","title":"Discovery by ATLAS, Rio Hurtado, Chile","kind":"discovery","description":"The ATLAS survey telescope at Rio Hurtado, Chile (station W68) discovered the object at apparent magnitude ~18, 4.51 AU from the Sun and 3.50 AU from Earth, inbound at ~61 km/s relative to the Sun.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/3i-atlas/"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20250701"},{"date":"2025-07-02","title":"Confirmed interstellar — designated 3I/ATLAS (C/2025 N1)","kind":"discovery","description":"Follow-up astrometry confirmed a strongly hyperbolic orbit (eccentricity 6.141, hyperbolic excess velocity ~58 km/s — faster than 1I/'Oumuamua at 26 km/s and 2I/Borisov at 32 km/s). The Minor Planet Center announced it as the third confirmed interstellar object; Nordic Optical Telescope and Teide Observatory confirmed cometary activity the same day.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/3i-atlas/"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20250702"},{"date":"2025-07-16","title":"Loeb raises 'alien technology' hypothesis; anomaly list begins","kind":"statement","description":"Avi Loeb (with Adam Hibberd and Adam Crowl) posted the arXiv paper 'Is the Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS Alien Technology?' (arXiv:2507.12213, v1 2025-07-16), starting a running numbered list of anomalies (retrograde ecliptic-aligned trajectory ~0.2% probability, fine-tuned Mars/Jupiter arrival geometry, etc.) that grew to 22 items by March 2026. NASA/ESA and most astronomers maintained it is a natural comet throughout.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12213","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20250716"},{"date":"2025-07-21","title":"Hubble images constrain nucleus size","kind":"observation","description":"Hubble Space Telescope imaging constrained the nucleus diameter to 0.32-5.6 km and revealed a sunward dust fan / anti-solar tail. Later non-gravitational-acceleration analyses pointed to a sub-kilometer nucleus — roughly 0.8-1.1 km diameter and mass ~1.6e11 kg if the acceleration is driven by CO2 outgassing (Thoss, Loeb & Burkert).","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/3i-atlas/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.18341","https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.15735"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20250721"},{"date":"2025-08-06","title":"JWST: unusually CO2-rich composition","kind":"observation","description":"JWST NIRSpec observations (results announced 2025-08-25) found a coma unusually rich in carbon dioxide — CO2/H2O mixing ratio 8.0±1.0, among the highest ever measured — with CO2 at 129±1 kg/s, water 6.6±0.2 kg/s, CO 14.0±0.9 kg/s, plus water ice and carbonyl sulfide.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20250806"},{"date":"2025-08-21","title":"SPHEREx maps giant CO2 coma; VLT confirms cyanide and nickel","kind":"observation","description":"NASA's SPHEREx (observing 2025-08-08 to 08-12) reported a CO2 gas coma spanning at least 348,000 km in radius (~70 kg/s CO2) plus water ice. Concurrent VLT spectroscopy confirmed cyanide (17.6±2.0 g/s) alongside atomic nickel (4.6±0.7 g/s, first detected 2025-07-20) with no accompanying iron vapor — the nickel-without-iron signature became Loeb anomaly material, though nickel-carbonyl chemistry in normal comets can explain it.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15469"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20250821"},{"date":"2025-10-03","title":"Mars close approach — 0.194 AU; imaged by Mars orbiters","kind":"close_approach","description":"3I/ATLAS passed 0.194 AU (29.0 million km) from Mars at 04:38 UT. NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter HiRISE imaged it at ~30 km/pixel (a diffuse, comet-consistent smear), ESA's ExoMars TGO CaSSIS captured the coma in 5-second exposures, and MAVEN detected hydrogen in the UV. HiRISE image release was delayed ~6 weeks by the US government shutdown.","sources":["https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/mars/nasas-mars-spacecraft-capture-images-of-comet-3i-atlas/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20251003"},{"date":"2025-10-21","title":"Solar conjunction — unobservable from Earth","kind":"status","description":"The comet passed behind the Sun as seen from Earth around 2025-10-21, making its perihelion unobservable from the ground; Earth-based observers lost it for roughly late October through early November 2025 (solar elongation under ~25 deg from 2025-10-04 to 2025-11-06). Spacecraft (including GOES-19 and Mars/solar assets) provided partial coverage.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20251021"},{"date":"2025-10-29","title":"Perihelion — 1.36 AU from the Sun","kind":"close_approach","description":"Perihelion at 11:45 UT on 2025-10-29 at 1.356 AU (203 million km), between the orbits of Earth and Mars, moving at its maximum speed of ~68 km/s. It brightened faster near perihelion than typical comets — item 22 on Loeb's anomaly list, attributed by most astronomers to hypervolatile-driven outgassing.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/3i-atlas/"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20251029"},{"date":"2025-11-08","title":"Post-conjunction reemergence in the morning sky","kind":"observation","description":"Ground-based observers recovered the comet after solar conjunction; astrophotographer Michael Jaeger imaged the revived dust tail on 2025-11-08. Post-perihelion astrometry soon showed non-gravitational acceleration peaking at (89.3±4.6)e-9 AU/day^2 about a month before perihelion — normal for a small actively-outgassing nucleus, but logged by Loeb as an anomaly for its sideways component.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://skyandtelescope.org/astronomy-news/updates-on-interstellar-comet-3i-atlas-new-images-many-tails-and-non-gravitational-forces/"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20251108"},{"date":"2025-11-19","title":"NASA press event: 'it's a comet' — HiRISE, MAVEN, Hubble, JWST data released","kind":"statement","description":"After the government shutdown ended, NASA held a live press event releasing spacecraft imagery (HiRISE at 30 km/pixel, MAVEN UV hydrogen detection, Hubble, JWST, SPHEREx) and stating 3I/ATLAS 'exhibits behaviors typical of comets' — gas and dust shedding, gravitational response — i.e., a natural comet. Loeb publicly criticized the conference for dismissing his anomalies. ESA's JUICE spacecraft also observed the comet during November 2025 en route to Jupiter.","sources":["https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-to-share-comet-3i-atlas-images-from-spacecraft-telescopes/","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/there-is-nothing-more-deceptive-than-an-obvious-fact-nasas-press-conference-on-3i-atlas-9b44e632771f","https://www.primetimer.com/features/nasa-press-conference-shares-new-data-on-interstellar-comet-3i-atlas"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20251119"},{"date":"2025-12-19","title":"Earth closest approach — 1.80 AU","kind":"close_approach","description":"Closest approach to Earth at 06:01 UT on 2025-12-19 at 1.798 AU (269 million km), out beyond the 4:1 Kirkwood gap of the asteroid belt, at apparent magnitude ~19.9 — never any threat to Earth. Around the same period a second JWST epoch detected methane that had been absent pre-perihelion.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://www.livescience.com/space/comets/interstellar-comet-3i-atlas-makes-closest-pass-of-earth-wheres-it-heading-next"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20251219"},{"date":"2025-12-19","title":"Radio technosignature searches come up empty (ATA + MeerKAT)","kind":"observation","description":"A SETI Institute / Allen Telescope Array search (Sheikh et al., arXiv:2512.18142, submitted 2025-12-19) scanned 1-9 GHz for 7.25 hours around the Earth close approach: ~74 million narrowband hits reduced to 211 candidates by RFI filtering, all rejected — no technosignatures, with EIRP upper limits of ~10-110 W across the band. An earlier MeerKAT search (900-1670 MHz) had likewise found nothing, with a minimum EIRP limit of ~0.17 W (below cellphone-handset power).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.18142","https://www.seti.org/news/seti-institute-looks-for-signs-of-technology-in-interstellar-visitor-3iatlas/","https://phys.org/news/2026-06-interstellar-3iatlas-reveals-technosignatures-hour.html"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20251219-2"},{"date":"2026-01-22","title":"Opposition — best final viewing geometry","kind":"observation","description":"3I/ATLAS reached opposition at 177.6 deg solar elongation, visible all night but fading; Loeb separately flagged the 2026-01-22 alignment with the Sun-Earth axis to within 0.69 deg (phase angle under 2 deg for about a week) as a rare chance to measure the opposition surge of its dust. Large telescopes continued photometric monitoring of the outbound comet.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08624","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-rare-alignment-of-3i-atlas-with-the-sun-earth-axis-on-22-january-2026-b1bea4a553f7"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260122"},{"date":"2026-03-12","title":"Loeb publishes final tally of 22 anomalies","kind":"statement","description":"As the comet faded, Loeb's Medium essay enumerated 22 anomalies across mass/geometry, jet behavior, composition (nickel>iron, unusual isotopes), and trajectory coincidences (including a claimed 0.6%-probability alignment within 9 deg of the 1977 'Wow! Signal' arrival direction), ranking it Level 4 on his own 'Loeb Scale.' Mainstream rebuttals (e.g., Penn State's AstroWright) argued each item is explicable for a natural comet.","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260312"},{"date":"2026-03-16","title":"Jupiter close approach — 0.358 AU; Juno stays home","kind":"close_approach","description":"Closest approach to Jupiter at 12:22 UT at 0.358 AU (53.6 million km); it also passed 0.204 AU from the small Jovian moon Eupheme on 2026-03-17. A proposed Juno intercept (Loeb/Hibberd, arXiv:2507.21402 — requiring a ~2.7 km/s burn in September 2025) was never executed: Juno was low on fuel with engine problems and was not redirected; no Juno flyby observations resulted.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://arxiv.org/html/2507.21402v2","https://www.universetoday.com/articles/nasas-juno-spacecraft-could-intercept-3iatlas-as-it-approaches-jupiter"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260316"},{"date":"2026-04-02","title":"ESA JUICE results: an ordinary comet seen from close range","kind":"observation","description":"ESA released JUICE observations taken in November 2025, days after perihelion, from ~60 million km (JANUS camera and NavCam plus the MAJIS, SWI, and UVS spectrometers): a distinct coma with gas and dust extending over 5 million km, separate dust and anti-solar tails, and water output of ~2,000 kg/s on 2025-11-02 — equivalent to ~70 Olympic swimming pools per day, mostly from the sunward side — all consistent with a normal, if volatile-rich, comet.","sources":["https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Juice/Five_things_Juice_has_revealed_about_Comet_3I_ATLAS","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260402"},{"date":"2026-04-23","title":"Loeb status essay: a fading dot at 6.7 AU","kind":"statement","description":"Loeb's 'Where is 3I/ATLAS Now?' put the comet at ~1 billion km (6.7 AU) from the Sun, well beyond Jupiter, 'heading away from us back to interstellar space' and nearing the detectability horizon; he reorganized his claims into 12 anomaly categories at 'Loeb Scale' Level 4, while conceding the object is leaving without any close inspection.","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/where-is-3i-atlas-now-58f98865a20a"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260423"},{"date":"2026-05-15","title":"Second solar conjunction — ground observability ends","kind":"status","description":"Around May 2026 (date approximate) 3I/ATLAS entered its second solar conjunction, ending routine ground-based observation. When it reemerges around September 2026 it will be beyond Saturn's orbit and fainter than magnitude 20 — effectively the end of its observable apparition for all but the largest telescopes.","sources":["https://theskylive.com/articles/2025/07/3i-atlas-a-month-by-month-observing-guide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260515"},{"date":"2026-06-15","title":"Published ATA study reaffirms: no signals","kind":"statement","description":"The peer-reviewed Allen Telescope Array result (published in The Astronomical Journal, revised May 2026) circulated widely in June 2026 (date approximate): a seven-hour, 1-9 GHz scan found no technosignatures from 3I/ATLAS, closing out the SETI angle as the object receded. Loeb continued essays into June 2026 (e.g., on dust 'windshield' effects) but no new observational anomalies emerged.","sources":["https://phys.org/news/2026-06-interstellar-3iatlas-reveals-technosignatures-hour.html","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/ae6651","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/dusting-off-3i-atlas-interstellar-travelers-must-clean-their-windshields-132dcd338bda"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260615"},{"date":"2026-06-30","title":"Loeb co-authors a panspermia study — and rules out the directed version","kind":"statement","description":"Kakharov & Loeb ask whether an interstellar object like 3I/ATLAS could carry life between stars, either naturally or on purpose. They find natural panspermia plausible: microbes can survive at very low metabolic rates in ice films and veins, though matching the JWST methane production by frozen survival metabolism alone would need something like 10^14-10^15 kg of biomass. Directed panspermia they rule out on energy grounds — a direct 60 km/s impact releases 1.8x10^9 J/kg, hundreds of times the specific energy of TNT, which would destroy any biological capsule. Notable for the register because it is Loeb publishing a negative result about a deliberate-technology scenario.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.00202"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260630"},{"date":"2026-07-02","title":"FAST radio search: no periodic technosignature either","kind":"observation","description":"A team using China's Five-hundred-metre Aperture Spherical Telescope ran an L-band multibeam search aimed at 3I/ATLAS, specifically for periodically modulated signals — a signal type earlier searches had not covered — using canonical polyadic decomposition to separate a genuine centre-beam source from interference. No credible artificial periodic radio technosignature was detected above 0.146 W. This is the second independent radio null result on the object after the Allen Telescope Array study, and it closes a different part of the search space rather than repeating the first.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.01666"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260702"},{"date":"2026-07-09","title":"JUICE/MAJIS peer-reviewed: CO2 drove the activity, plus tentative organics","kind":"observation","description":"The ESA JUICE results announced by press release in April arrive as a paper. MAJIS observed 3I/ATLAS from 0.5 to 5.56 microns between 2 and 25 November 2025, just after perihelion, detecting water fluorescence at 2.7 microns and CO2 at 4.3 microns. Water production fell from 8x10^28 to 4x10^28 molecules per second across the period while the CO2/H2O ratio held near 10%, and the heliocentric trend indicates activity controlled by solar heating with CO2 playing the major role. Broad emission at 3.2-3.6 microns cannot be explained by common cometary CH-bearing volatiles; the authors read it as aliphatic C-H groups and tentative evidence for complex organic material released from dust grains.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08603"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260709"},{"date":"2026-07-17","title":"Crossing Saturn's orbital distance, hidden in conjunction","kind":"status","description":"In mid-July 2026 3I/ATLAS was about 9.25 AU from the Sun (~1.38 billion km; 10.25 AU from Earth per JPL Horizons), reaching Saturn's ~9.5 AU orbital distance in late July while hidden in solar conjunction (solar elongation ~9 deg) — unobservable and fainter than magnitude 20. It is escaping on its hyperbolic trajectory at ~58 km/s excess velocity, nearly symmetric to its inbound path; it will pass the inner Oort cloud region (~2,000 AU) around the year 2189 and leave the Sun's influence entirely in a few thousand years. Consensus verdict: the most-studied interstellar object ever, and a natural — though unusually CO2-rich, nickel-emitting, and ancient (possibly 7-14 billion years old) — comet.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/3i-atlas/","https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260717"},{"date":"2026-08-18","title":"Inbound campaign published: carbon-chain poor, and a persistent sunward jet","kind":"observation","description":"A multi-band monitoring campaign covering July to September 2025 — Faulkes and Maidanak imaging with SALT and Nordic Optical Telescope spectroscopy — reports a stable reddish colour with no secular variation on the way in, carbon-chain depletion with CN the only prominent molecular emission, and a persistent sunward jet at a position angle of about 280 degrees, read as sustained localized outgassing from a high-latitude active region. That last point bears directly on case A-05: an independent campaign confirms the sunward feature is real and long-lived, and attributes it to a fixed active area rather than anything exotic. Light-scattering modelling matches dense aggregates of organic matter and silicates, with water-ice sublimation too weak to change the optical properties.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.18371"],"object":"3i","id":"E-20260818"},{"date":"2017-09-09","title":"Perihelion at 0.256 AU (pre-discovery)","kind":"close_approach","description":"'Oumuamua made its closest approach to the Sun at 0.256 AU (inside Mercury's orbit), moving at ~87.7 km/s at perihelion — far too fast for the Sun to retain it. Nothing on Earth saw it: perihelion occurred ~40 days before discovery, so the entire inbound leg and the solar flyby were unobserved. This gap later mattered enormously — every acceleration measurement and every thermal model had to extrapolate back through a perihelion no one witnessed.","sources":["https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/oumuamua/","https://sci.esa.int/web/solar-system/-/60465-oumuamua-journey-through-our-solar-system"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20170909"},{"date":"2017-10-14","title":"Closest approach to Earth at ~0.162 AU (still undetected)","kind":"close_approach","description":"The object passed ~0.16175 AU (~24.2 million km, about 63 lunar distances) from Earth — its minimum Earth distance — five days before anyone found it. It was detected only on the outbound leg, already receding, which compressed the entire global observing campaign into a few frantic weeks before it faded beyond reach of ground-based telescopes.","sources":["https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/oumuamua/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1I/%CA%BBOumuamua"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20171014"},{"date":"2017-10-19","title":"Discovery by Robert Weryk / Pan-STARRS1","kind":"discovery","description":"Robert Weryk found the fast-moving ~20th-magnitude object (it had peaked at magnitude 19.7 the night before) in Pan-STARRS1 survey images from Haleakala, Hawaii, while searching for near-Earth asteroids. Follow-up astrometry (including pre-discovery images from Oct 18) quickly showed an eccentricity of ~1.2 — the first solidly hyperbolic small body ever recorded, meaning it came from outside the Solar System. Initially designated comet C/2017 U1 (PANSTARRS).","sources":["https://www.minorplanetcenter.net/mpec/K17/K17V17.html","https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/oumuamua/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1I/%CA%BBOumuamua"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20171019"},{"date":"2017-11-06","title":"Designation saga ends: first-ever 'I' class — 1I/'Oumuamua","kind":"status","description":"A two-week identity crisis: designated comet C/2017 U1 at discovery, reclassified asteroid A/2017 U1 on Oct 25 when deep imaging showed no coma or tail, then on Nov 6 the Minor Planet Center (MPEC 2017-V17) created an entirely new designation class — 'I' for interstellar — making it 1I/'Oumuamua, with the IAU-approved Hawaiian name meaning roughly 'a messenger from afar arriving first' (suggested by the Pan-STARRS team). No object had ever forced the IAU to invent a new category before.","sources":["https://www.minorplanetcenter.net/mpec/K17/K17V17.html","https://www.space.com/38798-first-interstellar-object-name-oumuamua.html"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20171106"},{"date":"2017-11-20","title":"VLT/Gemini lightcurve: extreme 10:1 elongation (Meech et al., Nature)","kind":"observation","description":"Meech et al. published the coordinated ESO VLT / Gemini / CFHT / UKIRT / Keck lightcurve campaign in Nature: brightness varied by a factor of ~10 every 7.3 hours, implying an axis ratio of at least ~10:1 (later refined to ~6:1–8:1) — more elongated than any known Solar System asteroid or comet. Reddish surface, no dust, no coma, tumbling rotation. The 'cigar' (or 'pancake', per later modeling) shape became anomaly #1 in the alien-tech debate.","sources":["https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1737/","https://www.nature.com/articles/nature25020"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20171120"},{"date":"2017-12-13","title":"Breakthrough Listen scans 'Oumuamua with Green Bank — radio silence","kind":"observation","description":"Breakthrough Listen pointed the 100-m Green Bank Telescope at 'Oumuamua for the first of four observing blocks (3:45–9:45 pm ET), sweeping 1–12 GHz across the L, S, C and X bands and accumulating 90 TB of raw data over the ~2-hour on-target observation. Sensitivity was good enough to detect a transmitter with the power of a cellphone at the object's distance in under a minute. No artificial signals were found then or in the completed analysis (Enriquez et al. 2018) — the natural-vs-artificial question would instead be fought over dynamics, not radio.","sources":["https://breakthroughinitiatives.org/news/14","https://breakthroughinitiatives.org/news/15","https://www.sci.news/astronomy/breakthrough-listen-no-artificial-signals-interstellar-asteroid-oumuamua-05550.html"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20171213"},{"date":"2018-06-27","title":"Non-gravitational acceleration detected (Micheli et al., Nature)","kind":"observation","description":"Micheli et al. reported in Nature that 'Oumuamua's trajectory — reconstructed from ground data plus late Hubble astrometry through January 2018 — required a non-gravitational acceleration of ~4.9e-6 m/s^2 at 1 AU, falling off roughly as 1/r^2. For a comet this is normal outgassing; for an object with no detected coma, dust, or tail it was a paradox. This single measurement is the load-bearing anomaly behind every exotic hypothesis that followed, from hydrogen icebergs to lightsails.","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-018-0254-4","https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1820/"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20180627"},{"date":"2018-10-26","title":"Bialy & Loeb lightsail paper ignites global media storm","kind":"statement","description":"Shmuel Bialy and Avi Loeb (Harvard CfA) posted 'Could Solar Radiation Pressure Explain 'Oumuamua's Peculiar Acceleration?' (arXiv:1810.11490, published ApJL 868 L1 on 2018-11-12). They showed radiation pressure could produce the observed acceleration if the object were a thin sheet with mass-to-area ratio ~0.1 g/cm^2 — about 0.3–0.9 mm thick, a lightsail geometry — and floated that it 'may be a fully operational probe sent intentionally to Earth vicinity by an alien civilization.' The paper triggered a worldwide media storm and made Loeb the public face of the alien-artifact hypothesis.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11490","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/aaeda8"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20181026"},{"date":"2018-11-14","title":"Spitzer non-detection caps the size and rules out normal outgassing","kind":"observation","description":"Trilling et al. (AJ; NASA release Nov 14, 2018) reported that Spitzer stared at 'Oumuamua's position for 30.2 hours on 2017 Nov 21–22 and saw nothing in the infrared. The non-detection capped the size (effective radius roughly 49–220 m depending on albedo, implying a possibly highly reflective surface) and set tight limits on CO/CO2 outgassing — deepening the acceleration paradox: whatever pushed it was not detectable as gas or dust. Bialy & Loeb leaned on these limits.","sources":["https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasa-learns-more-about-interstellar-visitor-oumuamua/","https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08072"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20181114"},{"date":"2019-07-01","title":"ISSI team verdict: 'no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation' (Nature Astronomy)","kind":"statement","description":"The 14-member International Space Science Institute (ISSI) 'Oumuamua team ('The natural history of 'Oumuamua', Bannister et al., Nature Astronomy) published the mainstream consensus review: every observed property — shape, tumbling, color, acceleration — lies within plausible ranges for a natural interstellar planetesimal, and 'we find no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation.' Loeb publicly rejected the conclusion; the paper remains the standard natural-origin reference against which his claims are measured.","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-019-0816-x","https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.01910"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20190701"},{"date":"2020-06-09","title":"Seligman & Laughlin hydrogen-iceberg hypothesis (ApJL)","kind":"statement","description":"Darryl Seligman and Gregory Laughlin proposed (ApJL 896 L8; arXiv:2005.12932) that 'Oumuamua was a hydrogen iceberg formed in the coldest cores of giant molecular clouds: H2 outgassing is nearly invisible (no dust, no detectable coma) yet provides the acceleration, and H2 sublimation sculpting naturally explains the extreme elongation. Critics (including Loeb, and Hoang & Loeb 2020) countered that H2 ice could not survive the interstellar journey — but the paper opened the 'exotic ice' family of natural explanations.","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab963f","https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.12932"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20200609"},{"date":"2021-01-26","title":"Loeb publishes 'Extraterrestrial' — the case goes mainstream","kind":"statement","description":"Avi Loeb's book 'Extraterrestrial: The First Sign of Intelligent Life Beyond Earth' (Houghton Mifflin Harcourt) hit shelves and became an international bestseller, arguing 'Oumuamua's anomalies — the acceleration without a coma, the extreme shape, the unusual initial rest-frame trajectory — are best explained as artificial technology, possibly a defunct lightsail. The book transformed a technical dispute into a public referendum on scientific open-mindedness and directly seeded Loeb's Galileo Project (announced July 2021).","sources":["https://www.hmhbooks.com/shop/books/Extraterrestrial/9780358278146","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20210126"},{"date":"2021-03-16","title":"Desch & Jackson: nitrogen-ice fragment of an exo-Pluto (JGR Planets)","kind":"statement","description":"Steven Desch and Alan Jackson (ASU) published paired papers in JGR: Planets (AGU release Mar 16, 2021) proposing 'Oumuamua was a chip of solid nitrogen ice blasted off a Pluto-like exoplanet ~half a billion years ago: N2 sublimation matches the acceleration with no visible coma, and the high albedo of N2 ice allows a smaller body. Loeb and collaborators (Siraj & Loeb) rebutted that the required mass of exo-Pluto ejecta strains the galactic budget; Desch defended the numbers. The 'nitrogen iceberg' remains a leading natural candidate.","sources":["https://news.agu.org/press-release/interstellar-object-oumuamua-may-be-chip-off-an-icy-n2-covered-exo-pluto/","https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2020JE006706"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20210316"},{"date":"2023-03-22","title":"Bergner & Seligman 'dark comet' H2 answer in Nature — and Loeb's rebuttal","kind":"statement","description":"Jennifer Bergner (Berkeley) and Darryl Seligman published in Nature that cosmic-ray radiolysis of ordinary water ice during 'Oumuamua's interstellar transit created trapped molecular hydrogen; solar warming then released the H2, producing the acceleration with no visible coma — no exotic composition needed. Widely reported as 'mystery solved.' Within days Thiem Hoang and Avi Loeb posted a rebuttal (arXiv:2303.13861; Loeb's Medium essay ''Oumuamua Was Not a Hydrogen-Water Iceberg', Mar 2023) arguing that evaporative cooling by the escaping H2 leaves the surface a factor of ~9 colder than Bergner & Seligman assumed, cutting the H2 thrust below what is required; Bergner countered that the cooling effects do not change the conclusion. The dispute remains unresolved in print.","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-022-05687-w","https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.13861","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/oumuamua-was-not-a-hydrogen-water-iceberg-1dd2f7a6107f","https://www.centauri-dreams.org/2023/03/27/oumuamua-avi-loebs-response-to-the-molecular-hydrogen-theory/"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20230322"},{"date":"2025-08-06","title":"Loeb Scale retroactively ranks 'Oumuamua Level 4 (vs Borisov Level 0)","kind":"statement","description":"Amid the 3I/ATLAS debate, Loeb formalized 'The Loeb Scale: Astronomical Classification of Interstellar Objects' (arXiv:2508.09167, submitted Aug 6, 2025; later published in the International Journal of Astrobiology) — a 0-10 anomaly scale for interstellar objects modeled on the Torino Scale (Levels 0-3 natural, 4-7 increasingly anomalous, 8-10 confirmed artificial). Applied retroactively: 1I/'Oumuamua scores Level 4 (multiple unexplained anomalies), 2I/Borisov Level 0 (the perfectly natural control comet), and 3I/ATLAS Level 4. The scale is Loeb's institutionalization of his 1I-vs-2I framing: Borisov is what a normal interstellar comet looks like; 'Oumuamua is not that.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.09167","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-loeb-scale-astronomical-classification-of-interstellar-objects-62b909644351","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/international-journal-of-astrobiology/article/interstellar-object-significance-scale-loeb-scale-astronomical-classification-of-interstellar-objects/33F927C9600721DF14D1AA8525D7EDC0"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20250806"},{"date":"2026-07-24","title":"Outbound: 53.6 AU from the Sun, headed for Pegasus","kind":"status","description":"Per JPL Horizons, 'Oumuamua is 53.58 AU from the Sun as of today (2026-07-24) — beyond Pluto's average orbit, receding at ~27.0 km/s and asymptotically slowing toward its hyperbolic excess speed of 26.33 km/s, headed for the constellation Pegasus. It passed Neptune's orbital distance in 2022 and is far beyond recovery by any existing telescope; its true nature is now permanently unmeasurable unless a chase mission (e.g. a Project Lyra concept) is ever launched. Every remaining argument is fought over the 2017-2018 data alone.","sources":["https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/ (COMMAND='1I', heliocentric r on 2026-07-24 = 53.580 AU, range-rate 27.0 km/s)","https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/oumuamua/"],"object":"1i","id":"E-20260724"},{"date":"2019-08-30","title":"Discovery by Gennadiy Borisov at MARGO, Crimea","kind":"discovery","description":"Amateur astronomer Gennadiy Borisov discovers a ~magnitude 17.8 comet at low solar elongation using his self-built 0.65 m telescope at his MARGO observatory (MPC code L51) in Nauchnij, Crimea — the first interstellar object ever found by an amateur, and found while it was still inbound (~3 AU from the Sun), unlike 'Oumuamua which was found outbound. It showed a condensed coma and short tail from the start: an unambiguously active comet.","sources":["https://www.minorplanetcenter.net/mpec/K19/K19RA6.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2I/Borisov","https://www.sci.news/astronomy/c2019-q4-second-interstellar-object-07595.html"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20190830"},{"date":"2019-09-11","title":"Provisional designation C/2019 Q4 (Borisov); hyperbolic orbit suspected","kind":"status","description":"The Minor Planet Center announces the object as comet C/2019 Q4 (Borisov) (MPEC 2019-R106), noting the orbit solution 'has converged to the hyperbolic elements shown below, which would indicate an interstellar origin.' The first published solution gave eccentricity e = 3.08 — far beyond any bound solar-system orbit (later refined to e = 3.36) — with JPL's Farnocchia, Bill Gray, and Dave Tholen independently reaching the same conclusion: a likely interstellar comet flagged within two weeks of discovery.","sources":["https://www.minorplanetcenter.net/mpec/K19/K19RA6.html","https://www.sci.news/astronomy/c2019-q4-second-interstellar-object-07595.html","https://www.centauri-dreams.org/2019/09/12/c-2019-q4-borisov-a-likely-interstellar-comet/"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20190911"},{"date":"2019-09-20","title":"First gas detected: cyanogen (CN) with the William Herschel Telescope","kind":"observation","description":"Fitzsimmons et al. detect the CN (0-0) emission band at 388 nm using the 4.2 m William Herschel Telescope + ISIS spectrograph on La Palma (2019 Sep 20.2 UT) — the first spectroscopic detection of gas from any interstellar object. CN production rate (3.7 +/- 0.4) x 10^24 molecules/s at r = 2.7 AU; follow-up work showed 2I is depleted in C2, but overall its gas chemistry looked like a normal solar-system comet (ApJL 885, L9; arXiv:1909.12144).","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab49fc","https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.12144","https://phys.org/news/2019-09-frontier-science-astronomers-gas-molecules.html","https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2019/11/aa36959-19/aa36959-19.html"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20190920"},{"date":"2019-09-24","title":"Confirmed interstellar: named 2I/Borisov (MPEC 2019-S72)","kind":"status","description":"The IAU/Minor Planet Center issues MPEC 2019-S72, confirming the interstellar hyperbolic orbit (e = 3.372 in the circular's solution, from 472 observations spanning Aug 30 - Sep 24) and assigning the permanent designation 2I/Borisov — the second interstellar object and the first unambiguous interstellar comet. The IAU retained the discoverer's name, honoring the amateur discovery.","sources":["https://www.minorplanetcenter.net/mpec/K19/K19S72.html","https://www.sci.news/astronomy/second-known-interstellar-object-2i-borisov-07626.html","https://www.virtualtelescope.eu/2019/09/25/2i-borisov-c-2019q4-the-first-interstellar-comet-is-now-official-a-new-image-24-sept-2019/"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20190924"},{"date":"2019-10-12","title":"First Hubble imaging: tiny nucleus, radius under 0.5 km","kind":"observation","description":"HST/WFC3 obtains the sharpest images yet (Jewitt, Luu et al.), beginning a monitoring campaign through perihelion. Convolution modeling of the coma profile sets a nucleus radius limit of r <= 0.5 km (with r > 0.2 km from the non-gravitational acceleration constraint) — roughly 15x smaller than early ground-based estimates (0.7-3.3 km), per NASA's release. A small, ordinary comet nucleus wrapped in dust (Jewitt et al. 2020, ApJL 888, L23; arXiv:1912.05422).","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab621b","https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.05422","https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.11457","https://hubblesite.org/contents/media/images/2019/53/4578-Image"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20191012"},{"date":"2019-12-08","title":"Perihelion at 2.006 AU","kind":"close_approach","description":"2I/Borisov reaches perihelion at 2.006 AU (just outside Mars' orbit), moving ~44 km/s — well above the ~30 km/s local solar escape speed, with a hyperbolic excess velocity of ~32 km/s that guarantees it will leave the solar system. Throughout, it behaves as a classic active comet — sustained water and CO outgassing, dust coma and tail — the natural 'control case' against 'Oumuamua's inert, accelerating anomaly. Hubble imaged it near perihelion on Dec 9.","sources":["https://esahubble.org/news/heic1922/","https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/interstellar-comet-2iborisov-swings-past-sun/","https://hubblesite.org/contents/media/images/2019/61/4588-Image","https://theskylive.com/c2019q4-info"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20191208"},{"date":"2019-12-28","title":"Closest approach to Earth: ~1.9 AU","kind":"close_approach","description":"The comet makes its closest approach to Earth at roughly 1.9 AU (~290 million km) — never a threat and never close, but well-placed for the VLT, ALMA, and Hubble campaigns that bracketed this date: ALMA had observed Dec 15-16, and Bagnulo's VLT/FORS2 polarimetric monitoring spanned late December 2019 through March 2020.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2I/Borisov","https://theskylive.com/c2019q4-info","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-22000-x"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20191228"},{"date":"2020-03-09","title":"Double outburst: two brightenings in five days","kind":"observation","description":"Between March 4-9, 2020 (outbound, ~2.8 AU), Polish astronomers Drahus and Guzik (Jagiellonian University) with the OGLE team report the comet brightening ~0.7 magnitudes in two distinct outbursts within days — ~0.3 mag between Mar 4.3-5.3 and ~0.4 mag between Mar 8.3-9.3 UT (ATel #13549, issued Mar 12). Jewitt et al. later measured the ejected dust: ~100 km^2 cross-section, ~2 x 10^7 kg — a warning sign of nucleus instability after perihelion heating.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.01242","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab99cb","https://www.astronomerstelegram.org/?read=13549","https://www.sciencealert.com/interstellar-comet-2i-borisov-is-breaking-apart"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20200309"},{"date":"2020-03-30","title":"Hubble catches the nucleus splitting: transient double nucleus","kind":"observation","description":"HST/WFC3 images from two programs (PI Jewitt, ATel #13611; PI Bolin, ATel #13613, announced early April 2020) reveal a fragment ~0.3 arcsec from the optocenter. A transient 'double nucleus' with ~0.6 km^2 cross-section (~10^5 kg of dust) appears on March 30 — absent March 28 and gone by April 3. Jewitt, Kim & Mutchler interpret it as rotational bursting of meter-sized boulders ejected in the March outburst, not catastrophic disruption: the comet shed a splinter but survived (ApJL 896, L39).","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab99cb","https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.01242","https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2020ATel13613....1B/abstract","https://epss.ucla.edu/comet-borisov-the-first-confirmed-interstellar-comet-is-breaking-up/"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20200330"},{"date":"2020-04-20","title":"ALMA: unusually CO-rich coma (Cordiner et al., Nature Astronomy)","kind":"observation","description":"Cordiner, Milam et al. publish ALMA observations (taken 2019 Dec 15-16) detecting HCN and CO — the first direct measurement of gas from another planetary system. CO is 9-26x more abundant relative to water than in average solar-system comets, suggesting 2I/Borisov formed in an extremely cold, CO-ice-rich outer region of its home system, perhaps around a carbon-rich or M-dwarf star (Nature Astronomy 4, 861; the chemically distinct fingerprint of an alien comet).","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-020-1087-2","https://www.almaobservatory.org/en/press-releases/alma-reveals-unusual-composition-of-interstellar-comet-2i-borisov/","https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2020/04/200420124927.htm"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20200420"},{"date":"2021-03-30","title":"VLT polarimetry: 'the most pristine comet ever observed' (Bagnulo et al.)","kind":"observation","description":"Bagnulo et al. publish VLT/FORS2 polarimetry (taken Dec 2019 - Mar 2020) in Nature Communications: 2I/Borisov's polarization exceeds that of all typical comets and its coma is polarimetrically homogeneous, resembling only the unique comet Hale-Bopp. Conclusion: it likely never passed close to any star before ours — possibly the first truly pristine comet ever observed, carrying unaltered material from its birth cloud (Nat. Commun. 12, 1797).","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-22000-x","https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso2106/","https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2021/03/210330121256.htm"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20210330"},{"date":"2021-05-19","title":"Atomic nickel vapor in a cold coma (Guzik & Drahus, Nature)","kind":"observation","description":"Guzik & Drahus report gaseous atomic nickel in 2I/Borisov's coma at 2.322 AU (~180 K equilibrium temperature), produced at 0.9 +/- 0.3 x 10^22 atoms/s, likely from photolysis of a short-lived nickel-bearing molecule (Nature 593, 375). Heavy-metal vapor had previously been seen only in ultrahot environments like sungrazing comets. Published back-to-back with Manfroid et al.'s discovery of Fe and Ni vapor in ~20 solar-system comets — a shared, previously unnoticed chemistry linking 2I to home-grown comets and reinforcing its natural-comet status.","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03485-4","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34012084/","https://www.sci.news/astronomy/nickel-interstellar-comet-2i-borisov-09675.html"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20210519"},{"date":"2025-08-06","title":"Loeb Scale ranks 2I/Borisov Level 0: the natural control case","kind":"statement","description":"In the wake of 3I/ATLAS, the Interstellar Object Significance Scale ('Loeb Scale') paper by Omer Eldadi, Gershon Tenenbaum, and Avi Loeb (arXiv:2508.09167, submitted Aug 6, 2025; later Int. J. Astrobiology) retroactively scores the three known interstellar objects on its 0-10 scale: 1I/'Oumuamua Level 4 (anomalous), 3I/ATLAS Level 4, and 2I/Borisov Level 0 — fully consistent with natural cometary behavior. Loeb has consistently treated Borisov as the benchmark 'ordinary comet' proving the detection pipeline works, sharpening the contrast with 'Oumuamua's anomalies.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.09167","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/international-journal-of-astrobiology/article/interstellar-object-significance-scale-loeb-scale-astronomical-classification-of-interstellar-objects/33F927C9600721DF14D1AA8525D7EDC0"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20250806"},{"date":"2026-07-24","title":"Outbound: receding toward interstellar space","kind":"status","description":"2I/Borisov is far beyond observability, now tens of AU from the Sun and departing on its hyperbolic trajectory at ~32 km/s excess velocity, never to return. Its legacy: it confirmed that other planetary systems make comets much like ours (CN, HCN, dust, outbursts, nickel vapor), yet with distinct fingerprints (extreme CO enrichment, C2 depletion, unusually pristine dust) — the well-behaved sibling that makes 'Oumuamua's and 3I/ATLAS's oddities stand out.","sources":["https://theskylive.com/c2019q4-info","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2I/Borisov","https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.00568"],"object":"2i","id":"E-20260724"}],"compare":[{"designation":"1I/2017 U1","name":"'Oumuamua","discovered":"2017-10-19, Pan-STARRS1 (Haleakala, Hawaii) — found ~40 days after perihelion, on the way out","eccentricity":1.20113,"inclination_deg":122.74,"perihelion_au":0.2559,"perihelion_date":"2017-09-09","closest_earth_au":0.1618,"v_infinity_kms":26.33,"size_estimate":"~100-400 m long (est. 100-1,000 m range), width/thickness ~35-167 m; extreme axis ratio ~6:1 to 10:1 (cigar or pancake shape)","nature":"Inert/rocky-appearing; no detected coma, dust, or gas despite deep searches — yet accelerated like a comet. Composition still debated (nitrogen ice, hydrogen ice, dark organic-rich crust all proposed)","weird_notes":["Non-gravitational acceleration (~5x10^-6 m/s^2 at 1 AU, radial ~2.8x10^-7 AU/d^2) with NO visible outgassing — no coma, no dust, no detected gas to explain it","Extreme lightcurve amplitude ~2.5 magnitudes implying axis ratio of at least 6:1, more elongated than any known solar-system asteroid or comet","Tumbling (non-principal-axis) rotation, period ~7.3-8 h","Red, comet-like surface color but zero cometary activity detected by Spitzer and deep ground-based imaging","Excess velocity vector close to the Local Standard of Rest — dynamically 'young' / unusual for an ejected planetesimal","Only detected post-perihelion at close range; observed arc was short (~2.5 months), so many properties remain unresolved"],"loeb_position":"Loeb (Bialy & Loeb 2018, ApJL) argued the non-gravitational acceleration without outgassing could be explained by solar radiation pressure on a thin, flat artificial object — a lightsail or discarded 'technological debris' from an alien civilization. He expanded this in his 2021 book 'Extraterrestrial,' maintaining the artificial-origin hypothesis is more plausible than exotic natural explanations like hydrogen icebergs."},{"designation":"2I/2019 Q4 (C/2019 Q4)","name":"Borisov","discovered":"2019-08-30, Gennadiy Borisov (amateur astronomer), 0.65-m MARGO observatory, Nauchnij, Crimea","eccentricity":3.356,"inclination_deg":44.05,"perihelion_au":2.007,"perihelion_date":"2019-12-08","closest_earth_au":1.9,"v_infinity_kms":32.2,"size_estimate":"Nucleus effective radius 0.2-0.5 km (HST); early ~975 m estimates were revised down. Coma/tail much larger (tail ~160,000 km)","nature":"Clearly cometary from first detection — bright coma and tail, normal dust and gas production. The 'ordinary' interstellar object; behaved like a moderately active solar-system comet","weird_notes":["Extremely CO-rich: CO/H2O ratio 3x+ higher than any comet measured in the inner solar system, suggesting formation beyond the CO ice line of a cold M-dwarf or outer disk (Bodewits et al. / Cordiner et al. 2020)","NH2-rich chemistry relative to typical comets","Unusually pristine dust — suggested it had never before passed close to any star","Nucleus fragmented/split in March 2020 after an outburst, post-perihelion (a normal thing for comets to do, but notable)","Otherwise the control case: proved interstellar comets can look exactly like ordinary comets"],"loeb_position":"Loeb accepted Borisov as a genuine natural comet and repeatedly uses it as his control case: 'Borisov looked like a comet from day one, which shows astronomers can recognize a comet when they see one — making 'Oumuamua's weirdness stand out.' He noted its pristine condition was mildly interesting but did not claim artificiality."},{"designation":"3I/ATLAS (C/2025 N1)","name":"ATLAS","discovered":"2025-07-01, ATLAS survey telescope, Rio Hurtado, Chile (prediscovery images: ATLAS back to 2025-06-05, ZTF to 2025-06-14; TESS imagery from May 2025 recovered later)","eccentricity":6.14135,"inclination_deg":175.12,"perihelion_au":1.35645,"perihelion_date":"2025-10-29 (11:45 UT); perihelion occurred behind the Sun as seen from Earth","closest_earth_au":1.798,"v_infinity_kms":58,"size_estimate":"Nucleus diameter 0.32-5.6 km (HST upper/lower bounds); non-gravitational-acceleration-based estimate ~0.82-1.05 km diameter (Forbes & Butler 2025, for density 0.5 g/cm^3); Loeb et al.'s pre-perihelion NGA upper limit instead implied a MINIMUM mass ~33 billion metric tons (3.3x10^13 kg) and diameter >~5 km — the two are in tension; rotation period 16.16 h","nature":"Active comet, CO2-dominated: JWST/NIRSpec (2025-08-06, 3.32 AU) measured CO2/H2O mixing ratio 7.6+/-0.3 (exceptionally high, 4.5-sigma above the comet trend), Q(CO2) 9.5x10^26 molecules/s (~70 kg/s) vs Q(H2O) 1.07x10^26 (~3 kg/s) pre-perihelion; also CO, carbonyl sulfide, water ice, later methane, plus nickel vapor. Likely an old, thick-crusted comet from the Milky Way thick disk. NASA/ESA and mainstream astronomy: natural comet","weird_notes":["Most eccentric orbit ever recorded for a solar-system-passing object: e = 6.14135 +/- 0.00002 (vs 1.2 for 1I, 3.36 for 2I) — v_inf ~58 km/s, faster than both predecessors","Retrograde orbit inclined only ~5 deg from the ecliptic plane (i = 175.12 deg) — Loeb puts chance alignment at ~0.2%","Trajectory threaded close to Mars (0.19 AU, 2025-10-03), Venus, and Jupiter (0.357 AU, 2026-03-16) while unobservable from Earth at perihelion","CO2/H2O ratio ~8 (7.6+/-0.3, JWST) — among the highest ever measured in any comet","Nickel-rich gas plume with anomalously low iron and Ni/CN ratio orders of magnitude above known comets (detected Aug 2025, VLT/UVES; Ni production ~0.9 g/s rising to ~5 g/s by 2.8 AU)","Pre-perihelion sunward anti-tail/collimated jet, later roughly symmetric jets post-perihelion; reported mini-jets separated ~120 deg","Non-gravitational acceleration detected at perihelion with a substantial non-radial (sideways) component (per Loeb's analysis of MPC/JPL astrometry)","Extreme negative polarization reported as unprecedented among known comets; near perihelion it brightened faster than known comets and appeared bluer than the Sun","Post-perihelion chemistry shift: methane emerged as ices faded — first CH4 detection on an interstellar object (Belyakov et al. 2026, ApJL, JWST/MIRI, Dec 2025 obs): CH4/H2O mixing ratio 11.0+/-0.5% rising to 21.6+/-1.3%, vs 0.1-10% for typical solar-system comets; separate June 2026 Nature paper (Cordiner et al., JWST isotopes: 12C/13C = 147, 14N/15N = 343) points to a cold, distant birth environment","Big and fast: nucleus estimated 3-5 orders of magnitude more massive than both 1I and 2I (Loeb: 'at least 100,000x Oumuamua'), in tension with expected interstellar-object mass distribution","Jupiter flyby 2026-03-16 at 0.357 AU passed as predicted on the gravitational trajectory (perijove 53.445 Mkm ~ Jupiter's Hill radius 53.502 Mkm — Loeb anomaly); ESA's Juice spacecraft (MAJIS, obs 2025-11-02, published 2026-04-02) measured water output ~2,000 kg/s (~70 Olympic pools/day) and a tail ~6 million km long","Status July 2026: outbound beyond Jupiter's distance in Gemini, magnitude ~15 and fading, leaving the solar system permanently (beyond Saturn's orbit and fainter than mag 20 by ~Sept 2026)"],"loeb_position":"Loeb maintained a running numbered anomaly file (reaching 22 anomalies by his March 2026 Medium summary '3I/ATLAS is Fading Away...'), assigning individual probabilities to coincidences (perijove ~ Jupiter's Hill radius, Wow! Signal alignment within 9 deg at 0.6% probability, ecliptic alignment, jet geometry). He rated it 4 on his 0-10 'Loeb scale' (10 = alien threat) — anomalous enough to warrant the technological hypothesis but 'slightly less anomalous' than 'Oumuamua — and repeatedly urged agencies to collect more data rather than dismiss the possibility; NASA/ESA and nearly all astronomers concluded it is a natural, unusually CO2-rich interstellar comet."}],"quotes":[{"text":"As of now, I assign a 30 to 40 percent likelihood that 3I/ATLAS does not have a fully natural origin","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-10-09","camp":"loeb","context":"Probability estimate of non-natural origin, as reported while anomaly count grew","source":"Futurism (futurism.com/space/astronomer-probability-alien-craft-comet)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"This low-probability scenario includes the possibility of a black swan event akin to a Trojan Horse, where a technological object masquerades as a natural comet.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-10-09","camp":"loeb","context":"Explaining his 'black swan' framing of the technological hypothesis","source":"Futurism, quoting Loeb's Medium blog","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"As far as I know, there are no protocols for responding to the discovery of functioning alien devices near Earth.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-10-09","camp":"loeb","context":"On planetary preparedness if 3I/ATLAS proved technological","source":"Futurism, quoting Loeb","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"As largely a pedagogical exercise, in this paper we present additional analysis into the astrodynamics of 3I/ATLAS, and hypothesize that this object could be technological","speaker":"Avi Loeb (with Adam Hibberd, Adam Crowl)","date":"2025-07-16","camp":"loeb","context":"Abstract of the paper 'Is the Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS Alien Technology?'","source":"arXiv:2507.12213","verify":"VERBATIM","verify_source":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12213"},{"text":"possibly hostile as would be expected from the 'Dark Forest' resolution to the 'Fermi Paradox'","speaker":"Avi Loeb (with Adam Hibberd, Adam Crowl)","date":"2025-07-16","camp":"loeb","context":"The 'dark forest' line in the same arXiv paper's abstract","source":"arXiv:2507.12213","verify":"VERBATIM","verify_source":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12213"},{"text":"If it is technological — it may maneuver to benefit from the Sun's gravitational assist or release probes that could intercept the Earth or other planets owing to its retrograde orbit.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-10-03","camp":"loeb","context":"Warning about the perihelion pass hidden behind the Sun, in his Mars-flyby anomaly recap","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (A Recap of the Anomalies of 3I/ATLAS on the Day of Its Closest Approach to Mars)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"If we find technological satellites of Jupiter that we did not send, it would imply that Jupiter is of interest to an extraterrestrial civilization.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2026-03","camp":"loeb","context":"On the March 16, 2026 Jupiter approach at ~Jupiter's Hill radius and the idea of released 'devices'","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (Jupiter-approach essays); IBTimes UK coverage","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"NASA's representatives should have emphasized what we do not understand about 3I/ATLAS rather than insist that it is a familiar comet from a new birth environment.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-11-19","camp":"loeb","context":"Reaction to NASA's post-shutdown press conference","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (There is Nothing More Deceptive than an Obvious Fact)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"We should be humble about the little we know and curious about 3I/ATLAS rather than insist that we know its nature before data is shared and analyzed.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-11-03","camp":"loeb","context":"Essay inviting Kim Kardashian to join his research team, criticizing NASA data withholding","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (Kim Kardashian is Welcome to Join my Research Team on 3I/ATLAS)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"Scientific truth is not decided by authority or popularity but by attention to data.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-11","camp":"loeb","context":"Response to astronomers insisting 3I/ATLAS is a natural comet","source":"Newsweek interview","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"It is the responsibility of scientists and NASA officials to acknowledge the existence of unexplained puzzles rather than display the arrogance of expertise while ignoring them.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2026-03-12","camp":"loeb","context":"Farewell essay as the object faded, tallying 22 anomalies and lowering it to Loeb Scale 3","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (3I/ATLAS is Fading Away, Leaving Us to Ponder Over Its 22 Mysterious Anomalies)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"Given how well aligned its trajectory was with the orbital plane of Earth around the Sun, we might never witness a visitor like it in our lifetime.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2026-04-23","camp":"loeb","context":"Status essay with 3I/ATLAS a billion km out, well beyond Jupiter, leaving the solar system","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (Where is 3I/ATLAS Now?)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"This object is a comet. It looks and behaves like a comet, and all evidence points to it being a comet.","speaker":"Amit Kshatriya, NASA Associate Administrator","date":"2025-11-19","camp":"official","context":"NASA press conference releasing post-shutdown 3I/ATLAS imagery","source":"NASA live event; LiveNOW from FOX / Space.com coverage","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"We very much want to find signs of life in our universe... but 3I/ATLAS is a comet.","speaker":"Amit Kshatriya, NASA Associate Administrator","date":"2025-11-19","camp":"official","context":"Same NASA press conference, addressing alien-technology speculation directly","source":"NASA press conference; LiveNOW from FOX coverage","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"Everybody has got a camera and they're trying to get a picture of the ball. Nobody has the perfect view, and everybody has a different camera.","speaker":"Tom Statler, NASA lead scientist for solar system small bodies","date":"2025-11-19","camp":"official","context":"Baseball-stadium analogy for the multi-spacecraft observation campaign at the NASA press conference","source":"NASA press conference; Space.com coverage","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"Great question! NASA's observations show that this is the third interstellar comet to pass through our solar system. No aliens. No threat to life here on Earth.","speaker":"Sean Duffy, NASA Acting Administrator","date":"2025-11-03","camp":"official","context":"Reply on X to Kim Kardashian's question about 3I/ATLAS","source":"X (Twitter); quoted in Loeb's Medium essay and IBTimes UK","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"[paraphrase] Wait... what's the tea on 3I/ATLAS?!","speaker":"Kim Kardashian","date":"2025-11-03","camp":"media","context":"X post asking NASA about the interstellar object; outlets render the punctuation differently","source":"X (Twitter); IBTimes UK / Express Tribune coverage","verify":"PARAPHRASE","verify_source":""},{"text":"Any suggestion that it's artificial is nonsense on stilts, and is an insult to the exciting work going on to understand this object.","speaker":"Chris Lintott, University of Oxford astronomer","date":"2025-07","camp":"media","context":"Reacting to Loeb's alien-technology paper shortly after discovery","source":"Live Science (Here we go again! Controversial paper...)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"There have been numerous telescopic observations of 3I/ATLAS demonstrating that it's displaying classical signatures of cometary activity.","speaker":"Darryl Seligman, Michigan State University astronomer","date":"2025-07","camp":"media","context":"Lead author of the first characterization study, pushing back on the artificial hypothesis","source":"Live Science / Newsweek interviews","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"You have a piece of another star system that's close enough to home that we can actually study it in detail.","speaker":"Karen Meech, University of Hawaii astronomer","date":"2025-07","camp":"media","context":"On the scientific value of the third interstellar visitor after discovery","source":"University of Michigan News / phys.org","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"Considering an artificial origin, one possibility is that 'Oumuamua is a lightsail, floating in interstellar space as a debris from an advanced technological equipment.","speaker":"Shmuel Bialy & Abraham Loeb","date":"2018-11-12","camp":"loeb","context":"The lightsail sentence from the paper that launched the alien-technology hypothesis; verified verbatim against the arXiv full text.","source":"Bialy & Loeb, 'Could Solar Radiation Pressure Explain 'Oumuamua's Peculiar Acceleration?', ApJL 868 L1 (arXiv:1810.11490, posted 2018-10-26)","verify":"CORRECTED","verify_source":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11490"},{"text":"Alternatively, a more exotic scenario is that 'Oumuamua may be a fully operational probe sent intentionally to Earth vicinity by an alien civilization.","speaker":"Shmuel Bialy & Abraham Loeb","date":"2018-11-12","camp":"loeb","context":"The paper's most-quoted line, immediately following the lightsail-debris suggestion; verified verbatim against the arXiv full text.","source":"Bialy & Loeb, ApJL 868 L1 (arXiv:1810.11490)","verify":"VERBATIM","verify_source":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11490"},{"text":"'Oumuamua was like a buoy resting in the expanse of the universe, and our solar system was like a ship that ran into it at high speed.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2021-01-26","camp":"loeb","context":"Loeb's signature buoy analogy from his bestselling book — 'Oumuamua as a stationary object the solar system ran into, echoing its LSR-rest-frame kinematics.","source":"Loeb, 'Extraterrestrial: The First Sign of Intelligent Life Beyond Earth' (Houghton Mifflin Harcourt)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"If radiation pressure is the accelerating force, then 'Oumuamua represents a new class of thin interstellar material, either produced naturally . . . or is of an artificial origin.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2021-01-26","camp":"loeb","context":"Loeb's book-form framing of the radiation-pressure argument; near-identical sentence appears in the 2018 paper. Verified via published book excerpts.","source":"Loeb, 'Extraterrestrial' (restating the Bialy-Loeb argument)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"Thus, we find no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation for 'Oumuamua.","speaker":"The 'Oumuamua ISSI Team (Bannister, Meech, Knight, Fitzsimmons et al.)","date":"2019-07-01","camp":"official","context":"The 14-expert International Space Science Institute team's formal verdict, closing the section that assessed the artificial hypothesis; verified verbatim from the paper PDF.","source":"'The Natural History of 'Oumuamua', Nature Astronomy 3, 594-602 (arXiv:1907.01910)","verify":"VERBATIM","verify_source":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.01910"},{"text":"Assertions that 'Oumuamua may be artificial are not justified when the wide body of current knowledge about solar system minor bodies and planetary formation is considered.","speaker":"The 'Oumuamua ISSI Team","date":"2019-07-01","camp":"official","context":"The paper's concluding rebuttal sentence; verified verbatim from the arXiv PDF Conclusions.","source":"'The Natural History of 'Oumuamua', Nature Astronomy — Conclusions section","verify":"VERBATIM","verify_source":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.01910"},{"text":"We have never seen anything like 'Oumuamua in our solar system. It's really a mystery still.","speaker":"Matthew Knight","date":"2019-07-01","camp":"official","context":"ISSI team co-lead acknowledging the object remains strange even while ruling it natural — the honest tension in the official position.","source":"University of Maryland press release for the ISSI Nature Astronomy paper","verify":"VERBATIM","verify_source":"https://cmns.umd.edu/news-events/news/oumuamua-not-alien-spacecraft"},{"text":"The alien spacecraft hypothesis is a fun idea, but our analysis suggests there is a whole host of natural phenomena that could explain it.","speaker":"Matthew Knight","date":"2019-07-01","camp":"official","context":"The ISSI team's public-facing dismissal of the Loeb lightsail interpretation.","source":"University of Maryland / ISSI team press release (also carried by Sci-News, EurekAlert)","verify":"VERBATIM","verify_source":"https://cmns.umd.edu/news-events/news/oumuamua-not-alien-spacecraft"},{"text":"This unusually large variation in brightness means that the object is highly elongated: about ten times as long as it is wide, with a complex, convoluted shape.","speaker":"Karen Meech","date":"2017-11-20","camp":"official","context":"Discovery-team lead describing the record 10:1 elongation from the VLT lightcurve; she added it was 'completely inert, without the faintest hint of dust around it.'","source":"ESO press release eso1737, 'ESO Observations Show First Interstellar Asteroid is Like Nothing Seen Before' (VLT results, Meech et al. Nature 552)","verify":"VERBATIM","verify_source":"https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1737/"},{"text":"I think it's a remnant from another solar system. It's just something that happened to run into us, and we were very lucky to have been operating the telescope that night and looking in that direction.","speaker":"Robert Weryk","date":"2018-11-12","camp":"official","context":"The Pan-STARRS astronomer who discovered 'Oumuamua, dismissing the Bialy-Loeb lightsail idea as 'wild speculation' — 'we actually believe that's not true based on the data we obtained.'","source":"CBC News interview, 'Oumuamua not an alien probe, says scientist who discovered it' (also carried by Fox News 2018-11-12)","verify":"CORRECTED","verify_source":"https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/london/oumuamua-comet-not-an-alien-probe-says-robert-weryk-1.4899551"},{"text":"I saw a moving object in the frame, it moved in a direction that was slightly different from that of main asteroids.","speaker":"Gennadiy Borisov","date":"2019-09-16","camp":"official","context":"The Crimean amateur astronomer describing the 2019-08-30 pre-dawn discovery of 2I/Borisov with his self-built 0.65 m telescope at MARGO observatory. English is a translation.","source":"RIA Novosti interview (translated from Russian; as cited on the 2I/Borisov Wikipedia discovery account)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"Every amateur … dreams of discovering something unusual and extraordinary. When you manage to do that, you feel joy and happiness, and you set a new task.","speaker":"Gennadiy Borisov","date":"2019-12-08","camp":"official","context":"Borisov on what the discovery meant to him, published the day his comet reached perihelion (~2 AU).","source":"Space.com Q&A with Meghan Bartels (email interview via Roscosmos), published on 2I/Borisov's perihelion day","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"2I/Borisov could represent the first truly pristine comet ever observed.","speaker":"Stefano Bagnulo","date":"2021-03-30","camp":"official","context":"The 'most pristine comet' line: VLT polarimetry showed 2I/Borisov more pristine than Hale-Bopp, apparently never having passed close to any star — the classic-comet control case to 'Oumuamua.","source":"ESO press release eso2106, 'First interstellar comet may be the most pristine ever found' (VLT polarimetry; Bagnulo et al., Nature Communications)","verify":"VERBATIM","verify_source":"https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso2106/"},{"text":"1I/'Oumuamua is anomalous relative to 2I/Borisov and 3I/ATLAS … it had an extreme disk-like shape, it exhibited a non-gravitational acceleration and it did not show any evidence for cometary activity.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-07-09","camp":"loeb","context":"Loeb's retrospective three-object comparison after 3I/ATLAS's discovery: Borisov and ATLAS behave like comets, 'Oumuamua still stands apart. Ellipsis trims 'not only because it is much younger than they are.' Verified verbatim.","source":"Loeb, 'Origin and Age of Three Interstellar Objects: 1I/'Oumuamua, 2I/Borisov, and 3I/ATLAS', Medium essay","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""},{"text":"3I/ATLAS is most likely a comet of natural origin, but there are 8 anomalies that endow it with a rank of 4 on the Loeb scale.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-12-21","camp":"loeb","context":"Loeb applying his 0-10 anomaly scale to 3I/ATLAS; the companion Loeb-scale framework retroactively treats 2I/Borisov as a rank-0 natural baseline while 'Oumuamua keeps an elevated rank. Verified verbatim from the Medium essay.","source":"Loeb, 'Updating the Loeb Classification Scale of 3I/ATLAS', Medium (the Loeb scale itself defined in Trivedi & Loeb, arXiv:2508.09167, Aug 2025)","verify":"SECONDARY","verify_source":""}],"briefings":[{"id":"BR-01","question":"Is 3I/ATLAS a comet, or something else?","short":"It behaves like a comet in most ways and like nothing on record in several. None of the anomalies requires design; the argument is about how many coincidences one object is allowed.","tag":"26 CASES ON FILE","sections":[{"h":"What is not in dispute","p":"3I/ATLAS is the third confirmed interstellar object, after 1I/ʻOumuamua in 2017 and 2I/Borisov in 2019. Its orbit is unambiguously unbound — it came from outside the solar system and it is leaving. It reached perihelion on 2025-10-29 at 1.3566 AU, passed Mars at 0.1939 AU, and passed Jupiter at 0.3588 AU in March 2026. Those are measured positions, not interpretations, and the console draws its trajectory from JPL Horizons vectors rather than from an artist's idea of one."},{"h":"What is genuinely strange","p":"A cluster of measurements sit far outside the range of ordinary comets. A sunward anti-tail that persisted before and after perihelion. Nickel emission without the iron that normally accompanies it. Extreme negative polarization. Methanol enriched against hydrogen cyanide at levels beaten by exactly one solar-system comet. Deuterium far above any local object. A trajectory within about 5 degrees of the ecliptic, retrograde, arriving with close passes of three planets. Each has a published measurement behind it, and the register gives each its own case file."},{"h":"Why that is not the same as evidence of design","p":"Almost every anomaly has a cometary explanation that works, and several have more than one. The harder problem is that the anomalies are not independent: a single wrong assumption about the nucleus size, or one unusual formation environment, propagates into many of the individual oddities at once. A tally of 24 or 26 separate anomalies overstates the evidence if one cause produces a dozen of them — and that cuts both ways, because a single unifying mechanism is also exactly what a correct explanation looks like."},{"h":"Where the argument actually stands","p":"Avi Loeb ranked the object 4 on his own 0-10 scale in July 2025, held it at 4 that December, and reduced it to 3 in March 2026 after the Jupiter pass produced nothing unusual. That is the person making the strongest published case revising downward on evidence. The mainstream position was never that the object is boring — it is that an extreme natural comet from an old, low-metallicity system explains what has been measured without requiring anything new."}],"chart":"acceleration","cases":["3i/A-01","3i/A-05","3i/A-07","3i/A-26"],"sources":["https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/"]},{"id":"BR-02","question":"Are fireballs actually increasing?","short":"Four instruments, four biases, and only the bias-corrected statistics are worth comparing. The eyewitness figures are accurate; every measure that normalises for instrument growth is flat — including, now, one that sees daylight.","tag":"FOUR INSTRUMENTS","sections":[{"h":"The claim, and why it deserves a straight answer","p":"Through 2026 a widely-repeated argument holds that fireball activity has surged. The sharpest version of it is worth engaging precisely because it anticipates the obvious objection: not merely that more events were reported, but that events drawing 50 or more eyewitness reports roughly doubled — so the larger, brighter events themselves increased. Checked against the American Meteor Society's own published statistics, those numbers are correct. Q1 2026 logged 25 events in the 51-99 report band against a 2021-2025 mean of 11.8, and 16 above 100 reports against 8.8."},{"h":"Why the report count cannot answer it","p":"AMS counts people, not photons. A growing audience does two things at once: it surfaces more events, and it gives each event more reports — which pushes events upward through the report bands. An event drawing 20 reports in 2022 draws 45 in 2026 and leaves the 11-25 band entirely. So a flat middle band is the bias rather than evidence against it. The diagnostic is whether well-witnessed events outgrew the dataset as a whole. They did not: 1.21 times the baseline against 1.23 times for all events."},{"h":"Why the sensor record cannot answer it either","p":"NASA's CNEOS catalog measures energy directly and cannot be moved by how many people were watching. It shows 2026 at the bottom of an ordinary range. But its smallest entry in 1,069 rows is 0.048 kilotonnes, and almost every event in this argument falls far below that. Across all of 2025 and 2026, only four CNEOS rows fall anywhere in the regions this coverage discusses. The sensors are not staying quiet about these fireballs — with a handful of exceptions they cannot detect them at all."},{"h":"The instrument that can, and what it found","p":"The Global Meteor Network runs cameras that measure an absolute magnitude for every meteor, with nobody deciding whether to file a report. Its network grew from 73 stations to over 1,300, so raw counts are meaningless across years — but the bright-meteor share is not. That share ran 0.286% in Q1 2026 against a 0.264% five-year mean: a factor of 1.08, inside year-to-year scatter three times wider. The obvious confound was ruled out rather than assumed, since more sensitive cameras would swell the faint end and mask a rise: the median magnitude holds between -0.09 and +0.05 across six years."},{"h":"Three limits that cut the other way","p":"GMN sees nothing in daylight, and several of the most prominent 2026 events were daytime — the Lake Erie bolide at 8:57 a.m. Eastern drew 222 reports across 16 states. Its coverage is concentrated in Europe and North America rather than global. Its brightest bin is small and noisy. So this is not a refutation, and the central observation stands exactly as stated. What has moved is the burden: every measure that corrects for instrument growth is flat, and the cameras built to measure brightness did not record the change."},{"h":"The fourth instrument, and the objection it answers","p":"This briefing used to end on a limit it could not resolve: the Global Meteor Network carried the strongest evidence, and GMN is night-blind, while several of 2026's most talked-about events happened in daylight. NASA's Geostationary Lightning Mapper pipeline closes that gap. Staring down from geostationary orbit, day and night are the same to it — 47.3%% of its 11410 published bolide detections are daytime, which is roughly the half of the population a ground camera network never sees. Over the same window, its bright-category share runs 3.00%% in 2026 against a 2.80%% baseline, a factor of 1.07. GMN's figure was 1.07. Two instruments on different physics, one blind to half the clock, agreeing to two decimals."},{"h":"And the trap inside it","p":"GLM is not a free win, and the catalog carries a hazard worth naming because it is the kind that quietly ruins this sort of comparison. On 2025-03-06 its pipeline started auto-publishing detections that previously needed a human to review them, so the recent years hold a class of record the earlier ones do not. The intuitive fix — filter to high-confidence events — makes it worse, because review lags behind, so filtering on confidence really filters on how long ago something happened. Running the statistic with and without the auto-published events is the fix that works: the bright SHARE barely moves, the bright COUNT swings hard. That is why the share is quoted and the count is not."}],"chart":"glm-daylight","cases":["fb/F-03","fb/F-04"],"sources":["https://neo-bolide.ndc.nasa.gov/","https://fireball.amsmeteors.org/members/imo_fireball_stats/events_per_month_per_year","https://globalmeteornetwork.org/data/traj_summary_data/","https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/"]},{"id":"BR-03","question":"Has anything interstellar actually hit Earth?","short":"Two candidates, both from one classified sensor catalog that publishes no error bars. One got a memo from Space Command; neither has been confirmed by anything outside the dataset that reported it.","tag":"IM1 · IM2","sections":[{"h":"The two rows","p":"On 2014-01-08 a fireball over the Bismarck Sea was logged at 44.8 km/s — among the fastest entries CNEOS lists. On 2017-03-09 a larger one over the North Atlantic was logged at 36.5 km/s. At those speeds both are gravitationally unbound: they came from outside the solar system. Siraj and Loeb argued the first is interstellar at 99.999% confidence, and in April 2022 the US Space Command chief scientist reviewed the classified data and signed a memo saying the velocity estimate was accurate enough to indicate an interstellar trajectory."},{"h":"The problem with the only number that matters","p":"Everything rests on the speed, and CNEOS publishes no uncertainties on any field. Brown and Borovička compared USG fireball velocities against events with independent optical or infrasound data and found errors of 10-15 km/s at high speeds. Shave a few km/s off either event and the orbit is bound and ordinary. That also dissolves the separate argument that these bodies were unusually strong, because the strength was inferred from ablation fits that only work at the high assumed speed — two anomalies that are really one measurement."},{"h":"What the expedition found","p":"In 2023 the Galileo Project ran a magnetic sled over the 2014 search box and recovered hundreds of sub-millimetre spherules with an unusual beryllium-lanthanum-uranium enrichment. Critics matched that pattern to coal fly ash, a common shipping-lane contaminant, and a separate team reported that the seismic signal used to pick the site fitted a truck passing the Manus Island seismometer. Loeb's reply is that the search followed the 11-km satellite box, not the seismometer."},{"h":"Where it rests","p":"The most sympathetic independent verdict redid the statistics and put the probability of an interstellar origin at 94.1% — significant, and short of the three sigma a discovery claim needs. A survey of every nominally hyperbolic CNEOS entry concluded that without published uncertainties the data cannot confirm or reject an interstellar origin for any of them. The honest summary is not that these events did not happen. It is that a single un-audited catalog, with the error bars withheld, cannot carry a claim this size alone."}],"chart":"speed-dist","cases":["fb/F-01","fb/F-02"],"sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ac8eac","https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14267","https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/"]},{"id":"BR-04","question":"Three visitors. Why is only one of them contested?","short":"2I/Borisov settled the question of whether interstellar comets look normal: it looked entirely normal. That is what makes the other two interesting, and it is why the register keeps it on file.","tag":"1I · 2I · 3I","sections":[{"h":"The control case","p":"2I/Borisov arrived in 2019 and behaved like a comet in every respect that was measured — a coma, a tail pointing away from the Sun, a composition inside the range of solar-system comets apart from unusually high carbon monoxide. Nobody argued about it, and its file here is thin by design. It matters enormously anyway: it demonstrates that an object from another star can be completely ordinary, which removes 'it came from far away' as an explanation for strangeness."},{"h":"The original argument","p":"1I/ʻOumuamua in 2017 had no coma at all, an extreme elongation, and a non-gravitational acceleration with no visible outgassing to cause it. Bialy and Loeb proposed a thin lightsail. The competing explanations — a hydrogen iceberg, a nitrogen ice fragment, a fractal dust aggregate — each account for the acceleration without any of them being comfortable. It left too fast for anyone to check, which is the whole reason the case never closed."},{"h":"Why 3I is different from both","p":"3I/ATLAS is unambiguously active, which rules out the ʻOumuamua reading directly: it has a coma, jets, and measurable outgassing. But its composition and jet geometry are unlike Borisov's, and unlike any solar-system comet in several specific measurements. So it sits between the two earlier objects rather than resembling either, and it was observed for over a year by Webb, Hubble, ALMA and spacecraft near Mars and Jupiter — which is why its file runs to 26 cases and the others do not."},{"h":"What the three together suggest","p":"Three objects is not a population, but it is enough to say the visitors are not all alike. If interstellar objects were uniformly ordinary, Borisov would be the template and the other two would need explaining away. If they were uniformly strange, Borisov would be the anomaly. Neither holds. The most defensible reading is that these bodies come from varied environments and carry varied chemistry, and that our sample is far too small to know which of the three is typical."}],"chart":"trajectory","cases":["1i/O-07","1i/O-06","2i/B-01","3i/A-12"],"sources":["https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2018ApJ...868L...1B/abstract","https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/"]},{"id":"BR-05","question":"What can these datasets actually settle?","short":"Less than either side usually claims. Every dataset here has a bias that can manufacture a trend on its own, and knowing which bias applies to which question is most of the analysis.","tag":"HOW TO READ THIS CONSOLE","sections":[{"h":"A catalog is not a census","p":"The CNEOS fireball record contains 1,069 rows going back to 1988, and the count per year steps sharply upward in 1994 and again around 2000 before going flat for 26 years. Those early years are not quiet years; they are years with almost no reporting, because the satellite record these detections come from begins in the mid-1990s. CNEOS says as much itself: not all fireballs are reported, and the agency does not independently verify the events. A record whose own publisher says it is incomplete cannot be read as a measurement of the sky."},{"h":"Detection floors decide arguments","p":"CNEOS lists no event below 0.048 kilotonnes, and only 6 of its 1,069 rows fall under 0.073. That floor is where a great many arguments quietly live. A skeptic cannot cite sensor silence about events the sensors cannot detect; a proponent cannot cite confirmation from an instrument that never saw the events in question. The gap between what eyewitnesses can see and what sensors can measure is roughly 0.001 to 0.05 kilotonnes wide, and it is a property of the hardware rather than a debating position."},{"h":"Growing instruments look like changing skies","p":"Three datasets here grew enormously over their own record: US sensor coverage, the eyewitness reporting audience, and a camera network that went from 73 stations to over 1,300. Every one of them produces a rising raw count that has nothing to do with the sky. The fix is always the same — divide by something that grew with it. Bright events as a share of all events. Events above an energy a detection change cannot inflate. Meteors per detection rather than meteors. If a trend survives that division it is worth discussing; if it does not, what grew was the instrument."},{"h":"What this console does with that","p":"Every case file gives the claim and the counter-reading side by side, names the primary source, and marks whether the figures were verified against it. Where a claim is stated accurately, the register says so plainly before disagreeing with the inference. Where the data cannot settle something, it says that too instead of implying a verdict it has not earned. The fiction — the clearance banners, the stamps — is decoration. The datasets, the citations and the arithmetic are not."}],"chart":"fireball-rate","cases":["fb/F-03","fb/F-01"],"sources":["https://ssd-api.jpl.nasa.gov/doc/fireball.html","https://www.nature.com/articles/nature01238"]},{"id":"BR-06","question":"Do fireballs really come down at volcanoes?","short":"Tested directly against 883 located events and 1,608 volcanoes: no association at any distance, under either null model. The nearest bin sits below chance. But the reason the footage looks so convincing is worth understanding.","tag":"ORIGINAL ANALYSIS","sections":[{"h":"Why this claim is testable rather than arguable","p":"Most disputes in this register turn on interpretation. This one does not. It is a positional claim, and both halves are already published: the console ships 883 CNEOS fireballs with coordinates, and NOAA lists 1,608 volcano positions. Either the events fall closer to volcanoes than chance allows, or they do not, and a Monte Carlo settles it. Nobody appears to have run it, which is the main reason it is here."},{"h":"Getting the null right is the whole test","p":"Comparing against random points scattered over the globe would be wrong. Volcanoes sit on land and cluster in arcs, so any land or latitude bias in the detections would fake an association out of nothing. Two nulls were used instead, both preserving the events' own latitude distribution. The first applies a single random longitude offset to every event at once — keeping the event set's internal clustering perfectly intact, destroying only its alignment with volcano longitudes. The second gives each event a fresh longitude. A thousand trials of each."},{"h":"The answer","p":"Within 100 km of a volcano: 23 events observed, 32.8 expected by chance. Within 200 km: 78 against 82.3. Within 500 km: 260 against 250.5. Median distance to the nearest volcano: 838 km observed, 854 km by chance. Nothing significant anywhere, under either null — and the closest bin, the one the claim is actually about, comes in below chance rather than above it. The related 'three in the same place' clustering claim fails the same way: the median distance from an event to its nearest neighbour is 353 km against 354 km expected."},{"h":"Why the footage is still striking","p":"The answer is in the coverage itself, said in passing: 'Five Vulks cameras are rolling.' PHIVOLCS, the Philippine volcanology institute, keeps cameras pointed at Mayon's sky around the clock, because that is what a volcano observatory does. Active volcanoes are among the very few places on Earth with fixed cameras aimed upward twenty-four hours a day, recording continuously, watched by people who publish what they catch. A fireball over farmland is seen by whoever looked up; a fireball over a monitored volcano is filmed in high definition from several angles and posted the same day. That is enough to produce the whole pattern without anything unusual happening in the sky."},{"h":"The limit that cuts the other way","p":"Neither the Mayon event of 2026-05-25 nor the bright 2026-06-14 fireball has a CNEOS row at all — both sit below the catalog's 0.048 kt detection floor. The nearest CNEOS row to Mayon in the whole record is 312 km away and dates from 2015. So this test shows that events large enough for satellites to see have no volcano preference; it cannot directly test the smaller events the cameras actually caught. What it does establish is that the association is absent from the one dataset immune to the camera effect, which is where a real physical association should have appeared first."}],"chart":"volcano-dist","cases":["fb/F-05","fb/F-03"],"sources":["https://www.ngdc.noaa.gov/hazel/hazard-service/api/v1/volcanolocs","https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/","https://volcano.si.edu/"]},{"id":"BR-07","question":"Has a fireball wave like this happened before?","short":"One was reported in November 2013, and it is offered as the precedent for 2026. The reported figures are accurate. The month is not the interesting part of what the database actually holds.","tag":"NUFORC · 80,332 REPORTS TO 2014","sections":[{"h":"Why this question has a different dataset behind it","p":"The other fireball briefings argue from instruments: satellite sensors that report kilotonnes, camera networks that report absolute magnitude, eyewitness tallies that report how many people filed. The 2013 precedent comes from none of those. It comes from the National UFO Reporting Center, where every witness picks a SHAPE from a fixed list and 'fireball' is one of the choices. That makes it a record of what people called what they saw — which is the right instrument for a claim about a wave of fireball REPORTS, and the wrong one for a claim about the sky. nuforc.org returns 403 to a datacenter address; the route that works is a 2014 public mirror that geocoded the archive and kept the shape column. It holds 80,332 reports running to 2014-05-08."},{"h":"The claim, and how well it holds up","p":"Cheryl Costa, writing the New York Skies column for the Syracuse New Times, reported in November 2013 that the database had logged about 238 reports by the 11th — an ordinary month by volume — but that roughly 50 of them, near 20%, were fireballs, against a normal share of about 7% ranking fourth among roughly 30 categories. Checked against the archive, the baseline is very nearly exact: fireball is 7.92% of shape-bearing reports and ranks fourth of 29 shapes. The volume is close too — 227 US rows against 'about 238'. The elevation is real: 33 of those 226 shape-bearing US reports are fireballs, 14.6%, roughly double the long-run share."},{"h":"What the month is being measured against","p":"The 7.92% figure is the average of a century-long archive, and by 2013 it had stopped describing the present. Annual fireball share ran 4.85% in 2004 and 5.86% in 2009, then climbed: 8.56%, 10.72%, 14.54%, 13.97% across 2010 to 2013. Pool the twelve months immediately before the one in question and the fireball share is 14.33%. November 2013 came in at 14.37%. It was not a spike above the baseline that was current — it WAS that baseline. Ranked against all 172 complete months from January 2000 to April 2014 it comes 14th, and July 2012 holds the record at 22.26%."},{"h":"The part that is actually interesting","p":"The share of UFO reports labelled 'fireball' roughly tripled between 2009 and 2012 and then stayed high. That is a large, real, unexplained change in the record — and it is the kind of change the 2013 column was reaching for. It found the right dataset and pointed at the wrong month, three years late. What NUFORC cannot do is say why the rise happened: a genuine change in the sky, a camera arriving in every pocket, and the word 'fireball' spreading through popular usage would all look identical in a database of self-assigned shape labels. That ambiguity is the same one the AMS eyewitness series has, and it is why the console leans on camera photometry when it wants to know about the sky rather than about the reporters."},{"h":"Two limits, one of which decides the question","p":"The archive is 81% United States — 65,114 of the 80,332 rows — and it shows. On 2013-02-15, the day Chelyabinsk exploded over Russia and injured more than a thousand people, NUFORC logged 11 reports, 2 of them fireball, and February 2013 finished at 8.08%: among the quietest months of its year. The largest airburst since Tunguska is essentially invisible here, because it happened where the reporters are not. And the mirror stops on 2014-05-08. It can test the 2013 half of a 2013-versus-2026 parallel; it cannot test the 2026 half at all. So the honest answer to the question is: a fireball wave was reported before, the reported figures were sound, the month was ordinary for its time, and the change that WAS in the data arrived years earlier and has never been explained."}],"chart":"nuforc-share","cases":["fb/F-06","fb/F-03"],"sources":["https://github.com/planetsig/ufo-reports","https://nuforc.org/","https://fireball.amsmeteors.org/members/imo_fireball_stats/events_per_month_per_year","https://globalmeteornetwork.org/data/traj_summary_data/"]},{"id":"BR-08","question":"Do fireballs cluster at nuclear sites?","short":"The nuclear connection is the best-evidenced positional claim in the subject — and it rests on eyewitness testimony about craft, not on satellite detections of rocks. The catalog can only answer a narrower question, and it mostly answers no.","tag":"883 EVENTS · 195 REACTORS","sections":[{"h":"Why this one is different","p":"Most claims in this register are about a single object or a single dataset. The nuclear connection is neither. It starts in December 1948, when aircrews over New Mexico reported brilliant green objects flying too slowly and too flatly to be meteors, and the Air Force put Dr Lincoln LaPaz — the meteoriticist who had built America's first serious fireball-recovery programme — onto the problem. He concluded they were not ordinary meteors and that they appeared preferentially near Los Alamos, Sandia and Kirtland. Everything since has that shape: the March 1967 Malmstrom episode, Rendlesham beside the Bentwaters weapons store, the interviews Robert Hastings collected from former missile officers. It is a large, specific, first-hand evidentiary base gathered from people with clearances and careers to lose, and it deserves to be taken at full strength rather than waved at."},{"h":"And why this dataset is the wrong instrument for it","p":"Every row in the CNEOS catalog is an atmospheric airburst — a rock arriving at tens of kilometres per second and depositing energy in kilotonnes. The nuclear testimony describes structured objects, frequently hovering, watched from the ground at close range. Those are not the same phenomenon. A null result in a catalog of bolides cannot refute accounts of craft over missile silos, and this briefing does not claim otherwise. Two further limits: the catalog begins on 1988-04-15, forty years after LaPaz's fireballs, so none of his events are in it; and the 195 positions tested are civil POWER REACTORS, while the weapons complex he meant is a few dozen sites with no open positional dataset and far too few for a Monte Carlo."},{"h":"The narrower question, and its answer","p":"What the catalog can answer is whether the population of objects big enough for satellites to see shows any positional preference for nuclear sites. Same two nulls as the volcano test, both preserving each event's own latitude — one rotating the whole set by a single random longitude offset, one redrawing every longitude independently, 600 trials each. Within 100 km: 11 observed against 8.8 expected. Within 500 km: 68 against 71.5 — below chance. Median distance to the nearest reactor: 2682 km observed against 2687 by chance. Nothing."},{"h":"The one place something shows up","p":"Within 200 km there are 34 events against 24.8 expected — a 37% excess, reaching p=0.025 under the scatter null. That is reported here rather than buried, because it is in the direction the claim predicts. Two things stop it being a result. Three radii were tested, and one marginal value among three correlated tests is roughly what chance delivers. More telling is the shape: a genuine proximity effect is strongest where the proximity is tightest, and this one is absent at 100 km, present at 200 km, and gone by 500 km. A bump in the middle with nothing at the centre is not what an attraction looks like. Under the conservative null — the one that leaves the events' own clustering intact — it does not reach significance at all, at p=0.070."},{"h":"The same selection effect, found twice","p":"F-05 found that fireball footage concentrates at volcanoes because volcano observatories run fixed sky cameras around the clock. The nuclear case has the identical structure and nobody planted it there: LaPaz was employed by the New Mexico installations and based among them, and the observer network reporting to him was their own staff, under standing orders to watch the sky. A 1949 weapons complex was one of the few places on Earth with trained, instructed, cleared people looking up on purpose. That does not make a single report false. It means the geography of the reports is at least partly the geography of the watchers — and the one dataset with no watchers in it does not reproduce the pattern."}],"chart":"nuclear-dist","cases":["fb/F-07","fb/F-05"],"sources":["https://cneos.jpl.nasa.gov/fireballs/","https://datasets.wri.org/dataset/globalpowerplantdatabase","https://www.archives.gov/research/military/air-force/ufos"]}]};
